const CLOZE_PASSAGES = [{"id":"cl-kc-01-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"A Day at the Fishing Village","topic":"a school trip","passage":"Last Friday our class went on a trip to a small fishing village about two hours (1)____ the city. We had been looking forward (2)____ it for weeks, so nobody minded getting up at five in the morning. The bus left the school gate (3)____ time, and most of us fell asleep before we had even reached the motorway. When we arrived, a local guide (4)____ us around the harbour and explained how the boats are repaired after a storm. I had never realised how (5)____ work goes into keeping one small boat safe at sea. After lunch we were allowed to walk along the beach (6)____ we stayed in groups of at least three. My friend Linh found a piece of blue glass, (7)____ she has kept on her desk ever since. The only problem came at four o'clock, when two boys could not be found anywhere. Our teacher was about to call the police (8)____ they walked calmly out of an ice-cream shop with a cone in each hand. She was so relieved that she did not even punish them. (9)____ the way home everybody was quiet, partly because we were tired and partly because we were sad that the day was over. If I were asked to go again next year, I (10)____ say yes without thinking.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","of","at","from"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Distance or travel time is measured <b>from</b> a place: <i>two hours from the city</i>. <i>Of</i>, <i>at</i> and <i>by</i> do not express distance."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","on","to","for"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The phrasal verb is <b>look forward to</b> something. The other prepositions never follow <i>look forward</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","on","in","at"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>On time</b> means at the planned moment, neither early nor late. <i>In time</i> means early enough for something, which the sentence does not mention."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["showed","said","explained","told"],"correct":0,"explanation":"You <b>show</b> somebody around a place. <i>Told</i>, <i>said</i> and <i>explained</i> introduce words, not a guided walk, and cannot take <i>us around</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["many","lot","plenty","much"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>Work</i> is uncountable, so the quantifier is <b>how much</b>. <i>Many</i> goes with plural nouns; <i>lot</i> and <i>plenty</i> need <i>a lot of</i> / <i>plenty of</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["unless","although","provided","whereas"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Provided</b> (that) means <i>on condition that</i>: they could walk if they stayed in groups. <i>Unless</i> would reverse the condition, and <i>although</i> / <i>whereas</i> express contrast."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","which","what","who"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After a comma, a non-defining relative clause about a thing needs <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> cannot follow the comma, <i>who</i> is for people, and <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["when","while","until","since"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<i>Was about to do something</i> <b>when</b> something else happened is the pattern for an interrupted action. <i>While</i> needs a continuous action; <i>until</i> and <i>since</i> give the wrong time relation."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["By","In","At","On"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>on the way</b> home. <i>In the way</i> means blocking somebody, and <i>at</i> / <i>by the way</i> mean something different."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["will","would","can","must"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<i>If I were asked</i> is a second conditional, so the main clause takes <b>would</b>. <i>Will</i> belongs to the first conditional, and <i>can</i> / <i>must</i> do not express an imagined result."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-01-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Grandmother's Fish Soup","topic":"a family recipe","passage":"Every family has (1)____ least one dish that nobody outside the family can make properly. In ours it is my grandmother's fish soup. She learned it from her own mother, (2)____ used to sell it at the market in Nha Trang many years ago. Nobody has ever written the recipe (3)____, so the only way to learn it is to stand next to her in the kitchen and watch. Last summer I finally asked her to teach me. She laughed and said I would have to get up early, (4)____ the fish must be bought before seven, while it is still fresh. At the market she picked (5)____ each fish, looked at its eyes and put most of them back. Then, at home, she began chopping tomatoes and pineapple (6)____ measuring anything at all. When I asked how much salt she used, she simply said 'enough', (7)____ was not very helpful. The soup has to be cooked slowly (8)____ nearly an hour, and she never leaves the pot, not even to answer the phone. My first attempt, a week later, was too sour and (9)____ salty enough, but she tasted it and nodded. 'Next time,' she said, 'you will do it (10)____ better.' I hope she is right, because one day I want to cook it for my own children.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","in","at","for"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>at least</b>. No other preposition combines with <i>least</i> in this meaning."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","whom","whose","who"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>used to sell</i> and refers to a person, so it must be <b>who</b>. <i>Whom</i> is an object form, <i>which</i> is for things, and <i>whose</i> shows possession."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["down","on","over","in"],"correct":0,"explanation":"To <b>write down</b> something is to record it on paper. <i>Write on</i>, <i>write over</i> and <i>write in</i> do not carry this meaning."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["although","because","unless","so"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The second clause gives the <b>reason</b> for getting up early, so <b>because</b> fits. <i>So</i> would introduce a result, and <i>although</i> / <i>unless</i> give contrast or condition."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["over","off","up","on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"She lifted each fish to examine it: <b>pick up</b>. <i>Pick on</i> means to bully, and <i>pick over</i> / <i>pick off</i> do not fit a fish she then puts back."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["except","despite","unless","without"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Without</b> + -ing means she did not measure. <i>Unless</i> needs a full clause, and <i>except</i> / <i>despite</i> give the wrong meaning."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","that","what","it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Which</b> after a comma refers back to the whole idea of her saying 'enough'. <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma here, and <i>what</i> / <i>it</i> would need a new sentence."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","for","since","during"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A length of time takes <b>for</b>: <i>for nearly an hour</i>. <i>Since</i> marks a starting point, <i>during</i> needs a named period, and <i>by</i> gives a deadline."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["no","none","not","nothing"],"correct":2,"explanation":"To make an adjective negative you use <b>not</b>: <i>not salty enough</i>. <i>No</i>, <i>none</i> and <i>nothing</i> go with nouns, not adjectives."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["more","too","much","very"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A comparative such as <i>better</i> is strengthened with <b>much</b>. <i>Very</i> cannot modify a comparative, and <i>more better</i> / <i>too better</i> are impossible."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-01-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Three Days Without Bơ","topic":"a lost pet","passage":"Our dog Bơ disappeared (1)____ a Sunday afternoon in March. The gate had been left open by the man who delivers our water, and by the time we noticed, Bơ was nowhere to be (2)____. My little brother cried for an hour, and my mother kept saying that a dog (3)____ knows its way home will always come back. I was not so sure. That evening we printed fifty posters with his photo and stuck them (4)____ every lamp post in the neighbourhood. We also posted a message in the local group online, (5)____ was shared more than two hundred times. For three days nothing happened. Then, on Wednesday, a woman rang to say she had seen a brown dog sleeping outside a café near the river. We drove there (6)____ once, but the café owner told us the dog had run off that morning. We were about to (7)____ up when my brother spotted something moving under a parked car. It was Bơ, thinner and very dirty, but definitely ours. He was so happy to see us that he could not stop (8)____ his tail. Since then my father has put a new lock on the gate, and Bơ wears a collar with our phone number on it. (9)____ he escapes again, at least whoever finds him will know where he belongs. And my brother has promised that he will never leave the gate open (10)____.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","on","in","at"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A particular day or part of a day takes <b>on</b>: <i>on a Sunday afternoon</i>. <i>In</i> is for months and seasons, and <i>at</i> is for clock times."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["seen","watched","noticed","looked"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The fixed expression is <b>nowhere to be seen</b>. <i>Looked</i> needs a preposition, and <i>watched</i> / <i>noticed</i> are not used in this phrase."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["whose","who","what","that"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A defining relative clause about an animal uses <b>that</b> (or <i>which</i>). <i>Who</i> is for people, <i>whose</i> shows possession, and <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","by","on","in"],"correct":2,"explanation":"You stick a poster <b>on</b> a surface such as a lamp post. <i>In</i>, <i>at</i> and <i>by</i> describe position, not attachment."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","which","what","who"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After a comma, a relative clause about a thing (the message) needs <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> is not used after a comma, and <i>who</i> refers to people."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","in","for","on"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>At once</b> means immediately. The other prepositions do not form a phrase with <i>once</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["put","take","turn","give"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To <b>give up</b> is to stop trying. <i>Put up</i> means to build or tolerate, <i>take up</i> means to start a hobby, and <i>turn up</i> means to appear."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["to wag","wag","wagging","wagged"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<i>Stop</i> + -ing means to end an action, so <b>stop wagging</b>. <i>Stop to wag</i> would mean he paused in order to wag, which makes no sense here."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["Whether","If","Unless","Although"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence describes a possible future situation and its result, so <b>if</b> fits. <i>Unless</i> would mean <i>if he does not escape</i>, and <i>although</i> / <i>whether</i> do not introduce a condition here."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["yet","still","more","again"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>Never … <b>again</b></i> means not one more time. <i>More</i> would need <i>any more</i>, and <i>yet</i> / <i>still</i> do not follow <i>never</i> in this way."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-01-4","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"The Week It Never Stopped Raining","topic":"a rainy holiday","passage":"Last August my family spent a week at a small guesthouse by the sea, and it rained (1)____ single day. We had planned to swim, go fishing and take a boat to the islands, (2)____ none of that was possible. Instead, we spent most of our time indoors, playing cards and watching the rain run down the windows. At first my sister and I were bored (3)____ death. By the third day, (4)____, something changed. The owner of the guesthouse, an old man (5)____ had lived there all his life, began telling us stories about the storms he had seen as a boy. We listened (6)____ hours. He also taught us how to make a kite from bamboo and old newspaper, and on the one afternoon when the rain stopped for a while, we ran to the beach and tried to fly it. (7)____ it did not fly very high, we did not care. When we got home, my friends asked whether the holiday had been ruined. I told them it was the best one I had (8)____ had. I still cannot explain why. Perhaps it was because, for once, nobody was in a hurry and there was nothing we had (9)____ do. If the weather had been fine, we (10)____ probably have spent the whole week in the water and never spoken to the old man at all.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["every"],"answer":"every","explanation":"<b>Every single day</b> is the emphatic way of saying <i>all the days</i>. <i>Each</i> is not used with <i>single</i>, and <i>all</i> would need a plural noun."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["but","yet","though","although"],"answer":"but","explanation":"The plan and the reality contrast, so <b>but</b> (or <i>yet</i>, <i>though</i>, <i>although</i>) links them. <i>So</i> would wrongly make the bad weather a result of the plan."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The idiom is <b>bored to death</b>, meaning extremely bored. <i>Bored of</i> or <i>bored with</i> need a noun naming the cause, not <i>death</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["however","though","nevertheless","nonetheless"],"answer":"however","explanation":"A contrast word between commas in mid-sentence: <b>however</b> (or <i>though</i>). <i>But</i> cannot sit between two commas in this position."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["who","that"],"answer":"who","explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>had lived</i> and refers to a person, so <b>who</b> (or <i>that</i>). <i>Which</i> is for things and <i>whom</i> is an object form."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"A length of time takes <b>for</b>: <i>for hours</i>. <i>During</i> needs a named period such as <i>the evening</i>, and <i>since</i> marks a starting point."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["although","though","while","whilst"],"answer":"although","explanation":"A concession clause at the start of the sentence takes <b>although</b> (or <i>though</i>). <i>But</i> cannot begin a subordinate clause that is followed by a comma and a main clause."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["ever"],"answer":"ever","explanation":"With a superlative and the perfect tense, <b>ever</b> means <i>in my whole life</i>: <i>the best one I had ever had</i>. <i>Never</i> would make the sentence contradict itself."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"Obligation is <i>have <b>to</b> do</i>: <i>nothing we had to do</i>. <i>Must</i> cannot follow <i>had</i>, and no other particle joins <i>had</i> and <i>do</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["would","might","could"],"answer":"would","explanation":"<i>If the weather had been</i> is a third conditional, so the result needs <b>would</b> have (or <i>might</i> / <i>could</i> for a less certain result). <i>Will</i> cannot describe an imagined past."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-02-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Sunday Mornings with a Camera","topic":"a new hobby","passage":"Until last spring, I had never (1)____ much interest in photography. My uncle gave me his old camera when he bought a new one, and at first I left it in a drawer for weeks. Then one rainy Sunday, (2)____ there was nothing else to do, I took it out and walked around our neighbourhood. I was surprised by how (3)____ I enjoyed looking at ordinary things through the lens. A wet street, a cat on a wall, an old man selling bread – everything seemed more interesting than usual.<br><br>Since then, I (4)____ pictures almost every weekend. I have joined an online group (5)____ people share their photos and give each other advice. Some of the comments are quite honest, but I have learned to accept them (6)____ getting upset. My photos are still far from perfect. I often forget to check the light, and sometimes I press the button too early. However, I am (7)____ better than I was six months ago.<br><br>The hobby has changed my routine (8)____ well. I get up earlier, and I notice small details that I used to walk (9)____ without seeing. My mother says I am spending too much time on the camera and not enough on my homework. She may be right, but I think a hobby that makes you look (10)____ carefully at the world than before is worth a few hours a week.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the best option.","options":["made","given","taken","done"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>take an interest in</b> something. We do not say make, give or do an interest."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the best option.","options":["so","although","unless","as"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The clause gives the reason for taking the camera out, so a reason linker (<b>as</b> / because) is needed. <b>So</b> introduces a result, <b>although</b> a contrast and <b>unless</b> a condition."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the best option.","options":["much","many","very","too"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>How much I enjoyed</b> measures the degree of a verb. <b>Many</b> goes with countable nouns, and <b>very</b> or <b>too</b> cannot follow how before a subject and verb."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the best option.","options":["took","have taken","take","was taking"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Since then</b> links a past starting point to now, which calls for the present perfect. A simple past or past continuous would cut the action off in the past."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the best option.","options":["whose","whom","where","which"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The group is the place in which people share photos, so the relative adverb <b>where</b> fits. <b>Which</b> or <b>whom</b> would need to be the subject or object of the clause, and <b>whose</b> needs a possessed noun."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the best option.","options":["despite","unless","until","without"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Without + -ing</b> means the second action does not happen. <b>Despite</b> would wrongly suggest the writer does get upset, and <b>unless</b> or <b>until</b> make no sense here."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the best option.","options":["getting","turning","going","making"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Get better</b> is the everyday way to say improve. <b>Go</b> and <b>turn</b> are used for changes for the worse or for colours (go bad, turn red); <b>make better</b> needs an object."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the best option.","options":["so","as","too","also"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed phrase <b>as well</b> means also. <b>Too</b> and <b>also</b> already mean also on their own and do not combine with well."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the best option.","options":["through","across","past","over"],"correct":2,"explanation":"You <b>walk past</b> something when you go by it without stopping. <b>Over</b>, <b>through</b> and <b>across</b> describe going on top of, inside or from one side to the other."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the best option.","options":["much","very","more","most"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence ends with <b>than before</b>, so a comparative is needed: <b>more carefully</b>. <b>Most</b> is a superlative, and <b>much</b> or <b>very</b> cannot be followed by than."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-02-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Night the Lanterns Went Up","topic":"a village festival","passage":"Every autumn, the village (1)____ my grandmother lives holds a lantern festival. It takes place on the first full moon after the rice harvest, and people from the nearby towns come to watch. This year I was allowed to help with the preparations for the first time.<br><br>The work started a week (2)____ the festival. Every family was (3)____ for making its own lanterns from bamboo and coloured paper, and the children were given the job of painting them. My cousin and I painted fish, birds and stars, (4)____ of them very neat, but nobody seemed to mind. Meanwhile, the men built a wooden stage in the square, and the women prepared enough food to feed the whole village.<br><br>On the evening itself, the square was so crowded (5)____ we could hardly move. There was a drum performance, a dance by the local school, and a long speech by the village head, (6)____ nobody listened to very carefully. At nine o'clock the lanterns were lit and carried down to the river. Hundreds of small lights floated away in the dark, and for a moment everyone (7)____ silent.<br><br>My grandmother told me later that the festival had almost been (8)____ up twenty years ago because so many young people had left the village. Fortunately, a group of teachers decided to keep it going, and now it is more popular (9)____ ever. I hope it continues, and I have already promised to come back next year, (10)____ busy I am.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the best option.","options":["that","whose","which","where"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The relative clause tells us the place in which the grandmother lives, so <b>where</b> is needed. <b>Which</b> or <b>that</b> would need in before them, and <b>whose</b> shows possession."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the best option.","options":["until","since","before","ago"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>A week before the festival</b> gives a point of time measured back from an event. <b>Ago</b> counts back from now and cannot take an object; <b>until</b> and <b>since</b> describe a period, not a starting point."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the best option.","options":["proud","responsible","capable","interested"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The adjective that takes <b>for</b> and means having the duty to do something is <b>responsible</b>. <b>Capable</b> takes of, <b>interested</b> takes in, and <b>proud</b> takes of."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the best option.","options":["none","nothing","no","neither"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>None of them</b> is the pronoun used before of with a plural group. <b>No</b> must come directly before a noun, <b>neither</b> is for two things only, and <b>nothing</b> cannot take of them."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the best option.","options":["which","as","than","that"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>So + adjective + that</b> expresses a result. <b>As</b> and <b>than</b> belong to comparisons, and <b>which</b> would start a relative clause with no noun to refer to."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the best option.","options":["that","what","which","who"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The non-defining clause after the comma refers to the speech, a thing, so <b>which</b> is right. <b>Who</b> is for people, <b>that</b> cannot follow a comma in this kind of clause, and <b>what</b> does not refer back to a noun."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the best option.","options":["felt","fell","made","held"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Fall silent</b> is the collocation for suddenly stopping talking. <b>Felt silent</b> mixes up feel and fall, and <b>made</b> or <b>held</b> would need an object."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the best option.","options":["given","called","taken","put"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Give up</b> means to stop doing something for good, which fits a festival that nearly ended. <b>Take up</b> means to start, <b>put up</b> means to build or raise, and <b>call up</b> means to telephone."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the best option.","options":["as","then","that","than"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>More popular than ever</b> is a comparative, and comparatives are followed by <b>than</b>. <b>Then</b> is a common misspelling here, and <b>as</b> only pairs with as ... as."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the best option.","options":["whatever","however","whenever","although"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>However + adjective</b> means no matter how, so <b>however busy I am</b> is correct. <b>Whatever</b> and <b>whenever</b> cannot be followed by an adjective, and <b>although</b> needs a full clause after it, not busy I am."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-02-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Behind the Counter","topic":"my grandfather's shop","passage":"My grandfather (1)____ a small hardware shop on the corner of our street for more than forty years. It is not a large place – just one room with shelves that reach the ceiling – but he seems to know exactly (2)____ everything is. If a customer asks for a particular screw, he goes straight to the right box without looking.<br><br>When I was younger, I used to spend Saturday afternoons there. Grandfather (3)____ let me count the coins in the till, and if I did it correctly he gave me a sweet from the jar behind the counter. Later, he taught me how to (4)____ with customers: to greet them, to listen to what they needed, and never to make them feel foolish for asking a simple question.<br><br>These days the shop is much quieter. A big supermarket opened on the main road two years ago, and many people prefer to buy their tools there (5)____ it is cheaper. Grandfather does not complain, (6)____ I know he worries. He still opens at seven every morning and closes at six, (7)____ nobody has come in all afternoon.<br><br>Last month I suggested that he (8)____ sell some things online. He laughed and said he was too old for computers, but he did not say no. So now, every evening, I photograph a few items and put them on a website. We have (9)____ sold much yet, but last week a man from another city ordered three old brass door handles. Grandfather was so pleased that he told every customer (10)____ came in the next day.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the best option.","options":["has run","runs","ran","is running"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>For more than forty years</b> describes a period that continues up to now, so the present perfect <b>has run</b> is needed. <b>Ran</b> would mean the shop is closed, and the simple present or present continuous cannot take a for-period."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the best option.","options":["when","where","which","whether"],"correct":1,"explanation":"He knows the place of every item, so the indirect question needs <b>where</b>. <b>When</b> asks about time, <b>whether</b> asks a yes-or-no question, and <b>which</b> would need a noun after it."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the best option.","options":["used","should","would","might"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Would + verb</b> describes a repeated past habit, just like used to. <b>Used</b> needs to before the verb, and <b>should</b> or <b>might</b> do not describe habits."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the best option.","options":["treat","behave","manage","deal"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>deal with</b> people means to handle them. <b>Treat</b> takes a direct object with no with, and <b>behave</b> and <b>manage</b> do not combine with with in this meaning."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the best option.","options":["because","despite","so","although"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The clause gives the reason people choose the supermarket, so <b>because</b> fits. <b>So</b> introduces a result, <b>although</b> a contrast, and <b>despite</b> must be followed by a noun or -ing, not a clause."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the best option.","options":["so","but","or","nor"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Not complaining and secretly worrying are a contrast, so <b>but</b> joins them. <b>So</b> would make the worry a result of not complaining, and <b>or</b> or <b>nor</b> offer alternatives."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the best option.","options":["as if","in case","even if","even so"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Even if</b> means it makes no difference whether nobody has come, which fits keeping the shop open. <b>Even so</b> is an adverb, not a linker, <b>as if</b> compares, and <b>in case</b> gives a precaution."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the best option.","options":["would","will","must","should"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After <b>suggest that</b>, the verb takes <b>should</b> or the bare form. <b>Would</b> and <b>will</b> do not follow suggest, and <b>must</b> would make it an order, not a suggestion."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the best option.","options":["not","hardly","no","never"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Have not ... yet</b> is the normal negative present perfect with yet. <b>Never</b> and <b>hardly</b> do not combine with yet, and <b>no</b> cannot stand between have and a past participle."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the best option.","options":["who","whose","which","whom"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of came and refers to people, so <b>who</b> is right. <b>Whom</b> is only an object, <b>which</b> is for things, and <b>whose</b> needs a following noun."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-02-4","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"The Deep End","topic":"learning to swim","passage":"I did not learn to swim (1)____ I was fourteen, which is late in a town full of rivers. My friends had all learned as small children, and I was too embarrassed to admit that I could not. Whenever they went to the lake in summer, I made (2)____ excuse: a cold, a lot of homework, a visit to my aunt.<br><br>Everything changed when my school announced that swimming (3)____ be part of the sports programme the following year. There was no way to avoid it. That evening I told my father the truth, and instead (4)____ laughing, he offered to teach me himself.<br><br>Our first lesson was at the public pool early on a Saturday morning, when hardly anybody was there. I held (5)____ to the edge and refused to let go. My father did not push me. He simply stood in the water next to me and waited (6)____ I was ready. It took twenty minutes. After that, he showed me how to float on my back, and I was amazed to find that the water actually held me (7)____.<br><br>We went every weekend for two months. Some lessons went badly; once I swallowed so much water that I wanted to give (8)____ completely. But little by little I got (9)____ to the feeling of having nothing under my feet. By the end of the summer I could swim the length of the pool, slowly but without stopping.<br><br>When the school lessons began, I was not the best swimmer in the class, but I was no longer the boy who stayed on the bank. And I think my father was even (10)____ proud than I was.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["until","till","before"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Not ... until</b> (or till, before) marks the point at which the situation finally changed. <b>When</b> would say he did not learn at the age of fourteen, the opposite of the meaning."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["an","some","another","every"],"answer":"an","explanation":"<b>Make an excuse</b> is the collocation, and excuse is a countable noun that needs a determiner; <b>some</b>, <b>another</b> or <b>every</b> also work before the list of excuses. <b>The</b> fails because no particular excuse has been mentioned, and <b>up</b> fails because a bare noun cannot follow made up."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["would"],"answer":"would","explanation":"The announcement is reported after a past verb and refers to <b>the following year</b>, so will shifts back to <b>would</b>. <b>Will</b> breaks the sequence of tenses, and <b>could</b> or <b>might</b> are too uncertain for something there was no way to avoid."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"<b>Instead of + -ing</b> is the only form: laughing did not happen and teaching did. <b>Instead</b> alone cannot be followed by an -ing form, and <b>than</b> or <b>for</b> do not follow instead."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["on","tight","tightly","fast"],"answer":"on","explanation":"<b>Hold on to</b> something means to grip it and not let go, which matches refused to let go; <b>held tight / tightly / fast to</b> are also accepted. <b>Held out to</b> or <b>held up to</b> mean offering or lifting, not gripping."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["until","till"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Wait until</b> a clause happens is the pattern; till is the same word. <b>For</b> fails because wait for needs a noun or an object plus to-infinitive, not a subject and verb."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["up","afloat"],"answer":"up","explanation":"<b>Hold somebody up</b> means to support them so they do not sink, and <b>afloat</b> says the same thing. <b>Down</b> or <b>back</b> would mean the water pushed him under or stopped him, the opposite of floating."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["up","in"],"answer":"up","explanation":"<b>Give up</b> means to stop trying; <b>give in</b>, to admit defeat, also fits after a bad lesson. <b>Give out</b> means to distribute or run out, and <b>give away</b> means to hand something to someone."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["used","accustomed"],"answer":"used","explanation":"<b>Get used to + noun</b> means to become familiar with something; <b>accustomed</b> is the formal equivalent. <b>Use</b> without -d is a common slip, and <b>close</b> or <b>near</b> would need a physical thing after to."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["more"],"answer":"more","explanation":"<b>Than</b> later in the sentence shows a comparative, and with proud the comparative is formed with <b>more</b> before the adjective (prouder is also possible, but only without the word proud following). <b>Most</b> is a superlative, and <b>very</b> or <b>so</b> cannot be followed by than."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-03-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Beans and Music","topic":"a science fair","passage":"Every March our school holds a science fair in the sports hall, and this year I finally decided to take part (1)____ it. My partner, Minh, and I wanted to find out (2)____ or not plants grow faster when they listen to music, so we set up three pots of beans on the windowsill of his bedroom. One pot heard pop songs every evening, one heard classical music, and the third was left in silence. For four weeks we measured the plants every morning (3)____ we could compare them fairly. To be honest, the beans did not seem to care about the music at all, (4)____ we were a little disappointed. However, our teacher said that a result which surprises you is still a result. On the day of the fair, we (5)____ a large poster with our graphs and a photo of each pot. Dozens of parents and younger students walked past our table, and some of them stopped to ask questions. One little boy wanted to know if the beans liked pop or classical music, and Minh had to explain that they liked (6)____. In the afternoon, three judges, each of (7)____ carried a clipboard, came to look at every project. They were impressed by (8)____ carefully we had recorded our data, even though our idea was quite simple. We did not win first prize, (9)____ went to a girl who built a small water filter out of sand and charcoal. All the same, we got a certificate, and we have already decided (10)____ something with electricity next year.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","in","at","of"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed expression is <b>take part in</b> an event. <i>On</i>, <i>at</i> and <i>of</i> never follow <i>take part</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["whether","what","that","if"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Only <b>whether</b> can be followed directly by <i>or not</i>. <i>If</i> also introduces an indirect question, but <i>if or not</i> is not possible; <i>that</i> and <i>what</i> do not report a yes/no question."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["because","although","unless","so that"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>So that</b> + <i>could</i> expresses purpose: they measured every morning in order to compare the plants. <i>Because</i> would give a reason, not a purpose, and <i>although</i> and <i>unless</i> make no sense here."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["but","although","so","because"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The disappointment is the result of the beans ignoring the music, and a result is introduced by <b>so</b>. <i>But</i> and <i>although</i> would signal a contrast; <i>because</i> would reverse cause and effect."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["put off","put up","put out","put on"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You <b>put up</b> a poster, meaning you fix it on a wall or board. <i>Put off</i> means postpone, <i>put out</i> means extinguish, and <i>put on</i> is for clothes or a show."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["neither","none","either","both"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Two kinds of music were mentioned, and the beans liked not one and not the other, so <b>neither</b> is the answer. <i>None</i> is for three or more, <i>either</i> would need a negative verb, and <i>both</i> is the opposite meaning."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","which","them","whom"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After a preposition the relative pronoun for people is <b>whom</b>: <i>each of whom</i>. <i>Who</i> cannot follow <i>of</i> in this structure, <i>which</i> is for things, and <i>them</i> would create two main clauses joined by nothing."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","that","how","which"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>How</b> + adverb (<i>how carefully</i>) introduces the clause that the judges admired. <i>That</i> and <i>which</i> cannot go with <i>carefully</i>, and <i>what carefully</i> is not English."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","which","who","what"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A non-defining relative clause after a comma needs <b>which</b> to refer back to <i>first prize</i>. <i>That</i> is not used after a comma, <i>who</i> is for people, and <i>what</i> does not refer back to a noun."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["tried","trying","try","to try"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The verb <b>decide</b> is followed by a to-infinitive: <i>decided to try</i>. It never takes an -ing form or a bare verb."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-03-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Ninety Kilometres to the Sea","topic":"a bicycle journey","passage":"Last summer my cousin Huy and I cycled from our town to the coast, a distance of almost ninety kilometres. We had been planning the trip (1)____ the beginning of the year, but our parents only agreed after we promised to phone them every two hours. We set (2)____ at six in the morning, when the roads were still empty and the air was cool. For the first hour everything went smoothly, and we felt (3)____ proud of ourselves that we started singing. Then, just outside a village, Huy's back tyre went flat. Neither of us had ever repaired a tyre before, (4)____ luckily we had a small kit in Huy's bag. A farmer who was passing by on his motorbike stopped and showed us (5)____ to do, and twenty minutes later we were back on the road. By midday the sun was so hot that we (6)____ to rest under a tree and drink nearly all our water. I have to admit that I was tempted to give (7)____ and catch a bus, but Huy refused to listen. In the afternoon the land became flatter and we could smell the sea long before we saw it. We finally reached the beach at half past four, (8)____ tired than we had expected but extremely happy. My aunt, (9)____ house is near the harbour, had prepared a huge dinner for us. Looking back, I think the journey taught me more about patience than any lesson at school (10)____ has.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["since","from","during","for"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The past perfect continuous with a starting point takes <b>since</b>: <i>had been planning since the beginning of the year</i>. <i>For</i> needs a length of time, and <i>from</i> and <i>during</i> do not combine with this tense in this way."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","off","up","down"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Set off</b> means to begin a journey. <i>Set up</i> means to establish, <i>set down</i> means to put something down, and <i>set in</i> is used for weather or a season."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["such","too","so","very"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Before an adjective, the result structure is <b>so</b> + adjective + <i>that</i>. <i>Such</i> needs a noun, and <i>too</i> and <i>very</i> cannot be followed by a <i>that</i>-clause of result."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["because","unless","so","but"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The second half contrasts with the first: they had no experience, <b>but</b> they did have a kit. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would link cause and result, and <i>unless</i> makes no sense."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","that","how","which"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<i>Do</i> needs an object, and <b>what</b> supplies it: <i>showed us what to do</i>. <i>How to do</i> would need <i>it</i> after it, and <i>which</i> and <i>that</i> cannot stand alone here."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["could","had","must","should"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Only <b>had</b> can be followed by <i>to</i>: <i>had to rest</i> expresses necessity in the past. <i>Must</i>, <i>should</i> and <i>could</i> take a bare infinitive, never <i>to</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","away","up","out"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Give up</b> means to stop trying. <i>Give off</i> is for smells or heat, <i>give away</i> means to donate, and <i>give out</i> means to distribute."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["very","most","much","more"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The comparative before <i>than</i> is <b>more tired</b>. <i>Most</i> is a superlative, and <i>much</i> and <i>very</i> cannot be followed by <i>than</i> without a comparative."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["whose","which","that","who"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The house belongs to the aunt, so the possessive relative <b>whose</b> is needed before the noun <i>house</i>. <i>Who</i>, <i>which</i> and <i>that</i> cannot show possession."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["ever","always","yet","never"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After a comparison with <i>than any</i>, <b>ever</b> means <i>at any time</i>. <i>Never</i> would make the sentence illogical, and <i>always</i> and <i>yet</i> do not fit this structure."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-03-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Eighty Candles in the Garden","topic":"a birthday surprise","passage":"My grandmother turned eighty last month, and my family decided to throw her a surprise party. This was far (1)____ easy than it sounds, because she is the kind of person who notices everything. My mother told her that we were all going out for a quiet lunch, (2)____ she believed without asking a single question. The hardest part was keeping the secret (3)____ my little brother, who cannot stay quiet for more than five minutes. In the end we simply did not tell him until the morning of the party. I was (4)____ charge of the cake, which I had ordered from a bakery near my school. Unfortunately, when I went to collect it, the shop was closed, and I had to wait outside for nearly an hour (5)____ the owner arrived. (6)____, my cousins were hanging lanterns in the trees and my father was laying the tables in the garden. At exactly twelve o'clock my mother drove Grandma through the gate. When everybody jumped out and shouted, she was so shocked that she dropped her handbag. Then she started laughing and could not stop. She told us later that she (7)____ suspected something the week before, when my aunt asked her about her favourite songs, but she had decided to say nothing. We spent the whole afternoon eating, singing and looking (8)____ old photographs. My grandfather, who passed away three years ago, would have loved every minute of it. Before we left, Grandma made (9)____ promise to do the same thing for her ninetieth birthday. I do not think (10)____ of us would dare to refuse.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["least","little","fewer","less"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The comparative before <i>than</i> is <b>less easy</b>. <i>Fewer</i> goes with plural nouns, <i>least</i> is a superlative, and <i>little</i> is not a comparative form."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","who","which","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After a comma, the relative pronoun that refers to the whole idea of the previous clause is <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> cannot start a non-defining clause, <i>who</i> is for people, and <i>what</i> does not refer back."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","from","to","off"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You <b>keep a secret from</b> somebody, meaning you do not let that person know. <i>Of</i>, <i>to</i> and <i>off</i> do not form this collocation."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","under","on","at"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The expression is <b>in charge of</b> something, meaning responsible for it. The other prepositions never combine with <i>charge</i> in this sense."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["while","during","by","until"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The waiting continued up to the moment the owner arrived, which is expressed by <b>until</b> + clause. <i>While</i> would describe two actions happening together, and <i>during</i> and <i>by</i> are prepositions that cannot introduce a clause here."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["Otherwise","However","Meanwhile","Therefore"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Meanwhile</b> links two things happening at the same time: the narrator was waiting while the family prepared the garden. <i>However</i> needs a contrast, <i>therefore</i> a result, and <i>otherwise</i> a condition."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["has","had","was","would"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The suspicion came before the moment of telling, so the past perfect <b>had suspected</b> is needed. <i>Has</i> is present perfect, and <i>was</i> and <i>would</i> cannot combine with <i>suspected</i> in this meaning."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","after","up","for"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Viewing photographs is <b>looking at</b> them. <i>Look for</i> means search, <i>look after</i> means take care of, and <i>look up</i> means find information."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["our","we","ours","us"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The structure is <b>make somebody promise</b>, so the object pronoun <b>us</b> is needed. <i>We</i> is a subject pronoun, and <i>our</i> and <i>ours</i> are possessives."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["none","any","some","every"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After a negative verb (<i>do not think</i>), the quantifier is <b>any</b>: <i>I do not think any of us</i>. <i>Some</i> is for positive sentences, <i>none</i> would create a double negative, and <i>every</i> cannot be followed by <i>of us</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-03-4","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"The Peacock and the Sandwich","topic":"a visit to the zoo","passage":"When I was nine, my class went on a day trip to the city zoo, and I still remember it more clearly (1)____ any other trip from primary school. We arrived so early (2)____ the gates had only just opened and the paths were almost empty. Our teacher divided us (3)____ groups of five and gave each group a map and a list of animals to find. My group headed straight for the elephants, (4)____ we could hear long before we could see them. One of them was spraying water over its back with its trunk, and a baby elephant was trying to copy it (5)____ much success. After that we spent nearly half an hour watching the monkeys, which seemed just as interested (6)____ us as we were in them. The keeper told us that the oldest monkey had lived at the zoo (7)____ more than thirty years. At lunchtime we sat on the grass near the lake, and a peacock walked up to us and stole a piece of bread (8)____ of my friend's hand. Everyone laughed except my friend, who had been waiting for that sandwich all morning. In the afternoon it began to rain, so we sheltered in the reptile house, where I saw a snake for the first time in my life. I was not brave (9)____ to touch it, although the keeper said it was completely harmless. On the bus home, our teacher asked us to write a few sentences about our favourite animal. I chose the baby elephant, and I wrote that if I ever became a zookeeper, I (10)____ look after the elephants and nothing else.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): write one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The comparative <i>more clearly</i> must be completed with <b>than</b>. <i>As</i> is used only with <i>as ... as</i>, not after <i>more</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): write one word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<i>So early</i> sets up a result clause, and a result clause begins with <b>that</b>. <i>When</i> or <i>because</i> would break the <i>so ... that</i> structure."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): write one word.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"The verb <i>divide</i> takes <b>into</b> before the parts that are formed: <i>divided us into groups</i>. <i>In</i> does not show the change from one whole to several parts."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): write one word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause after a comma referring to <i>the elephants</i> needs <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> is not used after a comma, and <i>who</i> is reserved for people."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): write one word.","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"The baby elephant was trying and failing, so it copied its mother <b>without</b> much success. <i>With</i> would mean the opposite, and <i>much</i> after <i>with</i> would need a negative verb earlier."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): write one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"The adjective <i>interested</i> is always followed by <b>in</b>, and the sentence repeats the same preposition later (<i>in them</i>). <i>About</i> or <i>on</i> never follow <i>interested</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): write one word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"<i>More than thirty years</i> is a length of time, so it takes <b>for</b>. <i>Since</i> would need a starting point such as a year or a date."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): write one word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"The phrase is <b>out of</b> somebody's hand, showing movement from inside to outside. <i>From</i> cannot be followed by <i>of</i>, and <i>off</i> would describe a surface, not a hand holding something."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): write one word.","accept":["enough"],"answer":"enough","explanation":"After an adjective, the degree word that takes a to-infinitive is <b>enough</b>: <i>brave enough to touch</i>. <i>Too</i> comes before the adjective and gives the opposite meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): write one word.","accept":["would","could","might"],"answer":"would","explanation":"The <i>if</i>-clause uses the past simple (<i>became</i>), so this is a second conditional and the main clause needs <b>would</b> (or <i>could</i> / <i>might</i>). <i>Will</i> belongs to the first conditional and cannot follow an unreal past-tense condition."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-04-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Cup Final","topic":"a football final","passage":"Last Saturday our school football team played in the final of the district cup for the first time (1)____ ten years. The match was held at the stadium in the town centre, and the stands were full (2)____ students. My brother Nam is the goalkeeper, so my whole family sat in the front row with a banner that my mother had painted the night before. The first half was very slow. Both teams were nervous, and there were hardly (3)____ chances for either side. Then, just before the break, our captain scored (4)____ a corner and the crowd went wild. In the second half the other team attacked without stopping. Nam made three great saves. (5)____, with only ten minutes left, a striker finally got the ball past him. One-one. I could hardly watch. The referee had already looked at his watch (6)____ our youngest player, who is only in grade 7, picked up a loose ball near the halfway line. He ran past two defenders and shot from a long way out. The ball hit the post, rolled along the line and went in. Nobody could believe (7)____ had happened. When the final whistle blew, the players lifted the boy onto their shoulders and carried him round the pitch. My brother admitted later that he (8)____ never been so tired in his life. On the way home Dad said that if the boy (9)____ training, he could play for the city one day. I am not sure about that, but I know I will remember that afternoon (10)____ the rest of my life.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","for","since","during"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After <b>for the first time</b> we say <b>in</b> + a period: <i>for the first time in ten years</i>. <i>For ten years</i> describes how long something lasts, which is not the meaning here."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["from","of","with","by"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The adjective <b>full</b> takes <b>of</b>: <i>full of students</i>. <i>Filled with</i> and <i>packed with</i> exist, but <i>full with</i> does not."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["no","few","any","some"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Hardly any</b> means almost none. <i>Hardly</i> is already negative, so <i>hardly no</i> is a double negative, and <i>hardly some / hardly few</i> are not English."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","by","with","from"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In football you score <b>from</b> a corner, a free kick or a penalty, meaning the goal came out of that situation. <i>By</i> would introduce the scorer, not the move."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["However","Therefore","Moreover","Otherwise"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The goalkeeper made great saves, but then a goal was scored against him: a contrast, so <b>However</b>. <i>Therefore</i> gives a result and <i>Moreover</i> adds a similar point, which the sentence is not."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["since","when","while","until"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<i>Had already looked at his watch</i> + <b>when</b> + past simple describes a sudden event that interrupts a situation. <i>While</i> needs a continuing action, and <i>until</i> would mean he stopped looking at that moment."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","how","what","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The clause needs a subject: <b>what</b> had happened = the thing that had happened. <i>That</i> cannot be the subject of <i>had happened</i>, and <i>which</i> needs a noun before it."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["has","was","would","had"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After a past reporting verb (<i>admitted</i>) the present perfect moves back to the past perfect: <b>had</b> never been. <i>Has</i> keeps the original tense and <i>was never been</i> is not a tense at all."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["kept","keep","will keep","would keep"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Dad's actual words were <i>If he keeps training, he can play for the city</i>. After the past reporting verb <i>said</i>, the whole conditional moves one tense back: <i>keeps</i> becomes <b>kept</b> and <i>can</i> becomes <i>could</i>. <i>Keep</i> has no -s for the singular subject <i>the boy</i>, and <i>will / would keep</i> never go inside the <i>if</i>-clause."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","in","during","since"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>For the rest of my life</b> is a fixed expression of duration with <b>for</b>. <i>During</i> is possible with some periods but never with <i>the rest of my life</i>, and <i>since</i> needs a starting point."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-04-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Six O'Clock at the Market","topic":"a market morning","passage":"My grandmother has sold vegetables at the same market for nearly forty years, and last Sunday she let me help her (1)____ the first time. We left the house while it was still dark. (2)____ the time we reached the market, the lorries from the countryside had already arrived and men were carrying boxes of cabbages on their shoulders. Grandma greeted everyone (3)____ name. She has known most of the other sellers since they were children, and they clearly respect her. My job was simple: I had to arrange the tomatoes in neat rows (4)____ the best ones were at the front. By seven the first customers appeared. (5)____ of them were mothers who wanted to finish their shopping before work. One woman argued about the price for nearly five minutes, but Grandma just smiled and gave her an extra chilli. \"If you (6)____ her leave unhappy, she won't come back,\" she told me afterwards. At nine o'clock a boy on a motorbike delivered a basket of fresh herbs (7)____ smell filled the whole corner. I had never noticed how loud a market is: bells, radios, people calling out prices, and somewhere a rooster that would not stop. By eleven there was hardly (8)____ left on the table, and my feet hurt so much that I could barely stand. Grandma, (9)____ is seventy-two, did not look tired at all. She counted the money, gave me a note for my help and said I could come again (10)____ I promised not to eat the strawberries next time.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","for","at","in"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>for the first time</b>. <i>At first</i> exists but is not followed by <i>time</i>, and <i>in</i> and <i>on</i> do not collocate with <i>the first time</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["By","At","In","On"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>By the time</b> + clause means <i>not later than the moment when</i>, and it explains the past perfect <i>had already arrived</i>. <i>At the time</i> means <i>at that moment</i> and is not followed by a clause with <i>reached</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["with","in","on","by"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To greet or call somebody <b>by name</b> is the fixed expression. <i>With</i>, <i>in</i> and <i>on</i> never combine with <i>name</i> in this sense."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["as if","even though","so that","in order"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The second clause gives the purpose of arranging the rows, so <b>so that</b> is needed. <i>In order</i> requires <i>to</i> + verb or <i>that</i>, and <i>as if</i> and <i>even though</i> change the meaning."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["Every","Most","Almost","Much"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Most of them</b> = the majority. <i>Almost</i> is an adverb and needs <i>all</i> (<i>almost all of them</i>); <i>much</i> is for uncountable nouns, and <i>every</i> is never followed by <i>of</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["let","allow","permit","get"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Only <b>let</b> is followed by object + bare infinitive: <i>let her leave</i>. <i>Allow</i>, <i>permit</i> and <i>get</i> need <i>to</i>: <i>allow her to leave</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","that","whom","whose"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The smell belongs to the herbs, so the possessive relative <b>whose</b> is needed before the noun <i>smell</i>. <i>Which</i> and <i>that</i> cannot stand directly before a noun in this way."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["nothing","everything","anything","something"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Hardly anything</b> means almost nothing. <i>Hardly</i> is already negative, so <i>hardly nothing</i> is a double negative, and <i>something / everything</i> do not follow <i>hardly</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","who","whom","which"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Extra information about a person between commas takes <b>who</b>. <i>That</i> is not used in a non-defining clause, <i>which</i> is for things, and <i>whom</i> is only an object."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["unless","in case","even if","as long as"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>As long as</b> = on condition that: she can come again if she promises. <i>Unless</i> would mean the opposite, and <i>in case</i> introduces a precaution, not a condition."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-04-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Boxes Everywhere","topic":"moving house","passage":"We moved house in July, and I still have not (1)____ used to the new flat. For twelve years I had lived in the same room, with the same view of the same mango tree, and I did not want to leave. My parents, (2)____, were excited. The new place is closer to their offices and has a lift, which matters (3)____ my grandfather cannot manage stairs any more. The packing took two whole weeks. Mum wrote the name of a room on every box, but by the end nobody was paying attention, and the kitchen knives ended (4)____ in a box marked 'bathroom'. On moving day the lorry arrived an hour late, and the driver refused to carry the piano (5)____ we paid him extra. My father was furious, but there was nothing he could do. The strangest moment came when the old flat was completely empty. Our voices sounded different, and the walls had pale squares (6)____ the pictures used to hang. I took one last photo and closed the door. Since then I have slowly been getting to know the neighbourhood. There is a bakery downstairs, a park (7)____ five minutes' walk of our building, and a boy next door who plays the same online game as me. Last week I finally (8)____ round to unpacking my books, and the room started to feel like mine. My old friends have promised to visit, though I know that promises like that are not always (9)____. Still, Mum says that by next summer I will wonder (10)____ I ever made such a fuss.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["made","put","got","taken"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Get used to</b> something means become familiar with it, so the past participle <b>got</b> completes <i>have not got used to</i>. <i>Take</i>, <i>make</i> and <i>put</i> do not combine with <i>used to</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["therefore","although","despite","however"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The parents feel the opposite of the writer, and the word sits between commas as an adverb: <b>however</b>. <i>Although</i> and <i>despite</i> must begin a clause or phrase, not stand alone between commas."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["because","so","although","unless"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The grandfather's difficulty with stairs is the reason the lift matters, so <b>because</b>. <i>So</i> would make it a result, and <i>although</i> would make the two facts contrast, which they do not."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["over","up","out","off"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>End up</b> + place means finish in that place, usually by accident. <i>End out</i>, <i>end off</i> and <i>end over</i> are not phrasal verbs."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["although","whether","unless","if"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The driver would only carry the piano on the condition of extra money: <i>refused … <b>unless</b> we paid</i> = except if we paid. <i>If we paid</i> would mean he refused because of the payment, the opposite of the sense."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","when","that","where"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The clause tells us the place the pictures used to hang, so the relative adverb <b>where</b> is needed. <i>Which</i> and <i>that</i> would need to be the subject or object of <i>hang</i>, and <i>when</i> refers to time."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["within","inside","among","along"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Within</b> + distance + <i>of</i> a place means no further than that: <i>within five minutes' walk of our building</i>. <i>Inside</i> is for enclosed spaces, and <i>among</i> and <i>along</i> do not describe distance."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["turned","got","came","went"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Get round to</b> doing something means finally find the time to do it. <i>Come round</i> means visit or wake up, and <i>go round</i> and <i>turn round</i> do not take <i>to</i> + -ing."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["made","done","kept","held"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A promise is <b>kept</b> or broken; here it is passive, so the past participle. <i>Made</i> collocates with <i>promise</i> but means giving the promise, not carrying it out, and <i>held</i> and <i>done</i> do not collocate."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","what","why","if"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The writer will ask himself the reason for his behaviour: <i>wonder <b>why</b> I made such a fuss</i>. <i>If</i> would question whether the fuss happened at all, which he already knows, and <i>that</i> and <i>what</i> cannot introduce this clause."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-04-4","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Paper Wings","topic":"a kite competition","passage":"Every March the wind along the coast becomes strong and steady, and that is when the kite competition (1)____ place. Anyone can enter, as (2)____ as the kite is made by hand. This year my cousin Bao and I decided to build one together. We spent three weekends in his garage cutting bamboo and gluing paper, and we argued (3)____ the colours the whole time. In the end we painted it red and gold, (4)____ are the colours of our school. On the morning of the contest (5)____ were more than two hundred kites lying on the sand, and the beach was full of families, dogs and food sellers. The judges give points for beauty, for height and for how long a kite stays (6)____ the air. Our first flight was a disaster. The string broke after twenty seconds and the kite landed in the sea. Bao wanted to give (7)____, but an old man who had been watching came over and showed us how to tie the string properly. On our second try the kite rose slowly, then caught the wind and climbed higher (8)____ almost every other kite on the beach. It stayed there for nearly an hour. We did not win first prize, (9)____ we did get a certificate for the most beautiful kite. Bao has already started drawing plans for next year, and this time we are going to test the string (10)____ we go anywhere near the water.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): write one word.","accept":["takes"],"answer":"takes","explanation":"An event <b>takes place</b> = happens; the present simple fits a yearly routine. <i>Has place</i> and <i>makes place</i> are not English, and <i>took</i> would break the present-tense description."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): write one word.","accept":["long","far"],"answer":"long","explanation":"<b>As long as</b> = on condition that; <i>as far as</i> is also accepted here in the sense of <i>to the extent that</i>. <i>As soon as</i> would be about time, not a rule, and <i>if</i> cannot sit between <i>as … as</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): write one word.","accept":["about","over"],"answer":"about","explanation":"You argue <b>about</b> (or <b>over</b>) a subject. <i>Argue for</i> means support a side, and <i>argue with</i> needs a person, not the colours."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): write one word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause after a comma, referring to things (the colours), takes <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> is not used after a comma in this kind of clause, and <i>they</i> would need a new sentence."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): write one word.","accept":["there"],"answer":"there","explanation":"<b>There were</b> + plural noun introduces the existence of something, and the verb <i>were</i> shows this. <i>They</i> would need an earlier noun to refer to, and <i>it</i> is singular."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): write one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"A kite or plane stays <b>in the air</b>. <i>On the air</i> is used for radio and television, and <i>up</i> cannot be followed by <i>the air</i> here."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): write one word.","accept":["up","in"],"answer":"up","explanation":"<b>Give up</b> = stop trying; <b>give in</b> = stop resisting and accept defeat, also acceptable after a failure. <i>Give away</i> means hand something to someone else, and <i>give out</i> means distribute or stop working."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): write one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"A comparative (<i>higher</i>) is completed by <b>than</b>. <i>Then</i> is a time word and a common spelling trap, and <i>as</i> only pairs with <i>as high as</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): write one word.","accept":["but","although","though","yet"],"answer":"but","explanation":"The two halves contrast, so a contrast linker: <b>but</b>, or <i>although / though / yet</i>. <i>And</i> does not signal the contrast, and <i>however</i> cannot join two clauses after a comma without a full stop or semicolon."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): write one word.","accept":["before"],"answer":"before","explanation":"The lesson of the story is to check the string first, so <b>before</b> we go near the water. <i>After</i> would repeat the mistake, and <i>until</i> would mean they keep testing while going towards the sea."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-05-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Stars, Rain and a Goat","topic":"a night at camp","passage":"Every summer my cousins and I spend one night at a camp in the hills behind my grandmother's village. This year the weather forecast (1)____ rain, but we decided to go anyway. We (2)____ up our tent before the sun went down, which turned out to be a wise choice, because the first drops fell while we were still collecting firewood. My uncle, (3)____ has camped there since he was a boy, showed us how to keep the fire burning (4)____ spite of the wet wood. By nine o'clock the rain had stopped and the sky was full of stars. My little cousin Bao, who is only seven, had never seen (5)____ a clear sky in his life and kept asking whether the stars were real. We sat close to the fire and told stories until we could hardly (6)____ our eyes open. Sleeping on the ground is not comfortable, and I woke up several times during the night. At one point I heard a strange noise outside the tent and was (7)____ frightened that I could not move. In the morning we discovered that the noise had been made by a goat (8)____ to my grandmother's neighbour. Everyone laughed at me, but I did not (9)____ it seriously. Before we left, we made sure the fire was completely out and picked up every piece of rubbish. If we (10)____ the place dirty, my uncle would never take us again.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["informed","warned","told","predicted"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A forecast <b>predicts</b> rain: the verb takes the weather directly as its object. <i>Warned</i> needs <i>of/about</i>, and <i>told</i> and <i>informed</i> need a person as their object."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["got","took","put","made"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The phrasal verb for building a tent is <b>put up</b>. <i>Take up</i> means start a hobby, and <i>make up</i> and <i>get up</i> have nothing to do with tents."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["whom","who","whose","which"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>has camped</i> and refers to a person, so it must be <b>who</b>. <i>Whom</i> is only for objects, <i>which</i> is for things, and <i>whose</i> shows possession."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","on","at","by"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in spite of</b>, meaning despite. No other preposition can replace <i>in</i> here."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["too","very","so","such"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Before <i>a</i> + adjective + noun we use <b>such</b>: <i>such a clear sky</i>. <i>So</i>, <i>too</i> and <i>very</i> go directly in front of an adjective, not in front of <i>a</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["last","stay","keep","remain"],"correct":2,"explanation":"You <b>keep your eyes open</b>: <i>keep</i> takes an object and an adjective. <i>Stay</i>, <i>remain</i> and <i>last</i> cannot take <i>our eyes</i> as an object."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["very","so","such","too"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pattern is <b>so</b> + adjective + <i>that</i> clause: <i>so frightened that I could not move</i>. <i>Such</i> needs a noun, and <i>too</i> and <i>very</i> are not followed by a <i>that</i> clause of result."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["belonging","belongs","belong","belonged"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A reduced relative clause uses the -ing participle: <i>a goat <b>belonging</b> to my grandmother's neighbour</i> = a goat which belonged to her. The finite forms would need <i>which</i> or <i>that</i> before them."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["have","get","make","take"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>take something seriously</b>. <i>Get</i>, <i>make</i> and <i>have</i> do not combine with <i>seriously</i> in this way."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["leave","left","leaving","leaves"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The main clause has <i>would never take</i>, so this is a second conditional and the <i>if</i> clause needs the past simple <b>left</b>. Present forms would belong to a first conditional with <i>will</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-05-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Five Books at a Time","topic":"a library card","passage":"Until last month I had never been inside the public library in our district. My English teacher kept telling us that the library had (1)____ of graded readers, so one Saturday I finally went to have a look. To borrow anything you need a card, and getting one is not (2)____ difficult as I had expected. I (3)____ in a short form with my name, address and school, and a librarian checked my student card. She explained that members (4)____ borrow up to five books at a time and keep them for three weeks. If a book is returned late, you (5)____ pay a small fine for each day. I chose two novels and a book about space, (6)____ I had wanted to read since I saw a film about the planets. The librarian told me that the reading room on the second floor is (7)____ quietest place in the building, and she was right. I sat there for two hours (8)____ anybody disturbing me. Since then I have been going back every weekend. Sometimes I meet my friend Hoa there, and we test each other (9)____ new words from the books we have read. My mother says it is the best thing I have done all year, and I think she may (10)____ right. The card cost nothing, but it has already given me a new habit.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["several","much","plenty","many"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Plenty of</b> works before any noun and means a large number. <i>Much of</i>, <i>many of</i> and <i>several of</i> need <i>the</i> or a pronoun after them (<i>many of the readers</i>)."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","such","too","as"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The comparison of equality is <b>as</b> + adjective + <i>as</i>, and after <i>not</i> the first <i>as</i> still fits. <i>That</i>, <i>such</i> and <i>too</i> cannot be followed by the second <i>as</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["filled","completed","put","wrote"],"correct":0,"explanation":"You <b>fill in</b> a form. <i>Completed</i> would need no <i>in</i>, and <i>wrote in</i> and <i>put in</i> do not describe filling a form."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["need","can","should","must"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence describes what members are allowed to do, so the modal is <b>can</b>. <i>Must</i> and <i>should</i> would make borrowing five books an obligation, and <i>need</i> is not followed by a bare verb in an affirmative sentence."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["have","ought","must","need"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A rule that cannot be avoided uses <b>must</b> + bare verb. <i>Have</i> and <i>ought</i> would need <i>to pay</i>, and <i>need pay</i> is not used in an affirmative sentence."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","whom","that","which"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After a comma, a non-defining relative clause about a thing takes <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> is not used after a comma, <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun here, and <i>whom</i> is for people."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["the","an","every","a"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A superlative such as <i>quietest</i> is normally preceded by <b>the</b>. <i>A</i>, <i>an</i> and <i>every</i> do not go with a superlative that picks out one place."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["with","without","except","besides"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The meaning is that nobody disturbed the writer, which is expressed by <b>without</b> + -ing. <i>With anybody disturbing me</i> means the opposite, and <i>except</i> and <i>besides</i> do not fit the -ing phrase."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","for","on","about"],"correct":2,"explanation":"You <b>test somebody on</b> the material they have learned. <i>In</i>, <i>for</i> and <i>about</i> do not follow <i>test</i> in this meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["is","been","be","being"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After a modal verb such as <i>may</i> we need the bare infinitive <b>be</b>. <i>Is</i>, <i>been</i> and <i>being</i> cannot follow a modal directly."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-05-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Desks in a Circle","topic":"the first day of term","passage":"I always feel nervous on the first day of term, even though I have been going to the same school (1)____ six years. This September was different, because our class had been moved to a new building on the other side of the playground. I arrived early, (2)____ I could find my classroom without asking anyone. The corridor was so long that I walked past the door twice (3)____ I noticed the number. Inside, the desks were arranged in a circle rather than in rows, (4)____ meant we could all see each other. Our new form teacher, Mr Tuan, greeted us at the door and asked us to (5)____ ourselves up in alphabetical order. That took a surprisingly long time, as nobody could remember (6)____ to spell the other students' surnames. After that he handed out the timetable and explained the rules. We are not (7)____ to use our phones in class, but we may bring them to school for emergencies. At break my old friends and I went to look at the new library, which is twice (8)____ size of the old one. By the end of the day I had stopped worrying. Nothing had gone wrong, and I had even made a new friend (9)____ sits next to me in maths. Perhaps next year I will not feel nervous (10)____ all.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["since","for","during","in"],"correct":1,"explanation":"With the present perfect continuous, a length of time (<i>six years</i>) takes <b>for</b>. <i>Since</i> needs a starting point such as <i>2020</i>, and <i>during</i> and <i>in</i> do not measure how long an action has continued."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","although","unless","because"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Arriving early was done in order to find the room, so the linker of purpose is <b>so</b> (that). <i>Because</i> would give the reason, and <i>although</i> and <i>unless</i> make no sense here."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["while","since","after","before"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer walked past the door and only then noticed the number, so the two actions are joined by <b>before</b>. <i>After</i> reverses the order, and <i>while</i> and <i>since</i> do not fit the sequence."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","that","which","what"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A relative clause that comments on the whole previous idea, after a comma, uses <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma this way, <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun, and <i>who</i> refers to people."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["stand","line","put","set"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The phrasal verb is <b>line up</b>: to form a line. <i>Set up</i> means organise or prepare, and <i>stand</i> and <i>put</i> do not combine with <i>ourselves up</i> to describe forming a line."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["how","which","where","what"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The question word before <i>to spell</i> must be <b>how</b>, because spelling is a way of doing something. <i>What</i>, <i>which</i> and <i>where</i> would need a different structure or make no sense."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["made","forced","let","allowed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Permission in the passive is <b>be allowed to</b>. <i>Let</i> is not used in the passive with <i>to</i>, and <i>made</i> and <i>forced</i> would mean somebody obliged the students to use phones."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["its","that","the","a"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The comparison is <i>twice <b>the</b> size of</i>. <i>A</i>, <i>its</i> and <i>that</i> do not form this fixed pattern with <i>twice</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["whom","who","whose","which"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pronoun is the subject of <i>sits</i> and refers to a person, so it must be <b>who</b>. <i>Whom</i> is an object form, <i>which</i> is for things, and <i>whose</i> shows possession."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","on","in","at"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed phrase <b>not ... at all</b> adds emphasis to a negative. No other preposition combines with <i>all</i> in this meaning."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-05-4","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Two Days Without a Screen","topic":"a broken phone","passage":"Last Tuesday my phone fell (1)____ of my pocket on the stairs at school, and the screen broke into a hundred tiny pieces. It still switched on, but I could hardly read anything. My first thought was that my parents would be angry, (2)____ the phone was a birthday present from them only three months before. I decided to tell them straight away rather (3)____ hide it, and I am glad I did. My father looked at the screen, sighed, and said that accidents happen to everyone. The next day we (4)____ it to a small repair shop near the market. The man there told us that a new screen would cost about a third of the price of the phone and that it would be ready (5)____ two days. While I was waiting, I discovered how much I depend (6)____ my phone. I could not check the time, message my friends or look (7)____ words in my dictionary app. On the other hand, I finished my homework faster than usual and went to bed earlier. When I picked the phone up on Thursday, it looked (8)____ if nothing had ever happened to it. My father made me buy a proper case (9)____ that it would not happen again. I have learned a lesson (10)____ I hope I will not need to learn twice.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"Something falls <b>out of</b> a pocket; the two-part preposition <i>out of</i> shows movement from inside. <i>Off</i> or <i>from</i> cannot be followed by <i>of</i> here."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["because","as","since","for"],"answer":"because","explanation":"The second clause gives the reason the parents might be angry, so a reason linker such as <b>because</b> (or <i>as</i>, <i>since</i>) fits. <i>So</i> would reverse cause and result, and <i>but</i> would make the two ideas contrast, which they do not."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The fixed pattern is <b>rather than</b> + bare verb: <i>tell them rather than hide it</i>. <i>Then</i> is a common misspelling, and <i>instead</i> would need <i>of hiding</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["took","brought","carried"],"answer":"took","explanation":"You <b>take</b> something <i>to</i> a shop, and the narrative is in the past simple, so <i>took</i>. <i>Went</i> fails because it cannot have <i>it</i> as an object, and the present form <i>take</i> breaks the past narrative."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["in","within","after"],"answer":"in","explanation":"A future point measured from now is expressed with <b>in</b> two days (or <i>within</i>). <i>For two days</i> would describe a duration, not the moment the phone is ready."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["on","upon"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The verb takes a fixed preposition: <b>depend on</b> (or the more formal <i>upon</i>). <i>Of</i> is a common mistake, influenced by <i>independent of</i>, but <i>depend of</i> is not English."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"To search for a word in a dictionary is to <b>look it up</b>. <i>Look for</i> means to try to find something lost, and <i>look at</i> only means to direct your eyes."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The comparison with an imagined situation is <b>as if</b> + past perfect. <i>Like if</i> is not standard English, and <i>even if</i> would change the meaning."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["so"],"answer":"so","explanation":"Purpose is expressed by <b>so that</b> + clause: he made me buy a case so that it would not happen again. <i>In order that</i> is two words, and <i>because that</i> is not English."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["which","that"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A defining relative clause about a thing (<i>a lesson</i>) with no comma takes <b>which</b> or <i>that</i>. <i>What</i> cannot follow a noun as a relative pronoun, and <i>who</i> is for people."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-11-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Picnic Nobody Will Forget","topic":"a picnic gone wrong","passage":"Last month my cousins and I decided to have a picnic (1)____ the lake near my grandmother's village. We had planned everything carefully, or (2)____ we thought. My aunt packed sandwiches, fruit and a big bottle of lemonade, and my brother was (3)____ charge of the blanket and the games. The morning was bright and warm, so we set (4)____ early, laughing and singing on the way. The trouble started as soon as we arrived. First, we discovered that the blanket was still lying on the kitchen table at home. Then, (5)____ we were spreading our jackets on the grass instead, a group of ducks marched over and began to eat our bread. My little cousin tried to chase them away, (6)____ he slipped on the wet bank and fell into the water up to his knees. By midday, dark clouds had gathered, and within minutes it was raining (7)____ hard that we could barely see the path. We ran back to the village, carrying (8)____ was left of the food, and arrived soaked and shivering. My grandmother laughed when she saw us, but she made us hot soup and lent us dry clothes. Looking back, that terrible picnic is probably the day we talk about (9)____ often when the family gets together. Next summer we are going to try again, and this time I (10)____ definitely check the bag twice before we leave.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["inside","among","beside","between"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A picnic takes place <b>beside</b> (next to) a lake. <i>Inside</i> the lake is impossible, and <i>among</i> and <i>between</i> need more than one thing."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["such","as","too","so"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed expression <b>or so we thought</b> means 'that is what we believed, wrongly'. <i>As</i>, <i>such</i> and <i>too</i> do not form this phrase."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","on","by","at"],"correct":0,"explanation":"To be responsible for something is to be <b>in charge of</b> it. No other preposition is used with <i>charge</i> in this meaning."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["up","off","on","down"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Set off</b> means to start a journey. <i>Set up</i> means to arrange or establish something, and <i>set on</i> or <i>set down</i> do not describe leaving."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["during","unless","while","until"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>While</b> introduces a clause with a subject and verb (<i>while we were spreading</i>). <i>During</i> is followed by a noun, not a clause; <i>until</i> and <i>unless</i> give the wrong meaning."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["because","so","although","but"],"correct":3,"explanation":"He tried to chase the ducks, <b>but</b> he slipped: the second half is an unexpected contrast, not a result (<i>so</i>) or a reason (<i>because</i>). <i>Although</i> would need the clauses the other way round."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","too","such","very"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Before an adverb and a <i>that</i>-clause we use <b>so ... that</b>. <i>Such</i> goes with a noun, and <i>too</i> and <i>very</i> cannot be followed by <i>that</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","what","whom","which"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>What</b> means 'the thing(s) that' and can begin a clause with no noun before it. <i>Which</i> and <i>that</i> need a noun to refer back to, and <i>whom</i> is for people."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["much","more","most","many"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The picnic is compared with all the other days the family remembers, so the superlative <b>most often</b> is needed. <i>More often</i> compares only two things, and <i>much</i> and <i>many</i> do not go before <i>often</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["might","would","will","could"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<i>Next summer</i> and <i>are going to</i> place the sentence in the real future, so a promise takes <b>will</b>. <i>Would</i> belongs to an imagined situation, and <i>might</i> and <i>could</i> clash with <i>definitely</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-11-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Nobody Knew My Name","topic":"the first day at a new school","passage":"When my family moved to Da Nang in August, I was worried about starting at a new school (1)____ I knew nobody. On the first morning I woke up two hours (2)____ my alarm rang, put on my new uniform and stared at myself in the mirror. My mother, (3)____ had also changed schools as a child, told me that everybody feels nervous at first and that the feeling would pass. The school was (4)____ bigger than my old one, with three buildings and a large playing field. I got lost twice before I found my classroom, and by the time I sat down the lesson had already started. The teacher asked me to introduce (5)____, and I spoke so quietly that she had to ask me to repeat my name. At break, I stood alone (6)____ the corridor, pretending to read the notices on the wall. Then a girl with short hair walked up and asked (7)____ I wanted to join her table at lunch. Her name was Mai, and it turned (8)____ that she lived only two streets away from our new flat. We talked about music and discovered we liked the same bands. By the end of the day I had been (9)____ to a football team, a book club and a group chat with half the class. My mother was right, of course. (10)____ the time Friday came, I could not remember why I had been so afraid.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","when","which","where"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The clause describes the place in which the writer knew nobody, so the relative adverb <b>where</b> is needed. <i>Which</i> or <i>that</i> would need to be the subject or object of the clause, and <i>when</i> refers to time."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","after","before","since"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A nervous person wakes up early, so <i>two hours</i> <b>before</b> the alarm. <i>After</i> would mean oversleeping, and <i>until</i> and <i>since</i> cannot follow <i>two hours</i> here."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","who","whom","whose"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>had also changed</i> and refers to a person, so it must be <b>who</b>. <i>Whom</i> is an object form, <i>which</i> is for things and <i>whose</i> shows possession."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["much","more","so","very"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A comparative is strengthened with <b>much</b> (<i>much bigger</i>). <i>Very</i> and <i>so</i> go with the plain adjective, and <i>more bigger</i> doubles the comparative."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["my","mine","me","myself"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The subject and the object are the same person, so the reflexive pronoun <b>myself</b> is required. <i>Me</i> is a plain object, <i>mine</i> is possessive and <i>my</i> needs a noun after it."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","by","in","on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A corridor is an enclosed space, so we stand <b>in</b> it. <i>On</i> is for surfaces, <i>at</i> is for points, and <i>by</i> would mean next to it."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["why","whether","that","what"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A reported yes/no question begins with <b>whether</b> (or <i>if</i>). <i>That</i> reports a statement, while <i>what</i> and <i>why</i> report wh-questions."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["out","up","on","over"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>It turned out that</b> means 'it was discovered that'. <i>Turn up</i> means to arrive, and <i>turn on</i> and <i>turn over</i> have unrelated meanings."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["offered","asked","joined","invited"],"correct":3,"explanation":"People are <b>invited to</b> a team or a club. <i>Asked</i> would need <i>to join</i>, <i>joined</i> does not take <i>to</i>, and <i>offered</i> goes with the thing given, not with <i>to a team</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["In","By","At","Until"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>by the time</b> something happens. <i>At</i>, <i>in</i> and <i>until</i> are never used before <i>the time</i> + clause in this way."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-11-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Brown Wallet","topic":"a lost wallet","passage":"Last Saturday I went into town to buy a present (1)____ my father's birthday. I spent an hour in the bookshop, chose a book about mountains, and reached into my pocket to pay. My wallet was gone. I searched every pocket twice and then emptied my bag onto the counter, but there was no sign (2)____ it. The shop assistant was kind and suggested I check the café where I (3)____ been sitting earlier. I ran back, but the table had already been cleared and nobody had handed anything in. My heart sank. The wallet had not only my money in it (4)____ also my student card and a photo of my grandmother. I sat on a bench outside the station, wondering how I was going to tell my parents. Just as I was about to give (5)____, my phone rang. An unknown voice asked whether I was the owner of a brown wallet. A cleaner at the café had found it under a chair and had used my student card to get (6)____ touch with the school, (7)____ then passed on my number. I walked back to the café as fast as I (8)____. The cleaner, an elderly man named Mr Tan, refused to accept any reward. 'Somebody did the same for me once,' he said. I bought the book for my father that afternoon, but I also bought a small cake for Mr Tan. Since then I have (9)____ left home without checking that my wallet is safely zipped inside my bag. It is a lesson I would rather have learnt (10)____ an easier way.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","at","to","of"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A present is bought <b>for</b> an occasion or a person. <i>To</i> would follow <i>give</i>, not <i>buy a present</i>, and <i>of</i> and <i>at</i> do not fit."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["about","of","from","for"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>no sign of</b> something. <i>For</i>, <i>about</i> and <i>from</i> are not used after <i>sign</i> in this sense."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["would","have","had","was"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Sitting in the café happened before the past moment of the story, so the past perfect continuous <b>had been sitting</b> is needed. <i>Have</i> is present, and <i>was</i> or <i>would</i> cannot be followed by <i>been sitting</i> with this meaning."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["or","so","and","but"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>Not only</i> is always paired with <b>but also</b>. <i>And</i>, <i>or</i> and <i>so</i> break the pair."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["up","out","away","in"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Give up</b> means to stop trying. <i>Give in</i> means to surrender to someone, <i>give out</i> means to distribute, and <i>give away</i> means to donate."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["into","in","on","at"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To contact someone is to <b>get in touch with</b> them. The phrase never takes <i>on</i>, <i>at</i> or <i>into</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","who","which","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The clause adds extra information about <i>the school</i>, an organisation, so after the comma we use <b>which</b>. <i>Who</i> is for people, <i>that</i> cannot follow a comma in this kind of clause, and <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["did","can","was","could"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The story is in the past, so <i>as fast as I</i> <b>could</b>. <i>Can</i> is present, and <i>was</i> or <i>did</i> do not complete the comparison naturally."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["never","always","yet","ever"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The writer now always checks, so since that day he has <b>never</b> left without checking. <i>Ever</i> needs a negative or a question, <i>always left without checking</i> says the opposite, and <i>yet</i> goes at the end of a negative sentence."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","on","by","at"],"correct":0,"explanation":"We do something <b>in</b> a certain way. <i>By</i> is used before a method noun without <i>way</i> (<i>by post</i>), and <i>on</i> and <i>at</i> do not go with <i>way</i> here."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-11-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Twenty Minutes in the Chair","topic":"a trip to the dentist","passage":"For weeks I had been (1)____ off a visit to the dentist. A tooth at the back of my mouth hurt every time I drank something cold, but I kept telling myself it would get better (2)____ its own. Of course, it did not. One evening the pain became so bad that I could not sleep, and my mother booked me an appointment (3)____ nine o'clock the next morning. I sat in the waiting room with a magazine I was not really reading, listening to the drill in the next room and feeling (4)____ nervous than I had ever felt before an exam. When my name was called, my legs felt like jelly. The dentist, Dr Huong, was a cheerful woman (5)____ voice was surprisingly calm. She looked at the tooth, took an X-ray and explained that there was a small hole which needed to be filled. 'It will be over (6)____ twenty minutes,' she promised. She gave me an injection so that I would not feel (7)____, and then she got to work. To my surprise, the worst part was the sound, not the pain. I stared at a poster on the ceiling and counted the letters (8)____ it to pass the time. When she finished, she showed me how to brush the back teeth properly and told me to (9)____ down on sweets and fizzy drinks. I walked out into the sunshine feeling foolish for having worried so much. If I had gone earlier, the hole (10)____ have been even smaller. Next time, I will not wait for the pain to tell me.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["calling","putting","setting","taking"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To delay something is to <b>put it off</b>. <i>Call off</i> means to cancel completely, <i>take off</i> is what a plane does, and <i>set off</i> means to leave on a journey."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","at","in","by"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Without help is <b>on its own</b>. The similar phrase with <i>by</i> is <i>by itself</i>, so <i>by its own</i> is wrong, and <i>in</i> and <i>at</i> do not form the expression."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","in","on","at"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Clock times take <b>at</b> (<i>at nine o'clock</i>). <i>On</i> is for days, <i>in</i> for months and parts of the day, and <i>by</i> would mean 'no later than'."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["most","much","more","very"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The word <i>than</i> later in the sentence shows a comparison, so <b>more nervous</b> is needed. <i>Most</i> is the superlative, and <i>much</i> or <i>very</i> cannot be followed by <i>than</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","whose","that","which"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The voice belongs to the woman, so the possessive relative <b>whose</b> is needed before the noun <i>voice</i>. <i>Who</i>, <i>which</i> and <i>that</i> cannot show possession."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","for","since","during"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>In twenty minutes</b> means twenty minutes from now. <i>For</i> gives a length of time something lasts, and <i>during</i> and <i>since</i> do not fit a future point."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["everything","something","nothing","anything"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After <i>not</i> we use <b>anything</b>; <i>not ... nothing</i> is a double negative. <i>Something</i> belongs in positive sentences, and <i>not feel everything</i> changes the meaning."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","at","on","of"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Words printed on a poster are <b>on</b> it, because the poster is a surface. <i>In</i> is for containers, <i>at</i> is for points, and <i>of</i> shows possession."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["bring","cut","turn","put"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To eat less of something is to <b>cut down on</b> it. <i>Put down</i>, <i>turn down</i> and <i>bring down</i> exist, but none of them takes <i>on</i> with this meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["has","had","will","would"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>If I had gone</i> is a third conditional about the past, so the result needs <b>would have been</b>. <i>Will</i> belongs to the first conditional, and <i>had</i> or <i>has</i> cannot come before <i>have been</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-11-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Six Weeks at the Beach Café","topic":"a summer job","passage":"The summer I turned sixteen, I decided that I wanted to earn some money of my (1)____ instead of asking my parents for it every week. My uncle owns a small café near the beach, and he agreed to let me work there (2)____ six weeks. On my first day I arrived at seven o'clock, full of confidence, and by nine I had already dropped a tray of glasses and given two customers the wrong change. My uncle did not shout. He simply handed me a broom and told me that everybody makes mistakes (3)____ first. After that, things slowly got better. I learned how to carry three plates at once, how to remember an order (4)____ writing it down, and how to smile at a customer who had been waiting far too long. The hardest part was not the work itself (5)____ the heat; by the middle of the afternoon the kitchen felt like an oven. At the end of each week my uncle paid me in cash, and I put most of it (6)____ a jar under my bed. I had promised (7)____ that I would save enough for a second-hand guitar. Some of my friends spent the summer sleeping late and playing games, and there (8)____ days when I envied them. But when September came, I had the guitar, a tan, and a pair of arms that were stronger (9)____ they had ever been. I also had something harder to describe: the feeling of having done something useful. If I (10)____ offered the job again next year, I will not hesitate.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["own"],"answer":"own","explanation":"<b>Of my own</b> means belonging to me and nobody else. A word like <i>pocket</i> cannot follow <i>of my</i>; it would need <i>my pocket money</i>, with the noun after it."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"A length of time (<i>six weeks</i>) takes <b>for</b>. <i>During</i> is tempting but is used before the name of a period (<i>during the summer</i>), not before a number of weeks."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>At first</b> means in the beginning. <i>In first</i> and <i>on first</i> do not exist; <i>first</i> alone would not need a preposition, but the blank must be filled."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"The skill is remembering an order and NOT writing it, so <b>without</b> + -ing is needed. <i>Before writing it down</i> would mean he still writes it, which is not the point being made."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["but"],"answer":"but","explanation":"<i>Not X</i> <b>but</b> <i>Y</i> corrects one idea with another. <i>And</i> would make the sentence say both things were hardest, which contradicts <i>not the work itself</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["into","in","inside"],"answer":"into","explanation":"Money is put <b>into</b> (or <i>in</i>, <i>inside</i>) a jar, because the jar is a container. <i>On</i> or <i>at</i> would place the money on the outside of the jar."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["myself"],"answer":"myself","explanation":"The writer made the promise to the same person who made it, so the reflexive <b>myself</b> is required. <i>Me</i> cannot be the object when the subject is <i>I</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["were"],"answer":"were","explanation":"<i>There</i> + verb agrees with the noun after it, and <i>days</i> is plural and the story is in the past, so <b>were</b>. <i>Was</i> fails the agreement and <i>are</i> fails the tense."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"A comparative (<i>stronger</i>) is completed with <b>than</b>. <i>As</i> pairs with <i>as strong</i>, not with the <i>-er</i> form."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["am","get"],"answer":"am","explanation":"<i>Next year</i> and <i>I will not hesitate</i> make this a first conditional, so the <i>if</i>-clause takes a present passive: <b>am offered</b> (informally <i>get offered</i>). <i>Were offered</i> belongs to the second conditional and clashes with <i>will</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-12-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Night I Played the King","topic":"a school play","passage":"Every spring our school puts on a play, and this year I decided to take part (1)____ it for the first time. I did not expect to get a big role, so I was surprised when the drama teacher chose me (2)____ the king. At first I was terrified. I (3)____ never spoken in front of more than thirty people, and the school hall holds nearly four hundred. My best friend told me not to worry, (4)____ she was playing the queen and would be standing next to me the whole time. We rehearsed every afternoon for six weeks. The hardest part was not learning the lines but remembering (5)____ to stand, because the stage was much smaller than the classroom we practised in. Two days before the performance, the boy (6)____ played the messenger fell ill, and a girl from Year 8 had to learn his part overnight. She did (7)____ a good job that nobody in the audience noticed. On the night itself I could hear my heart beating as the curtain went up. Then I said my first line, somebody laughed in the right place, and suddenly I felt calm. The play lasted just over an hour, and when it ended the audience clapped for a long time. (8)____ the people in the front row were my parents, who said they had never seen me look so confident. I still cannot believe how quickly the evening went (9)____. If the teacher asks me again next year, I will certainly say yes, (10)____ if she only gives me the smallest part in the whole play.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","of","on","in"],"correct":3,"explanation":"You <b>take part in</b> an activity. <i>On</i>, <i>at</i> and <i>of</i> never follow <i>take part</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","by","as","like"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Choose somebody as</b> a role means give them that role. <i>Like</i> compares two things, and <i>for</i> or <i>by</i> would need a different sentence."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["have","had","did","was"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The story is in the past, and this experience came before it, so the past perfect <b>had never spoken</b> is needed. <i>Have</i> would describe the present."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["because","so","although","unless"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The second half gives the reason she said not to worry, so <b>because</b> fits. <i>Although</i> and <i>unless</i> would make the reason a contrast or a condition."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","which","when","where"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The problem is the size of the stage, so it is a question of position: <b>where to stand</b>. <i>When</i> would be about timing, and <i>what</i> or <i>which</i> need a noun."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["whom","whose","who","which"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>played</i> and refers to a person, so <b>who</b> is correct. <i>Which</i> is for things and <i>whom</i> can only be an object."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","such","too","very"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Before <i>a</i> + adjective + noun we use <b>such</b>: <i>such a good job that…</i>. <i>So</i> goes directly before an adjective (<i>so good that</i>)."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["Among","Along","Between","Inside"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Among</b> is used for being part of a group of people. <i>Between</i> is for two things, and <i>inside</i> or <i>along</i> describe places, not groups."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","off","up","by"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Time <b>goes by</b> means time passes. <i>Go on</i> means continue, and <i>go off</i> or <i>go up</i> have nothing to do with time passing."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["just","even","only","still"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Even if</b> introduces an extreme condition that does not change the decision. <i>Still</i>, <i>just</i> and <i>only</i> cannot combine with <i>if</i> in this way."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-12-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"A Tent in the Living Room","topic":"a sleepover","passage":"Last Saturday my cousin Mai stayed (1)____ at our flat for the first time. She is a year younger than me, and her mother was nervous about leaving her, so she phoned three times before nine o'clock. We built a tent in the living room using two chairs and a blanket, which took (2)____ longer than we had expected. Then we made popcorn, but I put in far too (3)____ salt, and Mai pretended to like it anyway. My little brother wanted to join us, but he was sent to bed at half past eight (4)____ he had football practice early the next morning. He spent twenty minutes complaining through the wall. After that we watched a film about a lost dog (5)____ finds its way home across a whole country. Mai cried at the end, and I have to admit that I did too. We were supposed to be asleep by eleven, but we (6)____ up talking until well after midnight. Mai told me about a girl in her class who can speak four languages, and I told her about the time I fell into the school pond. At some point my mother knocked on the door and asked (7)____ we knew what time it was. We said sorry and turned off the torch, but we carried (8)____ whispering for at least another hour. In the morning both of us were too tired to eat breakfast properly. Mai's mother arrived at ten and asked whether we (9)____ slept well. We looked at each other and said yes at exactly the same moment. She did not believe us, but she smiled and said Mai could come again (10)____ we promised to go to bed earlier next time.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","off","over","up"],"correct":2,"explanation":"To <b>stay over</b> is to sleep at somebody else's home for a night. <i>Stay up</i> means not go to bed, and <i>stay on</i> means remain longer than planned."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["very","more","too","much"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A comparative like <i>longer</i> is strengthened with <b>much</b> (or <i>far</i>). <i>Very</i> and <i>too</i> go with the base form, and <i>more longer</i> is a double comparative."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["much","many","plenty","lot"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<i>Salt</i> is uncountable, so <b>too much</b> is needed. <i>Too many</i> is for countable nouns, and <i>lot</i> or <i>plenty</i> cannot follow <i>too</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["unless","because","although","so"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Early football practice is the reason for the early bedtime, so <b>because</b> fits. <i>So</i> would make it a result, which is the wrong direction."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["whose","who","which","whom"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The relative pronoun refers to <i>a lost dog</i>, an animal, and is the subject of <i>finds</i>, so <b>which</b> is right. <i>Who</i> and <i>whom</i> are for people."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["held","went","kept","stayed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Stay up</b> means not go to bed. <i>Keep up</i> means maintain, <i>hold up</i> means delay, and <i>go up</i> means rise."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["if","what","that","why"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A reported yes/no question is introduced by <b>if</b> or <i>whether</i>. <i>That</i> reports a statement, and <i>what</i> or <i>why</i> would need a different question."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","on","out","over"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Carry on</b> + -ing means continue doing something. <i>Carry out</i> means perform a task, and <i>carry off</i> or <i>carry over</i> do not fit."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["were","have","had","would"],"correct":2,"explanation":"In reported speech after <i>asked</i>, the present perfect <i>have you slept</i> moves back to the past perfect: <b>had slept</b>. <i>Were slept</i> is not a correct form."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["whether","though","if","unless"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The promise is the condition for coming again, so <b>if</b> is needed. <i>Unless</i> means <i>if not</i>, which reverses the meaning."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-12-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Fried Rice and Onion Tears","topic":"learning to cook","passage":"When my mother started working evening shifts last year, I had two choices: eat instant noodles every night or learn to cook. I chose the second, (1)____ I had never even boiled an egg before. My grandmother agreed to teach me, on condition that I (2)____ the washing-up afterwards. Our first lesson was fried rice. She showed me how to hold the knife so that I would not cut (3)____, and then she watched while I chopped an onion. It took me ten minutes and I cried the whole time. She laughed and said that everybody cries (4)____ first. The rice itself was easier than I had thought. The secret, she explained, is to use rice (5)____ was cooked the day before, because fresh rice turns soft and sticky in the pan. After three weeks I could make four dishes without (6)____ at the recipe, and my father said my soup was better than the one at the restaurant near his office. I think he was being kind, but I did not mind. Now I cook for the family twice a week. It is not always a success. Last month I forgot to turn (7)____ the gas and the vegetables turned black, and once I mixed up sugar and salt in a sauce. (8)____, I have learned that a mistake in the kitchen is not the end of the world. You throw it away, open the window and start again. What I enjoy most is the moment (9)____ everyone sits down and tastes something I made myself. My grandmother says that by the time I finish school I (10)____ be a better cook than she is. I doubt it, but it is nice to hear.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["despite","although","however","whereas"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Never having boiled an egg is a contrast with choosing to cook, and it is a full clause, so <b>although</b> fits. <i>Despite</i> needs a noun or -ing form, and <i>however</i> cannot join two clauses with a comma."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["did","took","made","had"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>do the washing-up</b>, like <i>do the dishes</i> and <i>do the housework</i>. <i>Make</i> is used for creating things, not for chores."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["my","me","mine","myself"],"correct":3,"explanation":"When the subject and the object are the same person, English uses a reflexive pronoun: <b>cut myself</b>. <i>Me</i> is used only when somebody else does the action."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","by","at","in"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>At first</b> means in the early stage of doing something. <i>In</i>, <i>on</i> and <i>by</i> do not combine with <i>first</i> in this meaning."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["whose","which","what","who"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<i>Rice</i> is a thing and is the subject of <i>was cooked</i>, so the relative pronoun is <b>which</b> (or <i>that</i>). <i>What</i> cannot follow a noun in this way."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["looking","look","looked","looks"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After the preposition <i>without</i>, a verb must be in the -ing form: <b>without looking</b>. The other forms cannot follow a preposition."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["over","on","out","off"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The vegetables burned, so the gas was left burning: the writer forgot to <b>turn off</b> the gas. <i>Turn on</i> would mean the opposite, and <i>turn out</i> is for lights."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["Moreover","Therefore","However","Otherwise"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts the failures with a positive lesson, so <b>However</b> is the linker. <i>Therefore</i> gives a result and <i>moreover</i> adds a similar point."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","when","whose","where"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After a time noun like <i>the moment</i>, the relative word is <b>when</b>. <i>Where</i> follows a place, and <i>which</i> would need to be the subject or object of the verb."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["would","was","am","will"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>By the time I finish school</i> points to the future, so the main verb takes <b>will</b>. <i>Would</i> is for hypothetical or past situations."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-12-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Forty Kilometres from the Nearest Garage","topic":"a flat tyre","passage":"My father is a careful driver, but even careful drivers have bad luck sometimes. Last month we were driving to my aunt's house in the countryside when we heard a loud bang and the car began to pull (1)____ the left. Dad slowed down and stopped at the side of the road. The back tyre on the driver's side was completely flat. (2)____ of us had ever changed a tyre before, so Dad opened the boot and took out the instruction book. My mother suggested calling a garage, but the nearest one was forty kilometres (3)____, and there was almost no signal on her phone. So we decided to do it (4)____. The first job was to find the spare tyre, which was hidden under the floor of the boot beneath all our bags. Then Dad had to loosen the nuts on the wheel. They were (5)____ tight that he had to stand on the tool to move them. I held the torch, because by then it was getting dark. Once the nuts were loose, we lifted the car with the jack, took the old wheel (6)____ and pushed the spare one into place. It looked easy in the book, but the spare was heavy and kept slipping. (7)____ the whole thing took almost an hour, and our hands were black with dirt. My aunt, who had been waiting since six, had (8)____ eaten dinner when we finally arrived. She was not angry, though. In fact, she said it was a useful lesson, and that we (9)____ have been much more worried if it had happened at night on a busier road. Since then Dad has kept a pair of gloves in the boot, and I no (10)____ complain when he checks the tyres before a trip.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["to","at","in","on"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A car <b>pulls to</b> one side when it moves in that direction by itself. <i>At</i>, <i>on</i> and <i>in</i> do not show movement towards a side."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["Neither","None","Nobody","Any"],"correct":1,"explanation":"There are three people in the car, so <b>none of us</b> is correct. <i>Neither</i> is only for two, and <i>nobody</i> cannot be followed by <i>of us</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["far","apart","away","behind"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A distance is followed by <b>away</b>: <i>forty kilometres away</i>. <i>Far</i> is not used after a number, and <i>apart</i> is for the gap between two things."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["ours","our","us","ourselves"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Do it ourselves</b> means without help from anyone else, using the reflexive pronoun for <i>we</i>. <i>Us</i> would make the family the object of the action."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","such","too","very"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Before an adjective with a <i>that</i> clause of result, we use <b>so</b>: <i>so tight that…</i>. <i>Such</i> needs a noun, and <i>too</i> or <i>very</i> do not take <i>that</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["away","off","out","down"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You <b>take a wheel off</b> a car, because it is fixed onto the car. <i>Take out</i> is for something inside, and <i>take away</i> means remove from the place."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["Nevertheless","Otherwise","Altogether","Meanwhile"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Altogether</b> introduces the total time, adding all the steps together. <i>Meanwhile</i> means at the same time, and <i>otherwise</i> or <i>nevertheless</i> signal a contrast that is not there."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["yet","ever","still","already"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The aunt finished dinner before they arrived, so <b>had already eaten</b> is right. <i>Yet</i> is used in negatives and questions, and <i>still</i> would mean the action continued."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["would","should","will","must"],"correct":0,"explanation":"With <i>if it had happened</i>, this is a third conditional, so the result takes <b>would have been</b>. <i>Will</i> is for real future conditions."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["longer","sooner","further","more"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>No longer</b> means not any more, and it sits before the verb. <i>No more</i> is placed after the verb, and <i>no sooner</i> or <i>no further</i> have different meanings."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-12-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Everyday Heroes","topic":"a photo competition","passage":"In March our town library announced a photo competition for students (1)____ the age of sixteen. The theme was 'Everyday Heroes', and the winner would have their picture printed on the cover of the library's summer magazine. I had (2)____ entered a competition before, but my art teacher encouraged me to try. For two weeks I carried my camera everywhere. I photographed the man who sells bread outside the station, a nurse waiting for the bus at dawn, and my grandfather fixing a neighbour's bicycle. None of them seemed right. Then one afternoon I saw a girl of about seven helping an old woman (3)____ the road, holding her shopping bag in one hand and the woman's arm in the other. I took the photo quickly, (4)____ they could notice me, and only afterwards did I realise that the girl was a pupil at my old primary school. I printed the picture, wrote a short note (5)____ explained where I had taken it, and handed it in on the last day. Then came the difficult part: waiting. The results were not announced (6)____ the end of April, and every day I checked the library noticeboard on my way home. In the end I did not win. The first prize went (7)____ a boy from another school who had photographed his mother asleep on the sofa after a night shift. I have to admit it was a better picture than (8)____. But my photo was one of ten chosen for the exhibition in the library hall, and the old woman in it came to see it (9)____ her granddaughter. She told me she had never been in a photograph she liked before. That, I think, was worth more (10)____ any prize.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): write ONE word.","accept":["under","below"],"answer":"under","explanation":"<b>Under the age of sixteen</b> means younger than sixteen; <i>below</i> is also used. <i>Younger</i> fails because it would need <i>than</i>, not <i>the age of</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): write ONE word.","accept":["never","not"],"answer":"never","explanation":"<i>But my art teacher encouraged me</i> shows a lack of experience, so the negative <b>never</b> (or <i>not</i>) fits with <i>had entered … before</i>. <i>Ever</i> fails because it is only used in questions and negatives, not in a positive statement."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): write ONE word.","accept":["across","over"],"answer":"across","explanation":"You <b>help somebody across the road</b>, from one side to the other; British English also says <i>over the road</i>. <i>Through</i> fails because a road is crossed, not entered."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): write ONE word.","accept":["before"],"answer":"before","explanation":"She took the picture quickly so that it happened <b>before</b> the pair could notice her. <i>Until</i> fails because the photo was a single quick action, not something continuing up to a point."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): write ONE word.","accept":["which","that"],"answer":"which","explanation":"The relative pronoun refers to <i>a short note</i>, a thing, and is the subject of <i>explained</i>, so <b>which</b> or <i>that</i> is needed. <i>Who</i> fails because a note is not a person."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): write ONE word.","accept":["until","till","before"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<i>Not … </i><b>until</b> the end of April means the results came only at that time; <i>till</i> and <i>before</i> also work. <i>Since</i> fails because it marks a starting point, not an end point."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): write ONE word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"A prize <b>goes to</b> the person who wins it. <i>For</i> fails because <i>go for</i> means choose or attack, not be awarded."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): write ONE word.","accept":["mine"],"answer":"mine","explanation":"The comparison is with the writer's own picture, so the possessive pronoun <b>mine</b> stands alone. <i>My</i> fails because it needs a noun after it."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): write ONE word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"She came <b>with</b> her granddaughter, meaning they came together. <i>And</i> fails because it would make the granddaughter a second object of <i>see</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): write ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"After the comparative <i>more</i>, the second part of the comparison is introduced by <b>than</b>. <i>Then</i> fails because it is a time word, a common spelling confusion."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-13-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Balconies Full of Pots","topic":"a garden in the city","passage":"When my grandmother moved to the tenth floor of a block of flats, everyone thought she would miss her old garden terribly. She had spent forty years growing vegetables in the countryside, and the city seemed a strange place (1)____ someone like her. For the first few weeks she said very little. Then one morning she came home with three bags of soil and a box of seeds, and (2)____ the end of the day the balcony was full of pots. My mother was worried that the neighbours would complain (3)____ the mess, but the opposite happened. The old man next door asked if he could grow tomatoes too, and soon the family on the floor below joined (4)____. By summer, half the building had turned its balconies into tiny farms. My grandmother, (5)____ had never used a phone for anything but calls, now sends photos of her chillies to a group chat with twenty members. She gives advice on how to water the plants in the hot season, and she never gets tired (6)____ answering questions. Last month the building manager put up a notice (7)____ everyone for the greenery, and he even suggested a shared garden on the roof. (8)____ the project goes ahead, my grandmother will certainly be in charge. I asked her whether she still missed the countryside. She looked at the rows of green leaves and said she had (9)____ felt so at home. It turns out that a garden does not need a great deal of land; it only needs someone who refuses to (10)____ up.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","with","of","at"],"correct":0,"explanation":"We say a place is strange, difficult or perfect <b>for</b> somebody, meaning it suits or does not suit them. <i>Of</i>, <i>with</i> and <i>at</i> do not follow <i>a strange place</i> in this sense."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","by","until","at"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>By the end of the day</b> means not later than that point, which is what the sentence describes. <i>Until</i> would mean the balcony stayed full only up to that time, and <i>at</i> or <i>in</i> do not combine with <i>the end of the day</i> here."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","with","about","for"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The verb is <b>complain about</b> something. You can complain <i>to</i> a person, but the problem itself always takes <i>about</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["up","out","on","in"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Join in</b> means to take part in an activity that others have started. <i>Join up</i> means to enlist in the army, and <i>join on</i> or <i>join out</i> are not phrasal verbs."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","whom","whose","which"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The relative clause has its own subject role, so we need <b>who</b> for a person. <i>Whom</i> is only for an object, <i>whose</i> needs a noun after it, and <i>which</i> is for things."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["from","of","at","with"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Get tired of doing</b> something means to lose patience with it. <i>Tired from</i> describes the cause of physical tiredness, and <i>with</i> and <i>at</i> do not follow <i>tired</i> before an -ing form."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["thank","thanks","thanking","thanked"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A present participle clause describes the notice: <i>a notice <b>thanking</b> everyone</i>. <i>Thanked</i> would make the notice the receiver of thanks, and the bare verb <i>thank</i> or the noun <i>thanks</i> cannot follow <i>a notice</i> directly."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["Although","Whether","Unless","If"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence is a first conditional: <b>if</b> the plan happens, she will lead it. <i>Unless</i> would reverse the meaning, <i>although</i> expects a contrast, and <i>whether</i> cannot introduce a condition."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["never","already","yet","ever"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<i>She had <b>never</b> felt so at home</i> means this was the happiest she had been anywhere. <i>Ever</i> is used in questions, and <i>already</i> and <i>yet</i> do not fit the meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["give","put","keep","turn"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Give up</b> means to stop trying, which is what a gardener must refuse to do. <i>Keep up</i>, <i>turn up</i> and <i>put up</i> exist but have different meanings."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-13-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Kitten Under the Car","topic":"a rescued kitten","passage":"It was raining hard on the evening my brother found the kitten. He was cycling home from football practice (1)____ he heard a thin cry coming from under a parked car. At first he thought it was a bird, but when he bent down he saw two frightened eyes staring back (2)____ him. The kitten was so small that it fitted in one hand, and it was shaking with cold. My brother wrapped it in his jacket and rode the rest of the way with one hand, (3)____ is something my parents have told him never to do again. When he walked into the kitchen, my mother was about to shout at him for being late. Then she saw the tiny grey head and forgot everything else. We dried the kitten with a towel and gave it (4)____ warm milk, although the vet later told us that milk is not really good for cats. That night nobody (5)____ asleep before midnight, because the kitten cried every time we put it down. The next day we took it to the animal clinic, (6)____ it was checked, given an injection and declared healthy. The vet asked whether we planned to keep it. My father said no at once, but he was the one who spent the whole weekend building a little bed (7)____ of an old box. We named her Mưa, which means rain, (8)____ of the weather on the night she was found. She has now lived with us for six months and has grown into a confident cat (9)____ favourite place is the middle of the sofa. My brother still says he did not really rescue her. (10)____ his opinion, she rescued him.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","when","while","since"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A past continuous action is interrupted by a short past simple one: <i>he was cycling <b>when</b> he heard</i>. <i>While</i> introduces the longer action, not the short one, and <i>until</i> and <i>since</i> give the wrong time meaning."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","for","to","on"],"correct":0,"explanation":"You <b>stare at</b> somebody or something. The other prepositions never follow <i>stare</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","that","who","which"],"correct":3,"explanation":"After a comma, <b>which</b> can refer back to a whole action (riding with one hand). <i>That</i> cannot be used after a comma in this way, <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun, and <i>who</i> is for people."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["many","any","some","few"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<i>Milk</i> is uncountable and the sentence is positive, so <b>some</b> is the quantifier. <i>Any</i> is for questions and negatives, and <i>few</i> and <i>many</i> only go with countable nouns."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["went","fell","got","came"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed expression is <b>fall asleep</b>. <i>Go to sleep</i> exists, but <i>go asleep</i>, <i>get asleep</i> and <i>come asleep</i> do not."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["where","which","when","that"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The clause tells us what happened <b>at</b> the clinic, so the relative adverb <b>where</b> is needed. <i>Which</i> or <i>that</i> would need to be the subject or object of the clause, and <i>when</i> is for a time."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["up","away","off","out"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Something is made or built <b>out of</b> the material it comes from. <i>Up</i>, <i>off</i> and <i>away</i> do not combine with <i>of</i> to show material."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["instead","in case","because","in spite"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Because of</b> gives the reason for the name. <i>Instead of</i> and <i>in spite of</i> would give the wrong meaning, and <i>in case of</i> is used for possible future events."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","whose","that","which"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A noun (<i>favourite place</i>) follows the blank, so the possessive relative <b>whose</b> is needed. <i>Who</i>, <i>which</i> and <i>that</i> would have to be followed by a verb."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["On","By","At","In"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in my opinion</b> / <b>in his opinion</b>. The other prepositions are never used with <i>opinion</i> in this way."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-13-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Before the Sun Comes Up","topic":"my uncle the fisherman","passage":"My uncle Tám has been a fisherman (1)____ he was fourteen, and he says the sea has taught him more than any teacher. He lives in a small coastal town about three hours south of our city, and every summer I spend two weeks (2)____ him. His day starts long before sunrise. While the rest of the town is still asleep, he is already checking the nets and listening to the weather report (3)____ the radio. If the wind is (4)____ strong to go out, he stays at home and repairs equipment instead. He learned this lesson the hard way, after a storm nearly sank his boat twenty years ago. Once we are out on the water, my uncle hardly speaks. He keeps an (5)____ on the colour of the sea and the movement of the birds, and somehow he knows exactly where to throw the nets. I used to think this was luck, but now I understand it is the result of years of paying (6)____ to small details. The fish we catch are sold at the market the same morning. My aunt (7)____ care of the money, because my uncle admits he is hopeless with numbers. Life is not easy for fishermen these days. There are (8)____ fish than there were when my uncle was young, and the price of fuel keeps going up. Some of his friends have already given up and moved to the city to work in factories. My uncle refuses to do the same. He says that if he could choose again, he (9)____ still pick the sea. Whenever I ask him why, he simply points at the horizon, (10)____ the sun is coming up over the water, and says nothing at all.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["from","when","since","for"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The present perfect with a starting point (<i>he was fourteen</i>) takes <b>since</b>. <i>For</i> needs a length of time, and <i>from</i> or <i>when</i> do not work with <i>has been</i> here."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["to","by","at","with"],"correct":3,"explanation":"You <b>spend time with</b> a person. <i>At</i> would need a place, and <i>by</i> or <i>to</i> do not follow <i>spend two weeks</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","at","by","in"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Programmes are <b>on the radio</b> or <b>on television</b>. <i>In</i>, <i>at</i> and <i>by</i> are not used for broadcasts."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","too","very","enough"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pattern <b>too</b> + adjective + <i>to</i> + verb shows that something is more than acceptable: <i>too strong to go out</i>. <i>Very</i> and <i>so</i> cannot be followed directly by <i>to</i> + verb, and <i>enough</i> comes after the adjective."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["mind","hand","eye","ear"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Keep an eye on</b> something means to watch it carefully. <i>Keep an ear</i>, <i>keep a hand</i> and <i>keep a mind on</i> are not English expressions."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["interest","care","notice","attention"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>pay attention to</b>. We <i>take notice of</i> and <i>take care of</i>, and <i>interest</i> is not paid to something."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["takes","gets","makes","keeps"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>take care of</b> something. <i>Make</i>, <i>get</i> and <i>keep</i> do not combine with <i>care of</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["less","fewer","little","few"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<i>Fish</i> is countable here and the sentence compares with <i>than</i>, so the comparative <b>fewer</b> is needed. <i>Less</i> is for uncountable nouns, and <i>little</i> and <i>few</i> are not comparatives."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["can","shall","would","will"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The <i>if</i>-clause uses <i>could</i>, so this is a second conditional and the main clause takes <b>would</b>. <i>Will</i>, <i>shall</i> and <i>can</i> belong to real, first-conditional sentences."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","when","where","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The clause describes what is happening <b>at</b> the horizon, so the relative adverb <b>where</b> is correct. <i>Which</i> or <i>that</i> would need to be the subject of <i>is coming up</i>, which does not make sense."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-13-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"When the Final Bell Rang","topic":"the last day of term","passage":"Nobody does much work on the last day of term, and this year was no exception. Our teacher had promised that we would watch a film if we finished cleaning the classroom (1)____ ten o'clock, so for once everybody arrived early. Desks were pushed against the wall, the floor was swept, and Minh found a sandwich at the back of a cupboard that must have been there (2)____ March. By half past nine the room looked (3)____ cleaner than it had all year. The film was one I had seen before, but I did not mind (4)____ it again with the whole class laughing at the same moments. Afterwards we signed each other's notebooks. Some people wrote long messages; others just drew a face and their name. I noticed that even the students (5)____ had argued all year were suddenly friendly with each other. At lunchtime the head teacher made a speech about summer being a time to rest and read. Not (6)____ of us were listening, to be honest, because the ice-cream van had just parked outside the gate. In the afternoon there was a football match between the teachers and the students. The teachers lost 5–1, (7)____ they claimed the referee was on our side. When the final bell rang, nobody rushed to leave. We stood around in the yard, (8)____ our goodbyes and promising to meet up in the holidays. I know from experience that (9)____ of those promises will be kept, but it did not matter. Walking home, I felt happy and a little sad (10)____ the same time, which I suppose is exactly how the last day of anything should feel.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","on","at","by"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A deadline for finishing something is expressed with <b>by</b>: <i>finish it by ten</i>. <i>Until</i> describes how long an action continues, and <i>at</i> would mean the exact moment of finishing, not a limit."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","from","since","ago"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<i>Must have been there</i> is a perfect form and <i>March</i> is a point in time, so <b>since</b> is needed. <i>For</i> needs a length of time, and <i>ago</i> follows the time expression."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","much","more","very"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A comparative such as <i>cleaner</i> is strengthened with <b>much</b> (or <i>far</i>). <i>Very</i> and <i>so</i> go with the plain adjective, and <i>more cleaner</i> is a double comparative."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["watching","watch","to watch","watched"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Mind</b> is followed by an -ing form: <i>I did not mind watching</i>. It never takes an infinitive or a bare verb."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["whose","which","whom","who"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>had argued</i> and refers to people, so <b>who</b> is correct. <i>Whom</i> is only for an object, <i>whose</i> needs a noun after it, and <i>which</i> is for things."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["little","much","many","few"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<i>Us</i> refers to countable people and the phrase is negative, so <b>not many of us</b> is the natural form. <i>Much</i> and <i>little</i> are for uncountable nouns, and <i>not few</i> is not used."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["despite","although","because","unless"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Losing the match and blaming the referee are a contrast, so <b>although</b> is needed. <i>Despite</i> cannot be followed by a subject and verb, and <i>because</i> and <i>unless</i> give the wrong meaning."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["saying","speaking","telling","talking"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>say goodbye</b> / <b>say our goodbyes</b>. <i>Tell</i> needs a person as its object, and <i>speak</i> and <i>talk</i> are not used with <i>goodbye</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["less","fewer","little","few"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>Promises</i> are countable and the writer means <i>not many</i>, so <b>few of those promises</b> is right. <i>Little</i> is for uncountable nouns, and <i>less</i> and <i>fewer</i> are comparatives with nothing to compare."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","at","on","in"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>at the same time</b>. <i>In</i>, <i>on</i> and <i>by</i> are never used with <i>the same time</i> in this meaning."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-13-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Paid for with Washed Cups","topic":"a new phone","passage":"For two years my phone had a cracked screen, a battery that died by lunchtime and a camera (1)____ turned every photo slightly green. My friends laughed at it, but I did not want to ask my parents for a new one. So last spring I made a plan. I found a weekend job at my aunt's café and started saving every đồng I earned. It took (2)____ longer than I expected. Some weeks there were so (3)____ customers that my aunt could not give me any hours. Other weeks I was so busy I barely had time to eat. By August I had finally saved (4)____ money for the phone I wanted. The first thing I did was take a photo of my aunt's café, a proper one without the green colour. Having a new phone has changed a few things. I no longer worry about the battery (5)____ out in the middle of the day, and my photos actually look like the places I visited. But the biggest change is not about the phone itself. I (6)____ care of it far more carefully than I ever did with the old one, because I know exactly how many hours of washing cups it cost. My little brother has already asked if he can have my old phone. I told him yes, (7)____ condition that he promises to keep it away from water. He agreed at once, (8)____ I doubt he will remember by next week. My parents were surprised that I had managed to save so (9)____ on my own. My father said he would have helped if I had asked, and I believe him. Still, I am glad I did it myself. (10)____ you buy something with your own money, you understand its real value.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["which","that"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A defining relative clause about a thing (<i>a camera</i>) takes <b>which</b> or <b>that</b> as its subject. <i>Who</i> fails because a camera is not a person, and <i>what</i> is never a relative pronoun."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["much","far","even","rather","way","slightly","somewhat","considerably","significantly"],"answer":"much","explanation":"A comparative (<i>longer</i>) is strengthened with <b>much</b> or <b>far</b> (<i>even</i>, <i>rather</i>, <i>slightly</i> and <i>somewhat</i> also work). <i>Very</i> fails because it only modifies the plain adjective: <i>very long</i>, never <i>very longer</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["few"],"answer":"few","explanation":"<i>Customers</i> are countable, so <b>so few customers that</b> is the pattern. <i>Little</i> fails because it is only used with uncountable nouns."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["enough","the","sufficient"],"answer":"enough","explanation":"<b>Enough</b> goes before a noun to show the amount needed: <i>enough money for the phone</i>. <i>Much</i> fails because <i>saved much money</i> is not used in a positive statement, and <i>many</i> fails because <i>money</i> is uncountable."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["running","giving"],"answer":"running","explanation":"A battery <b>runs out</b> when its power is finished, and after <i>worry about the battery</i> the verb takes the -ing form. <i>Going out</i> fails because it is used for lights and fires, not for a battery."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["take"],"answer":"take","explanation":"The collocation is <b>take care of</b> something, in the present simple because it is a habit. <i>Make</i> fails because <i>make care</i> is not English, and <i>keep</i> fails because <i>keep care of</i> is not a phrase."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The fixed expression is <b>on condition that</b>, meaning <i>only if</i>. <i>In</i> or <i>under</i> fail because they do not form this phrase before <i>condition that</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["but","although","though","yet"],"answer":"but","explanation":"The two halves contrast (he agreed, the writer doubts him), so a contrast linker such as <b>but</b>, <b>although</b> or <b>though</b> is needed. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> fail because they express result or reason, not contrast."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["much"],"answer":"much","explanation":"After <i>save so</i> with no noun, the quantity of money is expressed with <b>much</b>: <i>save so much</i>. <i>Many</i> fails because money is uncountable, and <i>a lot</i> is two words."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["When","when","If","if","Once","once","Whenever","whenever"],"answer":"When","explanation":"A general truth uses a zero conditional: <b>when</b> (or <i>if</i>, <i>once</i>) + present, present. <i>Because</i> fails because the sentence describes a condition, not a reason, and <i>although</i> fails because there is no contrast."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-14-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Lanterns on the Village Road","topic":"a village wedding","passage":"When my cousin Hoa announced that she was getting married in her husband's village, the whole family agreed to travel there together. The village is (1)____ far from our town that we had to leave before dawn. My grandmother, (2)____ had not left the city for ten years, sat by the window and pointed at every rice field we passed. When we arrived, the big red tent (3)____ already been put up in the yard, and dozens of neighbours were busy carrying chairs and bowls. Nobody in the village seemed to be at work that day; (4)____, everybody had come to help. My uncle told me that in the countryside a wedding is not a private event (5)____ a celebration for the whole community. Around eleven o'clock the groom's family walked slowly up the road, carrying red boxes of tea, fruit and cake. I had never (6)____ such a long procession, and I could not stop taking photos. After the ceremony we sat at round tables (7)____ the trees and were served plate after plate of food. The old men sang, the children ran (8)____ and down the path, and my grandmother laughed more than I had heard her laugh in years. By the time we left, it (9)____ getting dark and the lanterns along the road had been lit. My cousin thanked us for coming, but honestly it was us (10)____ should have thanked her.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["too","so","very","such"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pattern is <b>so + adjective + that</b>: <i>so far that we had to leave early</i>. <i>Too</i> takes <i>to</i>, <i>such</i> needs a noun, and <i>very</i> cannot introduce a <i>that</i> clause."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","whose","which","whom"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>had not left</i> and refers to a person, so it must be <b>who</b>. <i>Whom</i> is an object form, <i>which</i> is for things and <i>whose</i> shows possession."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["has","was","have","had"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The tent was put up before we arrived, so the past perfect passive <b>had already been put up</b> is needed. <i>Has</i> would be present perfect, <i>have</i> does not agree with the singular <i>tent</i>, and <i>was been put</i> is not a possible verb form."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["therefore","otherwise","instead","however"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Nobody was at work; <b>instead</b>, they were helping with the wedding. <i>However</i> would signal a contradiction, <i>therefore</i> a result and <i>otherwise</i> a condition, none of which fits."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["and","but","or","so"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Not A but B</b> corrects one idea with another: <i>not a private event but a community celebration</i>. <i>And</i>, <i>or</i> and <i>so</i> do not form this contrast."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["seen","seeing","saw","see"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After <i>had never</i> the past participle is required: <b>had never seen</b>. <i>Saw</i> is the simple past and <i>see</i>/<i>seeing</i> cannot follow <i>had</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["over","above","across","under"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Tables are placed <b>under</b> the trees, in their shade. <i>Over</i> and <i>above</i> would put the tables higher than the trees, and <i>across</i> does not describe position here."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["over","off","up","on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The fixed pair is <b>up and down</b>: <i>the children ran up and down the path</i>. None of the other words pairs with <i>down</i> in this way."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["is","was","were","has"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The story is in the past, and <i>it</i> takes the singular: <b>it was getting dark</b>. <i>Is</i> is the wrong tense, <i>were</i> is plural and <i>has</i> cannot go with <i>getting</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["whose","which","whom","who"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In the cleft sentence <i>it was us who should have thanked her</i>, the pronoun is the subject of <i>should have thanked</i> and refers to people, so <b>who</b> is correct. <i>Whom</i> cannot be a subject."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-14-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Fourteenth Across the Line","topic":"the bicycle race","passage":"Every spring the town where I live holds a bicycle race (1)____ the lake, and this year I decided to take part for the first time. I had been training (2)____ February, riding to school the long way round and climbing the hill behind our house every weekend. My father, who used to race when he was young, gave me plenty of advice, (3)____ most of it was about eating breakfast. On the morning of the race, more than two hundred riders lined up at the start. I was so nervous that I could hardly hold the handlebars. When the whistle blew, everyone rushed forward (4)____ once, and for the first few minutes I simply tried not to fall off. The road was flat at first, but after five kilometres it began to climb, and one by one the riders in front of me slowed (5)____. This was the part I had practised, so I kept my rhythm and passed at least twenty people before the top. On the way down, however, a girl in a yellow shirt flew past me as if I were standing still, and I never caught (6)____ with her again. I crossed the line in fourteenth place, (7)____ was far better than I had expected. My father was waiting near the finish with a bottle of water and a huge grin. \"Not bad,\" he said, \"but you (8)____ have eaten a bigger breakfast.\" Later I found out that the girl in yellow had won the whole race. Next year I intend to train (9)____ harder, and I have already told my father that there is no (10)____ to worry about my breakfast.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["around","into","across","through"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A race that follows the shore of a lake goes <b>around</b> it. Bicycles cannot go <i>across</i> or <i>through</i> water, and <i>into</i> would mean entering the lake."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","since","for","during"],"correct":1,"explanation":"With the present perfect continuous and a starting point, we use <b>since February</b>. <i>For</i> needs a length of time, <i>during</i> would limit the training to that month, and <i>until</i> gives an end point."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["despite","because","although","however"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Although</b> joins two clauses in one sentence to show contrast. <i>However</i> would need a full stop or semicolon before it, <i>despite</i> takes a noun, and <i>because</i> gives a reason, not a contrast."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","on","in","at"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>At once</b> means immediately, all at the same moment. <i>For once</i> means <i>for this one time</i>, which does not describe the start of a race, and <i>in</i>/<i>on once</i> do not exist."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["down","over","up","off"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>slow down</b> means to go more slowly, which is what riders do on a hill. <i>Slow up</i>, <i>slow off</i> and <i>slow over</i> are not standard."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","up","on","out"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Catch up with</b> someone means to reach them after they have gone ahead. <i>Catch on</i> means to understand, and <i>catch out</i>/<i>catch off</i> do not fit."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","it","which","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After a comma, a relative clause that comments on the whole previous idea uses <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma in this way, <i>what</i> would need no antecedent, and <i>it</i> would create two sentences joined only by a comma."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["may","would","must","should"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The father is criticising a past action: <b>should have eaten</b> means it was the right thing to do but was not done. <i>Must have</i> and <i>may have</i> express a guess about what happened, not a criticism, and <i>would have</i> needs an <i>if</i> condition."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["even","too","very","more"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Even harder</b> strengthens a comparative. <i>Very</i> and <i>too</i> cannot go before a comparative, and <i>more harder</i> is a double comparative."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["need","point","use","worth"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>There is no need to</b> + verb is the fixed pattern. <i>No point</i> and <i>no use</i> are followed by <i>in + -ing</i>, and <i>worth</i> is used as <i>it is worth -ing</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-14-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"A Knock in the Rain","topic":"an unexpected visitor","passage":"It was raining hard on Sunday evening, and I was alone in the house (1)____ my parents were at my aunt's. I had just settled down on the sofa with a bowl of noodles when somebody knocked (2)____ the door. I froze. We were not expecting anyone, and the knock came again, louder this time. I told myself there was nothing to be afraid (3)____ and walked to the door. Through the glass I could see a small figure in a wet raincoat. When I opened the door, an old woman looked up at me and asked (4)____ she could use our telephone. Her car had broken down at the corner, and her mobile phone had run out of battery. I let her in, gave her a towel and (5)____ her a cup of hot tea while she called her son. She turned out to be a retired music teacher who had once taught at my school. We talked for nearly an hour, and she told me stories about teachers (6)____ had left long before I was born. By the time her son arrived, the rain had almost stopped. She thanked me twice and said that not many teenagers (7)____ have opened the door to a stranger. A week later a small parcel arrived (8)____ my name on it. Inside was a second-hand book of piano music and a note that said, \"For the young man who let me in.\" I still cannot play the piano, (9)____ I keep the book on my shelf. Whenever my parents go out now, I (10)____ longer feel nervous when someone knocks.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","although","unless","because"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The second clause gives the reason for being alone, so <b>because</b> is needed. <i>So</i> would make it a result, <i>although</i> a contrast, and <i>unless</i> a condition."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["to","by","on","in"],"correct":2,"explanation":"You <b>knock on</b> (or <i>at</i>) a door. <i>Knock in</i>, <i>knock to</i> and <i>knock by</i> are not used for this action."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["from","of","about","with"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The adjective takes a fixed preposition: <b>afraid of</b> something. <i>Afraid from</i>, <i>afraid about</i> and <i>afraid with</i> are all incorrect."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["whether","why","that","what"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A reported yes/no question is introduced by <b>whether</b> (or <i>if</i>). <i>That</i> reports a statement, and <i>what</i>/<i>why</i> would need a question word in the original."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["did","cooked","boiled","made"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>make someone a cup of tea</b>. We <i>boil</i> water and <i>cook</i> food, but we do not <i>boil</i>, <i>cook</i> or <i>do</i> a cup of tea for someone."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["whom","whose","who","which"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The pronoun is the subject of <i>had left</i> and refers to teachers, so <b>who</b> is correct. <i>Which</i> is for things, <i>whom</i> is an object form and <i>whose</i> needs a noun after it."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["will","would","should","must"],"correct":1,"explanation":"She is imagining what other teenagers <b>would have</b> done in the same situation, so this is a hypothetical past. <i>Should have</i> would mean it was their duty, <i>must have</i> a deduction, and <i>will have</i> refers to the future."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["with","from","by","of"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A parcel arrives <b>with</b> a name written on it; <i>with</i> describes what it carries. <i>By</i> and <i>from</i> would show the sender, and <i>of my name</i> makes no sense."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","because","or","but"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Not being able to play and still keeping the book are contrasting ideas, joined by <b>but</b>. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would suggest one causes the other, and <i>or</i> offers a choice."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["any","no","not","never"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed expression is <b>no longer</b>, meaning not any more. <i>Not longer</i> is incorrect here, <i>never longer</i> does not exist, and <i>any longer</i> is only used after a negative verb."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-14-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Sleeping Under the Wooden Boat","topic":"a museum at night","passage":"Last month our history teacher, Mr Tran, arranged something (1)____ of us had ever done before: a night at the city museum. Twenty students were chosen, and I was lucky (2)____ to be one of them. We arrived at eight o'clock with sleeping bags and torches, just as the last daytime visitors were leaving. A guide (3)____ Mai met us at the entrance and explained the rules. We were not allowed to touch anything, and we had to stay together (4)____ all times. The museum feels completely different after dark. In the daytime it is full of noise, but at night it is so quiet (5)____ you can hear your own footsteps on the stone floor. The first hall we visited was the one with the ancient drums, and Mai told us that the largest of them (6)____ found in a river more than sixty years ago. Some of my classmates whispered that the drum looked (7)____ if it were watching us. At around eleven we spread our sleeping bags on the floor of the main hall, under a huge wooden boat. I did not think I would sleep at all, but I must have (8)____ off, because the next thing I remember is Mai shaking my shoulder at six. Before we left, she let us look at a few objects that are usually kept (9)____ of sight in the storerooms. It was the best school activity I have ever taken part in, and I would go again (10)____ they asked me.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["nobody","neither","none","no"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Before <i>of us</i> the pronoun must be <b>none</b>. <i>No</i> needs a noun, <i>nobody</i> is not followed by <i>of</i>, and <i>neither</i> is only for two people."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","very","too","enough"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Adjective + enough + to</b>: <i>lucky enough to be chosen</i>. <i>Too</i> would mean the luck prevented it, and <i>so</i>/<i>very</i> cannot be followed directly by <i>to</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["called","calls","calling","call"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A reduced passive relative clause uses the past participle: <i>a guide (who was) <b>called</b> Mai</i>. <i>Calling</i> would mean the guide was doing the calling, and <i>call</i>/<i>calls</i> cannot follow a noun like this."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","at","in","on"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>at all times</b>, meaning always. <i>In</i>, <i>on</i> and <i>for</i> do not combine with <i>all times</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","than","that","which"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>So + adjective + that</b> introduces the result: <i>so quiet that you can hear</i>. <i>Which</i> and <i>than</i> do not follow <i>so quiet</i>, and a second <i>so</i> would be ungrammatical."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["has","had","is","was"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The drum did not find anything; it <b>was found</b>, a past simple passive with the time phrase <i>sixty years ago</i>. <i>Has found</i> and <i>had found</i> are active, and <i>is found</i> is the wrong tense."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["as","such","like","even"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>As if</b> introduces an imagined comparison: <i>looked as if it were watching us</i>. <i>Like if</i> is not English, and <i>even if</i> and <i>such if</i> do not fit after <i>looked</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["slept","dropped","fallen","gone"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Drop off</b> (like <i>doze off</i> or <i>nod off</i>) means to fall asleep. <i>Fall off</i> means to fall from something, <i>go off</i> is what an alarm does, and <i>sleep off</i> is not a phrasal verb here."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["away","back","out","off"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The expression is <b>out of sight</b>, meaning where nobody can see. <i>Off</i>, <i>away</i> and <i>back</i> do not combine with <i>of sight</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","unless","if","whether"],"correct":2,"explanation":"This is a second conditional: <i>I would go again <b>if</b> they asked me</i>. <i>Unless</i> would reverse the meaning, <i>whether</i> does not form a condition, and <i>until</i> gives a time limit."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-14-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Be Patient and Be Kind","topic":"my favourite teacher","passage":"Of all the teachers I have had, the one I remember (1)____ clearly than any other is Mrs Lan, who taught me maths in grade seven. She was not the kind of teacher (2)____ shouts or gives extra homework as a punishment. Instead, she had a quiet way of making everyone feel that they mattered. On my first day in her class, I was so bad at fractions that I could not even add two of them (3)____. Most teachers would have moved on to the next topic, but Mrs Lan stayed after school (4)____ help me twice a week for a whole month. She never once made me feel stupid. By the end of the term I was getting the highest marks (5)____ the class, and to this day I do not know how she did it. What I liked most was that she treated mistakes as something interesting rather (6)____ something shameful. If a student gave a wrong answer, she would ask the whole class to work out where the thinking had gone wrong. In that way we learned (7)____ from our mistakes than from the textbook. Mrs Lan left our school two years (8)____ to look after her elderly mother in the countryside. I have not seen her (9)____ then, but I wrote to her last month to tell her that I want to become a teacher myself. She wrote back a short letter which ended with the words \"Be patient and be kind.\" I have kept that letter in my desk, and I read it (10)____ time I feel like giving up.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["more"],"answer":"more","explanation":"The following <i>than any other</i> requires a comparative, so <b>more clearly</b> is the only fit. <i>Most</i> would be a superlative, which cannot be followed by <i>than</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["who","that"],"answer":"who","explanation":"A relative pronoun acting as the subject of <i>shouts</i> and referring to a person: <b>who</b> (or <i>that</i>). <i>Which</i> is not used for people, and <i>whom</i> cannot be a subject."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["together","up"],"answer":"together","explanation":"You <b>add</b> two numbers <b>together</b> (or <i>add them up</i>). <i>Add them on</i> or <i>add them in</i> would need something else to add them to."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The infinitive of purpose <b>to help me</b> explains why she stayed. <i>For</i> is tempting, but <i>for help me</i> is not English; <i>for</i> would need <i>helping</i> or a noun."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["in","of"],"answer":"in","explanation":"A superlative is usually followed by <b>in</b> before a place or group: <i>the highest marks in the class</i>. <i>Of</i> is also accepted, but <i>at</i> or <i>from the class</i> is wrong."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The fixed pair is <b>rather than</b>, meaning instead of. <i>Rather then</i> is a common spelling mistake, and <i>rather to</i> does not exist."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["more","better"],"answer":"more","explanation":"The <i>than</i> later in the sentence needs a comparative before it: <i>learned <b>more</b> (or <i>better</i>) from our mistakes than from the textbook</i>. <i>Much</i> or <i>a lot</i> on their own cannot be followed by <i>than</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["ago","back"],"answer":"ago","explanation":"With the past simple <i>left</i>, a length of time is followed by <b>ago</b> (informally <i>back</i>). <i>Before</i> is used with the past perfect, and <i>since</i> would need a point in time, not two years."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["since"],"answer":"since","explanation":"The present perfect <i>have not seen</i> with a starting point takes <b>since then</b>. <i>From then</i> is not used with the present perfect in this way, and <i>for</i> would need a length of time."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["every","each","any"],"answer":"every","explanation":"<b>Every time</b> (or <i>each time</i>, <i>any time</i>) means on all the occasions when something happens. <i>All time</i> is wrong because <i>all</i> needs a plural or <i>the</i>, and <i>the time I feel</i> would refer to a single occasion."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-15-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Afternoon the Sky Turned Grey","topic":"a storm at the beach","passage":"Last summer my family spent a week at a small beach town (1)____ the east coast. For the first four days the weather was perfect, and we spent every morning swimming and every afternoon (2)____ in the shade with a book. On the fifth day, however, the sky turned grey soon after lunch. My father, who has never trusted weather apps, said we should pack up, but the rest of us wanted to stay a (3)____ longer. Within twenty minutes the wind was so strong that our umbrella was blown across the sand, and we had to chase (4)____ it while the first big drops of rain hit our faces. By the time we reached the car, we were completely soaked. We sat there watching the waves, (5)____ were now higher than I had ever seen them. A lifeguard in a yellow jacket was running along the beach, shouting at a few swimmers who had not (6)____ come out of the water. My little brother was frightened, so my mother turned the radio on and (7)____ us to talk about something else. The storm lasted for about two hours. When it finally passed, the beach looked totally different: pieces of wood, seaweed and even a broken boat (8)____ been thrown onto the sand. The next morning the sun came out again as (9)____ nothing had happened. My father did not say \"I told you so\", but he did not need to. Since then, I (10)____ never argued with him about the weather.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","at","on","in"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A place lies <b>on</b> a coast: <i>a town on the east coast</i>. <i>In</i> is used for a region or country, <i>at</i> for a point, and <i>by</i> would need <i>the sea</i>, not <i>the coast</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["lied","laying","lain","lying"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Spend time (doing)</b> takes an -ing form, and the verb for resting flat is <i>lie</i>, whose -ing form is <b>lying</b>. <i>Laying</i> needs an object (<i>laying a towel</i>); <i>lain</i> and <i>lied</i> are not -ing forms."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["little","more","few","much"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>A little longer</b> means for a short extra time. <i>A few</i> goes with plural nouns, and <i>a much</i> / <i>a more</i> cannot follow the article <i>a</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["behind","after","over","for"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You <b>chase after</b> something that is getting away from you. <i>Chase behind</i>, <i>chase for</i> and <i>chase over</i> are not phrasal verbs in English."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","what","which","who"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The clause adds extra information after a comma, so it is non-defining and needs <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> cannot start a non-defining clause, <i>who</i> is for people, and <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["again","already","still","yet"],"correct":3,"explanation":"In a negative perfect clause, <b>not yet</b> means something expected has not happened. <i>Still</i> would go before <i>had not</i>, <i>already</i> is used in positive sentences, and <i>again</i> makes no sense here."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["told","made","suggested","said"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The pattern is <b>tell somebody to do</b> something. <i>Say</i> and <i>suggest</i> do not take an object plus <i>to</i>-infinitive, and <i>make somebody do</i> has no <i>to</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["was","had","have","were"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The things were thrown onto the sand before the family looked at the beach, so the earlier past action takes the past perfect passive: <b>had been thrown</b>. <i>Have</i> is present, and <i>were/was</i> cannot come before <i>been</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["well","like","if","soon"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>As if</b> introduces a comparison with an imaginary situation: the sun came out <i>as if nothing had happened</i>. <i>As like</i> is not a phrase, and <i>as soon as</i> / <i>as well</i> give the wrong meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["am","had","have","was"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Since then</b> links the past to now, so the sentence needs the present perfect: <i>I have never argued</i>. <i>Had</i> would need another past event as a reference point, and <i>am/was</i> cannot combine with <i>argued</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-15-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Wrong Notebook","topic":"a homework mistake","passage":"Two weeks ago I made a mistake with my homework (1)____ I will not forget for a long time. Our maths teacher, Mr Quang, had given us twenty problems to finish over the weekend. I did all of them on Saturday evening and felt quite proud of (2)____. On Monday morning, though, I opened my bag and realised that I (3)____ the wrong notebook. The one on my desk at home contained the maths; the one in my bag contained my English vocabulary. I thought about explaining, but Mr Quang is known (4)____ being strict, and I did not want to give an excuse that sounded like a lie. So when he collected the homework, I quietly (5)____ in the English notebook and hoped he would not look at it before lunch. Of course, he looked at it immediately. He held it up, opened it, and read a page of irregular verbs to the whole class. Everyone laughed, and my face went bright red. (6)____, he did not shout. He simply asked me to bring the correct notebook the next day and to stay behind (7)____ five minutes after the lesson. During those five minutes he told me that he would rather hear the truth than receive nothing at all. I wish I (8)____ said something at the beginning instead of trying to hide the problem. The next day I handed in the right notebook, and he gave me full marks, (9)____ it was a day late. Since then I have checked my bag twice every evening, and I have not made the same mistake (10)____.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","what","whose","which"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The relative pronoun refers to <i>a mistake</i>, a thing, so it must be <b>which</b>. <i>Who</i> is for people, <i>whose</i> shows possession, and <i>what</i> cannot follow a noun."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["my","mine","myself","me"],"correct":2,"explanation":"When the subject and the object are the same person, English uses a reflexive pronoun: <b>proud of myself</b>. <i>Me</i> is for a different object, and <i>mine</i> / <i>my</i> are possessives."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the words that fit.","options":["have brought","had brought","would bring","was bringing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The bringing happened before the moment of realising, so the earlier action takes the past perfect: <b>had brought</b>. <i>Have brought</i> is present perfect, <i>was bringing</i> describes an action in progress, and <i>would bring</i> points to the future."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","with","as","by"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>known for</b> a quality or habit: <i>known for being strict</i>. <i>Known as</i> introduces a name or title, and <i>known by</i> / <i>known with</i> do not fit."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["held","took","gave","handed"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The phrasal verb for submitting work is <b>hand in</b>. <i>Give in</i> means to surrender, and <i>take in</i> / <i>hold in</i> have completely different meanings."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["Therefore","Otherwise","However","Moreover"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The class laughed, but the teacher did not shout, so the linker must show contrast: <b>However</b>. <i>Therefore</i> gives a result, <i>moreover</i> adds a similar point, and <i>otherwise</i> means 'if not'."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","for","since","during"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A length of time takes <b>for</b>: <i>stay behind for five minutes</i>. <i>In</i> says when something will happen, <i>during</i> needs a named period like <i>the break</i>, and <i>since</i> needs a starting point."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["had","could","have","would"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A regret about the past uses <b>wish + past perfect</b>: <i>I wish I had said</i>. <i>Have said</i> is present perfect, and <i>would</i> / <i>could</i> would need a bare verb, not <i>said</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["unless","because","despite","although"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Full marks for late work is a surprising contrast, and the blank is followed by a clause, so the linker is <b>although</b>. <i>Despite</i> takes a noun or -ing form, and <i>because</i> / <i>unless</i> give the wrong meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["too","again","also","more"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Not ... again</b> means the mistake has not been repeated. <i>More</i> would need <i>any</i> before it, and <i>also</i> / <i>too</i> add information rather than describe repetition."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-15-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Three Queues and a Weekly Menu","topic":"the school canteen","passage":"Until this year, hardly anybody in my school ate at the canteen. The food was cheap, but it was also cold, and the queue moved so slowly that lunch break was half over (1)____ the time you sat down. In September a new manager, Mrs Hoa, took (2)____ the canteen, and things changed almost immediately. First, she divided the counter into three lines, one for rice dishes, one for noodles and one for drinks, (3)____ that nobody has to wait behind people who want something different. Second, she put up a board (4)____ the menu for the whole week is written, and she asks students to vote for the dish they would like to see on Fridays. The most popular choice so far (5)____ been fried chicken with sticky rice, which I do not think is a surprise to anyone. Third, and this is my favourite change, she stopped (6)____ plastic boxes and replaced them with metal trays that are washed and used again. Some teachers complained that the trays are noisy, (7)____ most students agree that the canteen is much cleaner now. There are still a (8)____ problems. The room becomes very hot at midday, and there are not (9)____ chairs for everyone in the second lunch group. Mrs Hoa says she has asked the head teacher for a fan and forty more seats. If she gets them, I think the canteen (10)____ become the busiest place in the school.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","on","in","at"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>By the time</b> means 'when that moment arrives' and shows that something had already happened. <i>At the time</i> means 'then', and <i>in</i> / <i>on the time</i> are not phrases."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","over","out","up"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To become the person in charge of something is to <b>take over</b> it. <i>Take up</i> means to start a hobby, <i>take out</i> means to remove, and <i>take off</i> is what a plane does."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["even","only","so","as"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>So that</b> introduces the purpose of dividing the counter. <i>As that</i>, <i>even that</i> and <i>only that</i> are not linkers of purpose."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","which","whose","where"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The menu is written <i>on</i> the board, so the relative word must express place: <b>where</b> (= on which). <i>Which</i> or <i>that</i> would leave the clause without a preposition, and <i>whose</i> shows possession."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["has","have","had","was"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>So far</b> signals the present perfect, and the subject <i>choice</i> is singular, so the verb is <b>has been</b>. <i>Have</i> is plural, <i>had</i> is past perfect, and <i>was</i> cannot be followed by <i>been</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["used","using","to use","use"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Stop + -ing</b> means to give up an activity, which matches replacing the boxes. <i>Stop to use</i> would mean pausing in order to use them, and the bare verb or <i>used</i> cannot follow <i>stopped</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["because","unless","but","so"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The teachers' complaint and the students' approval are opposite views, so the two clauses are joined by <b>but</b>. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would make one the reason for the other, and <i>unless</i> means 'if not'."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["little","bit","lot","few"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>Problems</i> is a plural countable noun, so it takes <b>a few</b>. <i>A little</i> and <i>a bit</i> go with uncountable nouns, and <i>a lot</i> would need <i>of</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["enough","many","much","plenty"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The phrase <b>not enough ... for everyone</b> says the number is smaller than what is needed. <i>Plenty</i> needs <i>of</i>, <i>much</i> is for uncountable nouns, and <i>not many</i> does not pair naturally with <i>for everyone</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["will","would","is","was"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The <i>if</i>-clause is in the present simple, so this is a first conditional and the result takes <b>will</b>. <i>Would</i> belongs to the second conditional, and <i>is</i> / <i>was</i> cannot come before the bare verb <i>become</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-15-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Two Walls and a Plastic Roof","topic":"a treehouse","passage":"When I was eleven, my cousin Minh and I decided to build a treehouse in the old mango tree (1)____ the end of my grandmother's garden. Neither of us had built anything before, but Minh had watched a lot of videos and was sure it would be easy. We started by collecting wood from a building site nearby, (2)____ the workers let us take the pieces they no longer needed. Carrying them home took a whole afternoon. The next problem was getting the wood (3)____ the tree. Minh climbed up first and I passed each board to him, one (4)____ a time, while my grandmother watched from the kitchen window, shaking her head. By the end of the first week we had a floor, (5)____ was much smaller than the one in Minh's drawings. By the end of the second week we had two walls and a roof made of an old plastic sheet. It was not beautiful, but we were (6)____ proud of it that we slept there on the very first night. At about two in the morning it started to rain, and we discovered that the roof had (7)____ holes than we had thought. We ran inside soaking wet, and my grandmother, who had clearly been waiting for this, gave us dry clothes without a word. We (8)____ up spending the rest of the summer repairing that roof. The treehouse is still there today, (9)____ nobody has slept in it since. Every time I visit, I climb up and sit on the floor for a few minutes. If it had not rained that night, we (10)____ probably never have learned so much about building.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","at","in","by"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A position along something is <b>at the end of</b> it: <i>at the end of the garden</i>. <i>In the end</i> means 'finally', and <i>on</i> / <i>by the end</i> do not describe a place."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["where","which","when","that"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The clause tells us what happened <i>at</i> the building site, so it needs the relative adverb of place, <b>where</b>. <i>Which</i> / <i>that</i> would have to be the subject or object of the clause, and <i>when</i> is for time."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["into","over","across","up"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The wood had to go from the ground to the branches, so the preposition of movement is <b>up</b> the tree. <i>Into</i>, <i>over</i> and <i>across</i> describe the wrong direction."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","in","at","by"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>one at a time</b>, meaning separately, not together. <i>By</i>, <i>in</i> and <i>for</i> do not make this expression."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["that","which","what","it"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After a comma, extra information about <i>a floor</i> needs the non-defining relative pronoun <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> cannot be used after a comma, <i>it</i> would create two sentences with no linker, and <i>what</i> is not a relative pronoun."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","too","very","such"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Before an adjective and followed by <i>that</i>, the structure is <b>so + adjective + that</b>. <i>Such</i> needs a noun, and <i>very</i> / <i>too</i> cannot be followed by a <i>that</i>-clause of result."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["much","most","many","more"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>Than</i> after the noun shows a comparison, so the quantifier is the comparative <b>more</b>. <i>Many</i> and <i>much</i> are not comparatives, and <i>most</i> is a superlative."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["finished","turned","ended","went"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>End up + -ing</b> means to finally find yourself doing something you did not plan. <i>Finish up</i> means to complete, <i>turn up</i> means to arrive, and <i>go up</i> means to rise."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["despite","although","unless","however"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The blank joins two clauses with a contrast, so it needs the conjunction <b>although</b>. <i>Despite</i> takes a noun, <i>however</i> cannot join two clauses with only a comma, and <i>unless</i> means 'if not'."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["had","will","have","would"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>If it had not rained</i> is a third conditional, so the result clause takes <b>would (never) have learned</b>. <i>Will</i> is for real future results, and <i>had</i> / <i>have</i> cannot stand before <i>have learned</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-15-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Six Sentences and a Set of Motors","topic":"a robot toy","passage":"For my tenth birthday my uncle gave me a small robot toy (1)____ could walk, turn its head and say six different sentences. I had asked for a bicycle, so at first I was a little disappointed. That feeling did not last long. (2)____ the end of the first evening I had discovered that the robot could be programmed with a simple app on my mother's phone. You dragged coloured blocks onto the screen, pressed a button, and the robot did exactly (3)____ you had told it to do. I spent the whole weekend teaching it to walk to the kitchen door and back (4)____ falling over. My father, who is an engineer, was more interested in the toy than I was. He kept taking it apart to see how the motors worked, and on one occasion he could not put it back (5)____ again. We had to send it to the shop, which took three weeks. When it came home, I noticed that it walked slightly (6)____ slowly than before, but it still worked. Over the next year I lost interest in the robot, as children usually do (7)____ toys. It sat on a shelf collecting dust. Last month, however, my little sister found it and asked me to show her how it worked. Watching her face light up, I remembered exactly how excited I (8)____ been at ten. We spent an hour programming it to dance, and now she takes it to school (9)____ single Friday to show her friends. If my uncle had given me a bicycle instead, I would have ridden it for a summer and forgotten about it. The robot, (10)____ the other hand, is still teaching somebody something.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): write ONE word.","accept":["which","that"],"answer":"which","explanation":"The blank is the subject of a defining relative clause about <i>a robot toy</i>, a thing, so <b>which</b> (or <b>that</b>) fits. <i>Who</i> fails because it is only used for people."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): write ONE word.","accept":["by","before","at"],"answer":"by","explanation":"The past perfect <i>I had discovered</i> describes something completed no later than a deadline, and a deadline takes <b>by</b> (<i>before</i> and <i>at the end of</i> are also possible). <i>On</i> fails because it goes with days, not with <i>the end of</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): write ONE word.","accept":["what","as"],"answer":"what","explanation":"<i>Did exactly <b>what</b> you had told it to do</i> means 'the thing that you had told it'; <i>exactly as</i> ('in the way that') is also correct. <i>Which</i> fails because there is no noun before the blank for it to refer to."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): write ONE word.","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"The point of the training was for the robot to complete the walk and not fall, so the preposition is <b>without</b> + -ing. <i>Before falling over</i> fails because it would mean the robot was expected to fall at the end."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): write ONE word.","accept":["together"],"answer":"together","explanation":"The opposite of <i>take apart</i> is <b>put (back) together</b>. <i>Up</i> fails because <i>put up</i> means to build a wall or a tent, not to rejoin the parts of a machine."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): write ONE word.","accept":["more"],"answer":"more","explanation":"<i>Slowly</i> is a long adverb, so its comparative before <i>than</i> is <b>more slowly</b>. <i>Less</i> fails because the following <i>but it still worked</i> shows the change was a small fault, not an improvement."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): write ONE word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"The phrase <i>as children usually do <b>with</b> toys</i> means 'as children usually treat toys'. <i>Of</i> and <i>to</i> fail because <i>do</i> in this sense is followed by <i>with</i>, never by those prepositions."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): write ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"The remembering is in the past (<i>I remembered</i>) and the excitement came earlier still, so the verb is the past perfect <b>had been</b>. <i>Have</i> fails because <i>have been at ten</i> would place a past state in the present perfect."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): write ONE word.","accept":["every","each"],"answer":"every","explanation":"Before the singular noun phrase <i>single Friday</i>, the determiner that means 'all of them, one by one' is <b>every</b> (or <b>each</b>). <i>All</i> fails because it takes a plural noun, <i>all Fridays</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): write ONE word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The fixed linker of contrast is <b>on the other hand</b>. <i>In</i> fails because <i>in the other hand</i> would literally mean inside a person's second hand."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-16-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Two Hours Outside a Tunnel","topic":"a train delay","passage":"Last Sunday my aunt and I took the early train to visit my grandparents, who live in a small town about three hours (1)____ the coast. We arrived at the station in plenty (2)____ time and found our seats easily. The train left at exactly seven, and for the first hour everything went smoothly. Then, just outside a tunnel, it slowed (3)____ and finally stopped. At first nobody was worried, but after twenty minutes people began to look at their watches. A voice announced that a tree had fallen across the line and that the delay would last (4)____ least an hour. My aunt, who hates waiting, was about to complain to the guard (5)____ she noticed a little boy crying in the next row. His mother had run out of snacks, so my aunt took (6)____ a packet of biscuits and offered him one. Soon the whole carriage was sharing food and telling stories, and I forgot all (7)____ the time. When the train finally moved again, a few passengers even looked disappointed. We reached my grandparents' house two hours late, (8)____ my grandmother had kept our lunch warm. Looking back, I think that afternoon taught me something useful: a delay is only as bad (9)____ you let it be. If I had spent those two hours complaining, I (10)____ never have met the friendly people around me.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","at","of","from"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Distance or travel time is measured <b>from</b> a place: <i>three hours from the coast</i>. <i>Of</i>, <i>at</i> and <i>by</i> cannot express how far away something is."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","with","of","for"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in plenty of time</b> (early enough). <i>Plenty</i> is always followed by <i>of</i> before a noun."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["up","down","out","off"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>slow down</b> means to go more slowly. <i>Slow up</i>, <i>slow off</i> and <i>slow out</i> are not used for a vehicle losing speed."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","by","for","in"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>At least</b> means 'not less than': <i>at least an hour</i>. The other prepositions do not combine with <i>least</i> in this meaning."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["while","since","after","when"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Was about to do something when</b> describes an action that was interrupted at the last moment. <i>While</i> needs a longer, continuing action, and <i>after</i> or <i>since</i> would change the order of events."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","up","out","over"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Take out</b> means to remove something from a bag or pocket. <i>Take off</i> is for clothes or planes, <i>take up</i> is for hobbies, and <i>take over</i> means to gain control."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","about","with","for"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You <b>forget about</b> something when it goes out of your mind for a while. <i>Forget of/for/with</i> are not English collocations."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["but","because","so","unless"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Arriving late and still finding lunch warm are a contrast, so <b>but</b> is needed. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would make the warm lunch a result or a reason for being late."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["like","so","than","as"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The comparison of equality is <b>as + adjective + as</b>: <i>only as bad as you let it be</i>. <i>Than</i> follows a comparative such as <i>worse</i>, which is not used here."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["will","would","was","had"],"correct":1,"explanation":"This is a third conditional: <i>If I had spent…, I <b>would</b> never have met…</i>. <i>Will</i> belongs to the first conditional, and <i>had</i> or <i>was</i> cannot come before <i>have met</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-16-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"A Stadium Full of Stars","topic":"my first concert","passage":"I had been saving my pocket money for months, so when my cousin offered me a ticket to see my favourite band, I could hardly believe my luck. The concert was held (1)____ a Saturday evening in the big stadium near the river. We set (2)____ early because my cousin said the queue would be enormous, and she was right. Thousands of fans were already waiting, many of (3)____ were wearing the band's T-shirt. While we stood in line, a girl behind us explained that she had travelled all the way from another province (4)____ her own. I remember thinking how brave that was. When the gates finally opened, everybody rushed forward, and I nearly lost my cousin in the crowd. Inside, the noise was (5)____ loud that I could not hear myself speak. The band came on stage at eight o'clock sharp, and from the first note the whole stadium seemed to move together. I sang (6)____ to every song, even the ones I did not know well. Halfway through, the singer stopped and asked the audience to turn on their phone lights, (7)____ turned the stadium into a sky full of stars. I have never seen anything (8)____ it. By the end, my voice had almost disappeared and my feet hurt, but I did not care. On the way home my cousin asked whether I had enjoyed it. I told her it was the best night (9)____ my life, and I meant it. I am already saving (10)____ for the next one.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","in","on","at"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Days and parts of a named day take <b>on</b>: <i>on a Saturday evening</i>. <i>In</i> is used with <i>the evening</i> alone, and <i>at</i> with clock times."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["up","away","down","off"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Set off</b> means to start a journey. <i>Set up</i> means to establish or prepare something, and <i>set away</i> or <i>set down</i> do not describe leaving."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["whom","who","them","which"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After a preposition in a relative clause about people, the pronoun is <b>whom</b>: <i>many of whom</i>. <i>Them</i> would create two sentences joined by a comma, and <i>which</i> is for things."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","on","by","with"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The phrase for 'alone' is <b>on her own</b>. <i>By</i> would need <i>herself</i> (<i>by herself</i>), not <i>her own</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["very","such","so","too"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>So + adjective + that</b> expresses a result: <i>so loud that I could not hear</i>. <i>Such</i> needs a noun, and <i>too</i> or <i>very</i> cannot be followed by a <i>that</i> clause."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["out","on","away","along"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Sing along to</b> a song means to sing together with the music. <i>Sing on</i>, <i>sing out</i> and <i>sing away</i> are not followed by <i>to every song</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","what","that","it"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Which</b> after a comma refers back to the whole previous idea (asking everyone to turn on their lights). <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma in this way, and <i>it</i> would need a new sentence."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["as","like","than","such"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Anything like it</b> means anything similar to it. <i>As</i> would need to be paired with an adjective, and <i>than</i> needs a comparative."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","to","of","for"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A superlative is usually followed by <b>of</b> plus the group or period: <i>the best night of my life</i>. <i>At</i>, <i>for</i> and <i>to</i> do not link a superlative to <i>my life</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["over","away","up","on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Save up for</b> something means to keep money aside until you can buy it. <i>Save on</i> means to spend less on something, which is not the meaning here."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-16-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Cat Who Belongs to Everyone","topic":"the neighbourhood cat","passage":"Nobody in our street knows (1)____ the grey cat really belongs to. He appeared about two years ago, thin and nervous, and at first he ran away (2)____ soon as anyone came near. Mrs Hoa at number twelve started leaving a bowl of rice and fish by her gate, and little (3)____ little he became braver. These days he behaves as if he owns the whole neighbourhood. In the morning he sits on the wall outside the bakery, waiting (4)____ the baker to drop a piece of bread. At lunchtime he can usually be found asleep under the motorbike that belongs to the man who mends shoes. My little brother (5)____ to him as Captain, although the children next door call him something completely different. Last winter he disappeared for almost a week, and everybody started asking (6)____ another about him. My mother even put a notice on the lamp post. Then one evening he simply walked back through our gate, looking rather pleased with (7)____. We still have no idea where he had been. Some neighbours say he is a nuisance because he chases the birds, (8)____ most of us would miss him terribly if he left. A cat that belongs to nobody, it seems, ends up belonging (9)____ everyone. I sometimes wonder whether he would stay if one family finally decided to keep him inside. Something tells me he (10)____ rather keep his freedom.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","who","what","whose"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The cat belongs to a person, so the question word is <b>who</b>: <i>who the cat belongs to</i>. <i>Which</i> and <i>what</i> are for things, and <i>whose</i> would need a noun after it."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["as","so","very","too"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The linker is <b>as soon as</b>, meaning immediately after. <i>So soon as</i> is not modern English, and <i>too</i> or <i>very</i> cannot join two clauses."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["to","and","for","by"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Little by little</b> means gradually, in small steps, like <i>one by one</i> or <i>day by day</i>. The other prepositions do not form this expression."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["to","at","for","of"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The pattern is <b>wait for somebody to do something</b>. <i>Wait to</i> is followed directly by a verb, not by a person."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["says","refers","names","calls"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Only <b>refer to somebody as</b> takes both <i>to</i> and <i>as</i>. <i>Calls</i> and <i>names</i> would be followed directly by the name, with no <i>to … as</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["one","every","each","some"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>One another</b> means each person asked the others. <i>Each</i> pairs with <i>other</i> (<i>each other</i>), not with <i>another</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["itself","him","his","himself"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The subject and the object are the same cat, so a reflexive pronoun is needed, and the passage calls the cat <i>he</i>: <b>pleased with himself</b>. <i>Itself</i> does not match <i>he</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","because","but","unless"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Calling him a nuisance and saying people would miss him are opposite ideas, so <b>but</b> joins them. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would wrongly make one idea the result or cause of the other."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","to","of","with"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The verb pattern is <b>belong to</b> somebody, as in the first sentence of the passage. <i>Belong with</i> exists but means 'fit naturally with', not ownership."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["had","could","should","would"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Preference is expressed with <b>would rather + bare verb</b>: <i>he would rather keep his freedom</i>. <i>Should</i> and <i>could</i> do not combine with <i>rather</i> in this meaning."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-16-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Friday Puzzle","topic":"a maths puzzle","passage":"Every Friday our maths teacher, Mr Tuan, writes a puzzle on the board before the lesson ends. Anyone (1)____ solves it by Monday gets a small prize, usually a pencil or a sticker. Last week's puzzle looked simple: three friends bought a cake for ninety thousand dong and paid thirty thousand each. Later the shop gave five thousand back, and they kept one thousand each and gave the last two thousand to the waiter. Mr Tuan asked us to explain (2)____ the numbers did not seem to add up. At first I was sure I could solve it (3)____ ten minutes. I wrote the numbers down, added them, and (4)____ added them again. Each time I got the same confusing result, and I started to feel annoyed (5)____ myself. On Saturday I gave up and went to play football. But on Sunday evening, (6)____ I was brushing my teeth, the answer suddenly appeared in my head. I had been adding the wrong things together! The waiter's two thousand had already come (7)____ of the money the friends paid, so it should not be counted twice. I ran to my desk and wrote the explanation out neatly. On Monday I handed it in, and Mr Tuan smiled and gave me a blue pencil. He said that most people fail not (8)____ the puzzle is difficult, but because they trust the first idea they have. (9)____ then I have tried to check my first idea before I believe it. The pencil, by the way, has already been lost, (10)____ the lesson has not.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["who","which","whom","whose"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>solves</i> and refers to a person, so it must be <b>who</b>. <i>Whom</i> is only for objects, and <i>which</i> is for things."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["whom","why","which","what"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The teacher wants the reason, so the indirect question begins with <b>why</b>. <i>What</i>, <i>which</i> and <i>whom</i> would need a missing noun or object in the clause, and there is none."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","for","in","at"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The time needed to finish a task is expressed with <b>in</b>: <i>solve it in ten minutes</i>. <i>For ten minutes</i> describes how long an activity lasts, not how quickly it is completed."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["than","after","so","then"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Then</b> shows the next step in a sequence: <i>added them, and then added them again</i>. <i>Than</i> belongs to comparisons, and <i>after</i> would need an object or a clause."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["with","by","for","of"],"correct":0,"explanation":"You are <b>annoyed with</b> a person, including yourself. <i>Annoyed by</i> introduces the thing that causes the feeling, which is not the meaning here."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["since","while","during","until"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>While</b> introduces a clause describing a longer action in progress. <i>During</i> is followed by a noun, never by a subject and verb, and <i>until</i> or <i>since</i> would change the meaning."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","up","out","away"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Come out of</b> means to be taken from a sum of money. <i>Come off</i>, <i>come up</i> and <i>come away</i> are not followed by <i>of the money</i> in this sense."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["since","though","as","because"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The structure <b>not because … but because …</b> needs the same linker in both halves. <i>Since</i> and <i>as</i> cannot follow <i>not</i> in this contrast, and <i>though</i> gives the wrong meaning."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["Since","From","Until","After"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Since then</b> goes with the present perfect <i>have tried</i> to show something that started in the past and continues. <i>From then</i> would need <i>on</i>, and <i>after</i> or <i>until</i> do not match the tense."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["but","because","so","or"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The pencil is lost and the lesson is not — a contrast, so <b>but</b> is required. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would suggest a cause-and-effect link that does not exist."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-16-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"The Watch at the Top of the Hill","topic":"a birthday walk","passage":"On my fourteenth birthday my father announced that, instead (1)____ a party, we were going to climb the hill behind our town. I was not keen at first, because I had wanted to spend the day (2)____ my friends. However, he promised that the view from the top would be worth the effort, so I agreed. We set off at six, before the sun (3)____ even risen, carrying a bag of bread, bananas and cold water. The path was steep and muddy in places, and twice I slipped and nearly fell. My father, (4)____ walks in the hills every weekend, did not slip once. He kept stopping to point out birds and plants (5)____ I had never noticed before, even though I had lived below this hill all my life. After about two hours we reached the top (6)____ last, and we sat down on a flat rock to eat. He was right about the view. The whole town lay below us, so small that I could cover our house with one finger. I had never seen it (7)____ that. Then my father took a small box out of his bag. Inside was a watch that had belonged (8)____ my grandfather. He said he had been waiting (9)____ I was old enough to look after it. (10)____ I have had many presents in my life, none of them has meant as much as that one. Now I want to climb the hill every birthday.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>instead of</b> + noun: <i>instead of a party</i>. <i>Instead</i> on its own can only end a clause, and <i>rather</i> would need <i>than</i>, not a single word."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"You <b>spend time with</b> people. <i>Spend the day for my friends</i> would mean doing it for their benefit, and <i>among</i> is not used for a small group of friends."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"<i>Risen</i> is a past participle, so it needs <b>had</b> to form the past perfect: the sun rising happened later than setting off. <i>Has</i> is present perfect and does not fit a past narrative, and <i>was</i> would need <i>rising</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"The clause between commas gives extra information about a person and is the subject of <i>walks</i>, so it takes <b>who</b>. <i>That</i> is not used in non-defining clauses after a comma, and <i>whom</i> is only for objects."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<i>Birds and plants</i> are things and the object of <i>noticed</i>, so the defining relative pronoun is <b>that</b> or <b>which</b>. <i>Who</i> is only for people, and <i>what</i> cannot follow a noun."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>At last</b> means finally, after a long time. <i>In the end</i> and <i>finally</i> exist, but <i>in last</i> or <i>by last</i> do not."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["like"],"answer":"like","explanation":"<b>Like that</b> means in that way or from that view. <i>As that</i> is not used with a pronoun in this sense, and <i>such</i> would need a noun."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The verb pattern is <b>belong to</b> somebody. <i>Belong with</i> means 'fit naturally alongside', which is not about ownership."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["until","till"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Wait until</b> (or <i>till</i>) + clause means waiting up to the moment something becomes true. <i>Wait for</i> must be followed by a noun, not by <i>I was old enough</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["although","though","while","whilst"],"answer":"although","explanation":"The first clause admits a fact and the second contrasts with it, so a concession linker such as <b>Although</b> (or <i>Though</i>, <i>While</i>) is needed. <i>But</i> and <i>however</i> cannot introduce a clause that comes before the comma in this way."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-17-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Thirty Students on a Stage","topic":"a spelling bee","passage":"Every March our school holds a spelling competition, and this year I decided to enter for the first time. I had been practising (1)____ months, reading long lists of words aloud to my grandmother every evening. She cannot speak much English, (2)____ she was patient enough to check each letter against the list. On the day of the contest, thirty students sat in a row on the stage while the whole school watched (3)____ silence. The rules were simple: if you spelt a word wrongly, you had to leave the stage (4)____ once. In the first round the words were easy, and nobody was eliminated. By the fourth round, (5)____, only eight of us were left. My hands were shaking so badly that I could hardly hold the microphone. Then the judge gave me the word \"necessary\". I knew it well, but I suddenly could not remember (6)____ it had one C or two. I closed my eyes, imagined my grandmother's finger moving along the page, and spelt it slowly. The bell did not ring, (7)____ meant I was correct. In the end I finished third, which is (8)____ better than I had expected. My friend Nam, who had (9)____ practised at all, won the whole thing. He says he simply reads a lot. I have decided that next year I will read more and worry (10)____.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","during","from","since"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A length of time (<i>months</i>) after a perfect tense takes <b>for</b>. <i>Since</i> needs a starting point, and <i>during</i> names a period, not how long."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["or","but","so","because"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The two halves contrast: she cannot speak English, <b>but</b> she still helped. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would make one half the cause of the other."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","at","in","with"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in silence</b>. English does not say <i>with silence</i> or <i>at silence</i> for the way people watch."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","in","on","at"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>At once</b> means immediately. <i>In</i>, <i>on</i> and <i>for</i> do not combine with <i>once</i> in this meaning."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["however","moreover","otherwise","therefore"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Easy first round, then a hard fourth round: a contrast, so <b>however</b>. <i>Therefore</i> and <i>moreover</i> would add or conclude, not contrast."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","whether","that","what"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The doubt is between two possibilities (one C or two), so <b>whether</b> introduces the clause. <i>That</i> states a fact; <i>what</i> and <i>which</i> would need a missing noun."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","who","which","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After a comma, <b>which</b> refers back to the whole idea that the bell did not ring. <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma here, and <i>what</i> never refers backwards."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["too","very","more","much"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A comparative (<i>better</i>) is strengthened with <b>much</b>. <i>Very</i> goes with a plain adjective, and <i>more better</i> is a double comparative."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["hardly","almost","already","nearly"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Hardly ... at all</b> means almost not at all, which fits a boy who just reads. <i>Nearly</i> and <i>almost</i> would say he came close to practising, which makes no sense with <i>at all</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["less","fewer","least","little"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<i>Worry</i> is an activity, so the comparative is <b>less</b>. <i>Fewer</i> is for countable nouns, and <i>least</i> is a superlative with nothing to compare to."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-17-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Small Silver Key","topic":"a lost key","passage":"Last Tuesday I came home from school and reached into my pocket for my key, but it was not there. I searched my bag twice, took everything (1)____ and shook it, and still found nothing. My parents would not be back (2)____ seven o'clock, so I sat on the step outside and tried to think. I was sure the key (3)____ still be somewhere, because I remembered locking the door that morning. Had it fallen out on the bus? Had somebody (4)____ it up in the playground? I walked back to the bus stop, keeping my eyes (5)____ the pavement the whole way, and asked the woman at the newspaper stall (6)____ she had seen a small silver key. She shook her head kindly. By the time I got home again it was starting to rain, and I was beginning to feel sorry (7)____ myself. Then our neighbour, Mrs Hoa, opened her window. \"Are you looking (8)____ this?\" she called, holding up my key. It had been lying on the path between our houses all afternoon. It must have (9)____ out of my pocket when I ran for the bus. I thanked her about ten times, and she laughed and said I was not the first person on our street to lose a key. Since that day I have kept my key on a long cord (10)____ my neck, and I check it every time I leave the house.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["away","out","up","off"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You <b>take</b> things <b>out</b> of a bag to empty it. <i>Take off</i> is for clothes, <i>take away</i> means remove from a place, and <i>take up</i> means start a hobby."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","since","for","by"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A negative verb with a clock time takes <b>until</b>: not back until seven. <i>By</i> means no later than, <i>since</i> marks a starting point, and <i>for</i> gives a length of time."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["might","can","should","must"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>I was sure</i> plus a reason calls for the deduction modal <b>must</b>. <i>Might</i> expresses only a possibility, which contradicts being sure."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["put","given","picked","taken"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>pick up</b> means lift something from the ground. <i>Take up</i>, <i>put up</i> and <i>give up</i> all exist but mean different things."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["to","on","in","at"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>keep your eyes on</b> something. <i>Look at</i> takes <i>at</i>, but <i>eyes</i> with <i>keep</i> takes <i>on</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["if","what","when","that"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A yes/no question reported after <i>asked</i> needs <b>if</b> (or <i>whether</i>). <i>That</i> reports a statement, and <i>what</i> or <i>when</i> would report a wh-question."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["about","with","of","for"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed expression is <b>feel sorry for</b> someone. <i>Sorry about</i> is used for an event, never for a person you pity."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["after","up","for","at"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Look for</b> means search for. <i>Look at</i> is just to see, <i>look after</i> is to care for, and <i>look up</i> is to check in a book."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["falling","fallen","fall","fell"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After <i>must have</i> the verb is a past participle: <b>fallen</b>. <i>Fell</i> is the past simple and cannot follow <i>have</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["along","over","across","around"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A cord goes <b>around</b> (or <i>round</i>) your neck, forming a circle. <i>Across</i>, <i>along</i> and <i>over</i> describe a line or a surface, not a loop."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-17-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"A Goat Among the Tomatoes","topic":"the market on Sunday","passage":"On Sunday mornings the square in front of the old church (1)____ into a market. Farmers from the villages nearby start arriving before six, and by eight o'clock it is almost impossible to move (2)____ the crowd. My mother goes every week, and I usually go (3)____ her to help carry the bags. She never buys anything from the first stall she sees. Instead, she walks slowly around the whole market, comparing prices at (4)____ stall, before she decides. The sellers know her well, and some of them keep their best tomatoes (5)____ for her. My favourite part is the corner where an old man sells hot corn. There is always a queue, (6)____ it moves quickly, and the smell is worth the wait. Last Sunday, (7)____, something unusual happened. A goat escaped from a truck and ran straight through the middle of the market, knocking over baskets of eggs and a whole table of flowers. Nobody was hurt, but it (8)____ three men nearly twenty minutes to catch it. The flower seller was upset at first, but by the end of the morning she was telling the story to anyone (9)____ would listen, and laughing harder each time. My mother says the market has been in the same place for more than a hundred years, and I hope it will still be there when I (10)____ children of my own.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["gets","makes","turns","becomes"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Turn into</b> means change and become something else. <i>Become</i> takes no <i>into</i>, and <i>get into</i> or <i>make into</i> mean something different."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["between","across","along","through"],"correct":3,"explanation":"You push your way <b>through</b> a crowd, passing among the people. <i>Across</i> and <i>along</i> describe a surface or a line, and <i>between</i> needs two things."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["with","to","along","by"],"correct":0,"explanation":"You go somewhere <b>with</b> a person, meaning together. <i>Go by</i> is for transport, <i>go to</i> is for a place, and <i>along</i> would need <i>with</i> after it."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["whole","each","all","both"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A singular noun (<i>stall</i>) after the determiner needs <b>each</b> or <i>every</i>. <i>All</i> and <i>both</i> take plural nouns, and <i>whole</i> would need <i>the</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["out","on","back","off"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Keep something back</b> means save it rather than sell it. <i>Keep off</i> means stay away, <i>keep out</i> means do not enter, and <i>keep on</i> means continue."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["unless","so","because","but"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A queue sounds bad; that it moves quickly is a contrast, so <b>but</b>. <i>So</i> and <i>because</i> would make the speed a result or a cause of the queue."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["however","besides","instead","therefore"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The usual Sundays are contrasted with one unusual Sunday, so <b>however</b>. <i>Therefore</i> gives a result, and <i>besides</i> adds a point."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["needed","took","spent","cost"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pattern is <b>it takes</b> someone a length of time to do something. <i>Spend</i> needs a person as its subject, and <i>cost</i> is about money."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","whose","who","whom"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>would listen</i> and refers to a person, so <b>who</b>. <i>Whom</i> is only for objects, and <i>which</i> is for things."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["will have","had","have","am having"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After <i>when</i> in a future time clause, English uses the present simple: <b>when I have</b>. <i>Will have</i> is wrong in a time clause, and <i>had</i> puts it in the past."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-17-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Night the Tent Flew","topic":"a camping mishap","passage":"Last summer my cousin Minh and I went camping by a lake for the first time. We had borrowed a tent from his uncle, (1)____ warned us to practise putting it up in the garden first. Naturally, we did not listen. We arrived at the campsite late in the afternoon, (2)____ the sun was already low, and spread the tent out on the grass. There were poles, ropes and little metal pegs, but no instructions. After an hour we had built something that looked (3)____ a tent, so we crawled inside, ate our sandwiches and fell asleep. Around midnight the wind (4)____ up. The tent began to shake, and then one side of it simply lifted off the ground. Minh shouted at me to hold the pole (5)____ he searched for the torch. It was pointless. Within minutes the whole thing had collapsed on top of us, and we were lying in the dark wrapped in wet cloth. We spent the rest of the night in the car, (6)____ was uncomfortable but at least dry. In the morning a family in the next tent lent us a hammer and showed us (7)____ to knock the pegs in at an angle. They had clearly done this many times before. We stayed two more nights, and the tent did not move (8)____ an inch. When we returned it, Minh's uncle asked how we (9)____ got on. \"Fine,\" said Minh, \"once we had learned to listen.\" I think it was the most honest thing I have ever heard him say. Next year we are going again, but this time we will practise (10)____ we leave.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","whom","whose","who"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The relative pronoun is the subject of <i>warned</i> and refers to a person (the uncle), so <b>who</b>. <i>Which</i> is for things, and <i>whom</i> cannot be a subject."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","since","when","while"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>When</b> the sun was already low describes the moment they arrived. <i>Until</i> and <i>since</i> mark the end or start of a period, which is not the meaning here."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["such","like","similar","as"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Look like</b> plus a noun means resemble. <i>As</i> would mean in the role of, and <i>similar</i> needs <i>to</i> after it."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["picked","made","went","turned"],"correct":0,"explanation":"When the wind gets stronger it <b>picks up</b> (or <i>gets up</i>). <i>Turn up</i> means arrive, and <i>go up</i> is for prices or temperatures."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["during","meanwhile","until","while"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Two actions at the same time are joined by <b>while</b> plus a clause. <i>During</i> is a preposition and needs a noun, and <i>meanwhile</i> cannot join two clauses."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","it","which","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After a comma, <b>which</b> adds a comment about the whole fact of sleeping in the car. <i>That</i> is not used after a comma in this way, and <i>it</i> would create two separate sentences."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["why","how","that","what"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<i>Show somebody</i> <b>how</b> <i>to do</i> something teaches a method. <i>What to do</i> would need no object after the verb, and <i>that</i> cannot introduce <i>to</i> plus a verb."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["even","still","just","ever"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<i>Did not move</i> <b>even</b> <i>an inch</i> stresses the smallest possible amount. <i>Ever</i> and <i>still</i> describe time, not amount."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["have","were","did","had"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The uncle's question <i>How did you get on?</i> is reported after a past verb, so it moves back to <b>had got on</b>. <i>Have got</i> keeps the present, and <i>did got</i> is impossible."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["while","before","after","until"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The lesson is to practise first and then go, so <b>before</b> we leave. <i>After</i> reverses the order, and <i>until</i> would mean practising non-stop."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-17-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"A Small Shadow on a Bicycle","topic":"my little brother","passage":"My little brother Bao is six years old, and he follows me (1)____ the house like a small shadow. Whenever I sit down to do my homework, he climbs onto the chair next to me and asks (2)____ I am doing. I used to find this annoying, (3)____ lately I have started to enjoy it. Last month he decided that he wanted to learn to ride a bicycle. My father was busy, so the job was given (4)____ me. Every afternoon we went to the empty car park behind our building, and I ran beside him holding the back of the seat. He fell off at (5)____ twenty times in the first week. Each time, he sat on the ground for a moment, looked at his knees, and then got back on without a word. I do not think I (6)____ have been so brave at his age. On the tenth day, I let go of the seat without telling him, and he rode all the way to the far wall (7)____ his own. When he realised I was (8)____ longer behind him, he was so surprised that he forgot to use the brakes and rode straight into a bush. He was not hurt, and he laughed (9)____ loudly that two people came out onto their balconies to see what was happening. Now he rides everywhere, and I have to run to keep (10)____ with him. My mother says that one day he will be teaching me things. I think she is probably right.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["around","round","about","through"],"answer":"around","explanation":"<b>Follow someone around</b> (or <i>round</i>, <i>about</i>, <i>through</i>) <i>the house</i> means everywhere inside it. <i>In</i> would fit a place but not the idea of following from room to room, and <i>along</i> is for a line such as a road."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["what"],"answer":"what","explanation":"The question he asks is <i>What are you doing?</i>, so the reported form keeps <b>what</b>. <i>Which</i> needs a known set of choices, and <i>if</i> would turn it into a yes/no question that makes no sense here."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["but","yet","though","although"],"answer":"but","explanation":"The old feeling (annoying) contrasts with the new one (enjoy), so <b>but</b> (or <i>yet</i>, <i>though</i>, <i>although</i>). <i>So</i> would wrongly make the enjoyment a result of the annoyance."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"In the passive, <i>give something</i> <b>to</b> <i>someone</i> keeps its preposition: the job was given <b>to</b> me. <i>For</i> would mean the job was done on my behalf, not handed to me."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["least"],"answer":"least","explanation":"<b>At least</b> twenty times means twenty or more. <i>At most</i> would set an upper limit, and <i>at last</i> means finally, which makes no sense with a number."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["would","could"],"answer":"would","explanation":"An imagined past (<i>at his age</i>) takes <b>would</b> (or <i>could</i>) <i>have been</i>. <i>Should have been</i> talks about a duty that was not done, not about an imagined ability."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>on</b> <i>his own</i>, meaning without help. <i>By</i> goes with <i>himself</i>, not with <i>his own</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["no"],"answer":"no","explanation":"<b>No longer</b> means not any more; with the positive verb <i>was</i>, only <i>no</i> works. <i>Not</i> would need <i>any</i> and a negative verb (<i>was not any longer</i>), and <i>any</i> alone is wrong here."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["so"],"answer":"so","explanation":"<b>So</b> plus an adverb (<i>loudly</i>) plus <i>that</i> gives the result. <i>Very</i> and <i>too</i> cannot be followed by a <i>that</i> clause of result."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"<b>Keep up with</b> someone means move as fast as they do. <i>Keep on</i> means continue, and <i>keep away</i> means stay at a distance, which is the opposite idea."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-18-1","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Pepper the Classroom Hamster","topic":"a class pet","passage":"At the start of this school year, our form teacher, Mr Tuan, surprised us (1)____ a small cage on the shelf beside the window. Inside it was a brown hamster, (2)____ we later named Pepper after a long and noisy vote. Mr Tuan explained that looking after a pet would teach us responsibility, but he also warned that Pepper would go back to the shop (3)____ we forgot to feed him even once. We made a timetable straight away. Each pair of students is in charge (4)____ the cage for one week: they change the water, add fresh food and clean out the sawdust on Friday afternoon. At first, some of my classmates were nervous about touching him, (5)____ he is tiny and his claws feel strange on your skin. Now, however, almost everyone wants a turn. Pepper sleeps for most of the day and wakes up just (6)____ the last lesson, which is usually maths. It is hard to concentrate on equations when a hamster is running on his wheel a few metres away. Last month there was a moment of panic when Pepper disappeared. We (7)____ for him for twenty minutes before Lan noticed a tail sticking out of the teacher's bag. Since then we have been much (8)____ careful about closing the cage door. Mr Tuan says our class has become calmer and kinder since Pepper arrived, and I think he is right. I have even started (9)____ to school earlier so that I can say good morning to him. If somebody had told me a year ago that a hamster (10)____ change how a class behaves, I would not have believed them.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","with","for","at"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You <b>surprise someone with</b> something: the cage is the thing that caused the surprise. <i>By</i> follows the passive (<i>surprised by</i>), and <i>for</i> / <i>at</i> do not fit this pattern."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","whose","what","who"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The relative pronoun refers back to <i>a brown hamster</i>, an animal, so <b>which</b> is used. <i>Who</i> is for people, <i>whose</i> shows possession, and <i>what</i> cannot introduce a relative clause."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["although","while","unless","if"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The warning is a condition: he goes back to the shop <b>if</b> we forget. <i>Unless</i> would reverse the meaning, and <i>although</i> / <i>while</i> express contrast, not a condition."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","with","of","on"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in charge of</b> something. The other prepositions do not combine with <i>in charge</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","because","although","unless"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The clause gives the reason for their nervousness, so <b>because</b> is needed. <i>So</i> would introduce a result, and <i>although</i> / <i>unless</i> give the wrong logic."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["before","by","until","since"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Pepper wakes up <b>just before</b> the last lesson, that is, shortly earlier. <i>Until</i> and <i>since</i> describe a period, and <i>by</i> would need a deadline meaning that does not fit <i>wakes up</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["caught","lost","found","looked"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>look for</b> means to search. <i>Found</i>, <i>caught</i> and <i>lost</i> are not followed by <i>for</i> in this sense, and finding him is what happened afterwards."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["most","very","more","so"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A comparison with the past needs the comparative <b>more careful</b>, and <i>much</i> strengthens a comparative. <i>Much most</i>, <i>much very</i> and <i>much so</i> are not possible."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["come","coming","came","comes"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Start</b> is followed by an -ing form (or <i>to</i> + verb): <i>started coming</i>. The bare form, the past form and the -s form cannot follow <i>started</i> directly."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["ought","must","should","could"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Inside reported speech about the past, ability is expressed with <b>could</b>: a hamster <i>could change</i> how a class behaves. <i>Must</i> and <i>should</i> express obligation, and <i>ought</i> needs <i>to</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-18-2","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Pool at the End of Our Street","topic":"the swimming pool","passage":"The public swimming pool at the end of our street opened (1)____ 1998, and my parents still remember the queue on the first morning. For years it was the busiest place in the neighbourhood every summer. Children learned to swim there, teenagers met their friends there, and old people did slow lengths early in the morning, (2)____ the water was still calm. Then, about three years ago, the city council announced that the pool was too expensive to (3)____ and would close at the end of the season. (4)____ many people were angry that a petition was signed by nearly four thousand residents in a single week. The council agreed to think again, but only (5)____ the community could help pay for repairs. That is when something wonderful happened. Local shops donated money, a builder offered to fix the changing rooms (6)____ free, and my school held a fun run to raise the rest. The pool reopened last May, looking (7)____ than it had for a decade. I go there twice a week now. I am not a fast swimmer, but I have finally (8)____ to dive properly without hurting my stomach. On hot afternoons the pool is so crowded that you can hardly see the water, (9)____ nobody seems to mind. My grandfather, who swam there as a boy, says the smell of chlorine takes him straight back to his childhood. I understand exactly (10)____ he means. A pool is not just a hole full of water; it is a place where a whole street learns to share.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","at","on","since"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A year takes <b>in</b>: <i>in 1998</i>. <i>At</i> is for clock times, <i>on</i> for days and dates, and <i>since</i> would need a present perfect verb."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","when","unless","so"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>When</b> the water was still calm describes the time the old people chose to swim. <i>Until</i> would mean up to that point, and <i>unless</i> / <i>so</i> give the wrong logic."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["hold","stay","run","take"],"correct":2,"explanation":"You <b>run</b> a pool, a shop or a business, meaning you operate it. <i>Hold</i>, <i>stay</i> and <i>take</i> do not have this meaning with a building."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["Very","Such","Too","So"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>So many</b> people ... <b>that</b> introduces a result. <i>Such</i> goes directly with a noun (<i>such anger</i>), and <i>too</i> / <i>very</i> are not followed by a <i>that</i>-clause of result."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["if","unless","though","whether"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Only if</b> the community helped would the council think again: a condition. <i>Unless</i> reverses the condition, <i>whether</i> introduces a choice, and <i>though</i> shows contrast."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","for","at","by"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>for free</b>, meaning without payment. None of the other prepositions combines with <i>free</i> in this way."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["good","best","better","well"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A comparison with <i>than</i> needs a comparative: <b>better than it had</b>. <i>Good</i> and <i>well</i> are not comparatives, and <i>best</i> is a superlative."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["known","studied","taught","learned"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Learn to</b> + verb means gain a skill: <i>learned to dive</i>. <i>Taught</i> needs an object (<i>taught me</i>), and <i>known</i> / <i>studied</i> are not followed by <i>to</i> + verb in this sense."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["but","because","so","unless"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The crowding is unpleasant, yet nobody minds: a contrast, so <b>but</b> is the linker. <i>Because</i> and <i>so</i> express reason or result, and <i>unless</i> makes no sense here."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","how","that","which"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>What he means</b> is a noun clause, the thing he means. <i>That</i> and <i>which</i> would need a noun before them, and <i>how</i> would describe a manner, not the meaning."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-18-3","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"The Concert I Never Went To","topic":"a broken promise","passage":"When I was twelve, my older cousin Khoa promised to take me to my first real concert. He was nineteen and (1)____ a part-time job in a music shop, so he seemed to know everything about bands. The concert was in August, and I talked about it (2)____ May onwards. I saved my pocket money for a T-shirt, and I even practised the words of every song so that I (3)____ sing along without embarrassing him. Three days before the concert, Khoa phoned my mother instead of me. He said a friend had offered him a free ticket to a festival in another city on the same weekend and he could not (4)____ it down. My mother told me gently, but I still ran to my room and refused to come out for dinner. What hurt most was not missing the concert; it was the fact that he had not told me (5)____. For nearly a year I hardly spoke to him. (6)____ he sent a message, I answered with one word. Then, last spring, he turned up at our house with two tickets and an apology that sounded (7)____ if he had practised it in the mirror. He admitted he had been selfish and said he (8)____ thought about it many times since. I wanted to stay angry, but it is difficult to stay angry with someone who is clearly ashamed. We went to the concert together, and it was (9)____ better than the one I had missed, or at least that is what I tell myself. I learned two things from that summer. A promise is easy to make and hard to keep, and forgiving someone is a decision you make (10)____ yourself, not for them.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["kept","made","did","had"],"correct":3,"explanation":"You <b>have</b> a job: <i>had a part-time job</i>. <i>Made</i>, <i>did</i> and <i>kept</i> are not the natural verbs for simply holding a job."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","since","from","by"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>From May onwards</b> marks the starting point of a period that continues. <i>Since</i> is not used with <i>onwards</i>, and <i>by</i> / <i>until</i> mark an end point."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["need","could","must","should"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After <i>so that</i> in a past narrative, ability is expressed with <b>could</b>: so that I could sing along. <i>Must</i>, <i>should</i> and <i>need</i> express obligation, not ability."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["turn","let","put","give"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>turn something down</b> means refuse an offer. <i>Put down</i>, <i>give down</i> and <i>let down</i> do not mean refuse."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["him","his","he","himself"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The emphatic reflexive <b>himself</b> stresses that Khoa did not do the telling in person. <i>Him</i>, <i>his</i> and <i>he</i> cannot add this emphasis after <i>told me</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["Whatever","However","Whenever","Wherever"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Whenever</b> he sent a message means every time he sent one. <i>However</i> shows contrast, and <i>wherever</i> / <i>whatever</i> refer to place and things, not time."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["such","as","like","so"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The fixed comparison is <b>as if</b>: it sounded as if he had practised. <i>Like if</i>, <i>so if</i> and <i>such if</i> are not English phrases."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["had","was","would","has"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The reporting verb <i>said</i> is in the past, so present perfect <i>has thought</i> shifts back to past perfect: he said he <b>had thought</b>. <i>Was thought</i> is passive and <i>would thought</i> is not a form."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["more","most","very","even"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Even better</b> strengthens a comparative: better than the other concert, and by a surprising amount. <i>Very</i>, <i>more</i> and <i>most</i> cannot go directly before <i>better</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["with","for","at","to"],"correct":1,"explanation":"You do something <b>for yourself</b>, for your own benefit, and the sentence contrasts this with <i>not for them</i>. <i>To</i>, <i>with</i> and <i>at</i> do not express benefit."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-18-4","level":"kc","mode":"mcq","title":"Lights Off at the Star Cinema","topic":"the old cinema","passage":"The Star Cinema stood on the corner of our market square for almost seventy years. My grandmother saw her first film there in 1961, and she still remembers (1)____ excited she was when the lights went down. In those days there was only one screen, and people queued around the building on Saturday nights. By the time I was born, however, the cinema was (2)____ trouble. A modern complex with eight screens had opened at the shopping mall, and most families preferred its comfortable seats and cold air conditioning. The Star kept going for a few more years, mainly (3)____ of its loyal older customers, but the roof leaked and the sound system broke (4)____ again and again. It finally closed the winter I turned ten. (5)____ six years the building stayed empty. Posters faded in the glass cases, and pigeons moved in under the roof. Then last year a group of young people decided to bring it (6)____ to life. They cleaned every seat by hand and repainted the front in its original red and gold. Instead of showing new blockbusters, which they could not afford, they show old classics and films made by local students. Tickets cost (7)____ than half the price at the mall. My grandmother and I went to the opening night together. She cried a little when the curtain opened, and I pretended (8)____ to notice. The film was in black and white, and the picture jumped now and then, but nobody complained. In fact, the audience clapped at the end as (9)____ the actors could hear them. Walking home, my grandmother said that some places deserve a second chance, and I could not have (10)____ more.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["which","what","how","that"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>How excited she was</b> is an indirect question about degree: <i>how</i> + adjective. <i>What</i> and <i>which</i> need a noun, and <i>that excited</i> would not form a clause here."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["under","at","on","in"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in trouble</b>, meaning in a difficult situation. The other prepositions do not combine with <i>trouble</i> this way."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["because","despite","instead","thanks"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Because of</b> + noun gives the reason it kept going. <i>Thanks</i> would need <i>to</i>, <i>despite</i> takes no <i>of</i>, and <i>instead of</i> gives the wrong meaning."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","down","out","up"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A machine that stops working <b>breaks down</b>. <i>Break up</i>, <i>break off</i> and <i>break out</i> all exist but do not describe a sound system failing."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["Since","In","For","During"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A length of time takes <b>for</b>: <i>for six years</i>. <i>During</i> needs a named period, <i>since</i> needs a starting point, and <i>in six years</i> would point to the future."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["out","over","up","back"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Bring something back to life</b> means make it alive or active again. <i>Bring up</i>, <i>bring out</i> and <i>bring over</i> do not carry this meaning with <i>to life</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["less","lower","smaller","fewer"],"correct":0,"explanation":"With an amount of money, <b>less than</b> is used. <i>Fewer</i> is for countable plurals, and <i>lower</i> / <i>smaller</i> would need <i>prices are</i>, not <i>tickets cost</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["no","not","none","nothing"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An infinitive is made negative with <b>not</b> before <i>to</i>: <i>pretended not to notice</i>. <i>No</i>, <i>none</i> and <i>nothing</i> cannot negate a verb form."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["soon","long","if","far"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>As if</b> the actors could hear them describes an imagined situation. <i>As soon as</i>, <i>as long as</i> and <i>as far as</i> are real phrases but need <i>as</i> again and give the wrong meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["admitted","argued","agreed","accepted"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The idiom <b>could not have agreed more</b> means completely agree. <i>Accepted</i>, <i>admitted</i> and <i>argued</i> do not form this expression."}]},{"id":"cl-kc-18-5","level":"kc","mode":"open","title":"Thirty Seconds in the Air","topic":"a paper aeroplane contest","passage":"Every year on the last day (1)____ term, our school holds a paper aeroplane contest in the gym. The rules are simple. You are given one sheet of A4 paper and five minutes, and you are not allowed to use scissors, glue (2)____ tape. The plane that stays in the air the longest wins. This year I was determined to do better (3)____ my brother, who had come second twice and never lets anyone forget it. For a whole week I practised in the garden after dinner. I discovered that a plane with wide wings floats gently, but a narrow (4)____ flies fast and then crashes into the ground. I also learned that the way you throw matters just as (5)____ as the way you fold. On the day of the contest, about eighty students lined up along the wall of the gym. When my turn came, my hands were shaking so badly that I nearly dropped the plane. I threw it slightly upwards, as I (6)____ practised, and it began to glide in a slow circle. Everyone went quiet. The plane seemed to hang in the air for ages, and when it finally touched the floor the teacher's stopwatch showed nineteen seconds. It was the best time (7)____ far. Unfortunately, the last competitor was a quiet girl from Year 7 (8)____ plane flew for twenty-three seconds and landed on the basketball net. She won, and she deserved to. My brother came fourth, so at (9)____ I beat him. On the way home he asked me how I had folded the wings, and for the first time in my life I was the one giving advice. Next year I am going to start practising in January, (10)____ though my brother says that is far too early.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"A part of a period is expressed with <b>of</b>: <i>the last day of term</i>. <i>In</i> would be used with a longer period (<i>the last day in June</i>) but not with <i>term</i> here."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["or"],"answer":"or","explanation":"After a negative such as <i>not allowed</i>, alternatives are joined with <b>or</b>: none of the three is allowed. <i>And</i> would suggest that only using all three together is forbidden."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"A comparative such as <i>better</i> is followed by <b>than</b> to name what is compared. <i>As</i> is only used with the equal form <i>as good as</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["one"],"answer":"one","explanation":"The pronoun <b>one</b> stands for <i>plane</i> after an adjective: <i>a narrow one</i>. Repeating <i>plane</i> is possible but a single pronoun is required here, and <i>it</i> cannot follow an adjective with <i>a</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["much"],"answer":"much","explanation":"The equal comparison is <b>as much as</b>: the throw matters as much as the fold. <i>Many</i> is only for countable nouns, and <i>well</i> would compare how well, not how much, it matters."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"The practising happened before the throw, so past perfect is needed: <i>as I <b>had</b> practised</i>. <i>Have practised</i> would clash with the past-tense story."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["so","by","thus"],"answer":"so","explanation":"<b>So far</b> means up to that point, which fits because more competitors were still to come. <i>By far</i> (by a large margin) is also acceptable, but <i>too far</i> or <i>as far</i> give no sensible meaning."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"The relative pronoun <b>whose</b> shows possession: the girl’s plane. <i>Who</i> or <i>which</i> would need a verb straight after them, not the noun <i>plane</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["least"],"answer":"least","explanation":"The fixed phrase <b>at least</b> means that one good thing is true even if others are not. <i>At last</i> means finally and would change the meaning completely."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["even"],"answer":"even","explanation":"<b>Even though</b> introduces a strong contrast: he says it is too early, yet I will still do it. <i>Although though</i> is impossible, and <i>as though</i> would mean <i>as if</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-06-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Leaves That Moved Empires","topic":"the history of tea","passage":"Few drinks have travelled as far as tea, and fewer still have altered the fortunes of so many nations along the way. According to one account, the leaves were first brewed by accident (1)____ than by design, when a few of them drifted into a pot of water being boiled beneath a tree. Whether or not the story is true, tea (2)____ certainly been cultivated in the hills of south-west China for well over two thousand years. For most of that time it remained a local pleasure; not (3)____ the ninth century did it become a commodity worth carrying across mountains on the backs of mules. Compressed into bricks, it was traded for horses along a route so arduous that some porters carried loads heavier (4)____ themselves.<br><br>When European merchants eventually (5)____ across the drink in the early seventeenth century, they had no idea what to make of it. Within a hundred years, however, the British were importing it (6)____ such quantities that the government came to depend on the tax it yielded. This dependence had consequences (7)____ which nobody had planned. Smuggling flourished, colonies protested, and one famous cargo ended up in a harbour rather than in a teapot. Had the tax been lighter, the course of at least one revolution (8)____ have run differently.<br><br>Today tea is grown on every inhabited continent and drunk more widely than any beverage (9)____ water. Global commodity (10)____ it now is, it has kept a curious intimacy: whatever the country, offering someone a cup still signals that they are welcome to stay.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["rather"],"answer":"rather","explanation":"The fixed contrast is <b>by accident rather than by design</b>. <i>More than by design</i> would compare degrees, not replace one cause with the other, and <i>other than</i> means <i>apart from</i>, which is not the meaning here."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["has"],"answer":"has","explanation":"<b>For well over two thousand years</b> stretches from the past up to now, so the present perfect <i>has been cultivated</i> is required. <i>Was</i> would close the period in the past, and <i>had</i> needs a later past event to be earlier than."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["until","till"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Not until</b> + time, fronted for emphasis, triggers the inversion <i>did it become</i>. <i>Before</i> cannot follow <i>not</i> in this fronted pattern, and <i>in</i> would make <i>not in the ninth century</i> a plain denial."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The comparative <i>heavier</i> must be completed with <b>than</b>. <i>As</i> pairs only with the equal form <i>as heavy as</i>, and <i>to</i> follows <i>superior</i> or <i>inferior</i>, never an <i>-er</i> comparative."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["came","stumbled","ran","happened"],"answer":"came","explanation":"To meet something by chance is to <b>come across</b> it (also <i>stumble</i>, <i>run</i> or <i>happen across</i>). <i>Found</i> and <i>discovered</i> take a direct object and cannot be followed by <i>across</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"Amounts are expressed with <b>in such quantities</b>, just as in <i>in large numbers</i>. <i>With</i> or <i>at</i> do not collocate with <i>quantities</i> in this sense."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"You <b>plan for</b> a consequence, so the relative clause keeps that preposition: <i>consequences for which nobody had planned</i>. <i>Of which</i> would need a verb like <i>thought</i>, and <i>which</i> alone leaves <i>planned</i> with a false object."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["might","would","could"],"answer":"might","explanation":"<i>Had the tax been lighter</i> is an inverted third conditional, so the result needs a past modal: <b>might</b> (or <i>would</i>/<i>could</i>) <i>have run</i>. <i>Will</i> or <i>can</i> would describe a real future, not a lost past possibility."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["except","but","save","bar","besides"],"answer":"except","explanation":"After <i>any beverage</i> the exclusion is marked by <b>except</b> (also <i>but</i>, <i>save</i>, <i>bar</i>, <i>besides</i>). <i>Than</i> has already been used with <i>more widely</i>, and <i>without</i> would mean the tea contained no water."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["though","as"],"answer":"though","explanation":"When a noun or adjective is fronted for concession, only <b>though</b> or <i>as</i> can follow it: <i>Global commodity though it now is</i>. <i>Although</i> and <i>despite</i> cannot take this inverted position."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-06-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"A Compass the Size of a Seed","topic":"how bees navigate","passage":"A honeybee on a foraging trip may travel several kilometres, visit hundreds of flowers and still find its way home with uncanny accuracy. How it manages this has puzzled naturalists (1)____ centuries. Only in recent decades have the pieces of the answer begun to fall (2)____ place.<br><br>The first clue lies in the sun. Bees perceive polarised light, a pattern invisible to us, (3)____ allows them to work out the sun's position even when it is hidden behind cloud. The second is an internal clock: because the sun moves, a bee that set out at nine must correct its bearings by noon, and it does so effortlessly. (4)____ far a bee may fly from the hive, its brain keeps a running tally of every turn and every straight stretch.<br><br>This tally is what makes the famous waggle dance work. A successful forager dances on the comb, and the angle of her run tells her sisters (5)____ direction to fly relative to the sun. The length of the run indicates the distance. Were the dance merely a display of excitement, other bees (6)____ hardly be able to locate a food source they had never seen.<br><br>Landmarks matter too. Near home, bees rely (7)____ familiar features such as trees and hedges, which is why a hive moved even a few metres can leave its residents circling in confusion. What is remarkable is the way these abilities are combined: an insect with a brain the size of a seed integrates light, time and memory (8)____ one coherent map. Scientists are now asking (9)____ damage to this navigation, blamed partly on pesticides, accounts for the decline in colonies across Europe and North America. Should the answer turn (10)____ to be yes, the consequences for crops that depend on pollination could be severe.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"<i>Centuries</i> is a length of time, so the present perfect takes <b>for</b>. <i>Since</i> needs a starting point such as <i>since 1700</i>, and <i>during</i> does not combine with a bare plural of duration here."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"When separate facts start to make sense together, they <b>fall into place</b>. <i>In place</i> describes something already fixed, so it cannot follow the movement verb <i>fall</i> in this idiom."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"The clause adds extra information after a comma, so it is non-defining and takes <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> is not used in non-defining clauses, and <i>it</i> would create a run-on sentence."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["however"],"answer":"however","explanation":"<b>However far a bee may fly</b> means <i>no matter how far</i>: <i>however</i> + adverb introduces a concessive clause. <i>Although</i> cannot be followed directly by <i>far</i>, and <i>whatever</i> needs a noun."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["which","what"],"answer":"which","explanation":"<i>Tells her sisters</i> is followed by an embedded question, so <b>which</b> (or <i>what</i>) <i>direction to fly</i>. <i>The</i> would need a full relative clause, and <i>where</i> cannot precede the noun <i>direction</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["would","could"],"answer":"would","explanation":"<i>Were the dance merely a display</i> is an inverted second conditional, so the main clause needs <b>would</b> (or <i>could</i>) <i>hardly be able</i>. <i>Will</i> belongs to a real condition, which this hypothesis is not."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["on","upon"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The verb is <b>rely on</b> (or <i>upon</i>) something. <i>Rely in</i> and <i>rely with</i> do not exist, and <i>rely to</i> confuses the verb with <i>refer to</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"Separate things are <b>integrated into</b> a single whole: the preposition expresses the result of combining. <i>In</i> would locate the map rather than describe its formation, and <i>as</i> would wrongly equate the inputs with the map."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["whether","if"],"answer":"whether","explanation":"<i>Asking</i> introduces an indirect yes/no question, so <b>whether</b> (or <i>if</i>) is needed. <i>That</i> would turn the open question into a statement, and <i>how</i> would ask for a method, not a yes/no answer."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>Turn out to be</b> means to prove in the end to be. <i>Turn up</i> means to appear unexpectedly, and <i>turn into</i> means to change into something, neither of which takes <i>to be</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-06-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Streets Given Back","topic":"a city without cars","passage":"Imagine a city where the loudest sound at rush hour is the ringing of bicycle bells. It sounds like a utopian fantasy, (1)____ a handful of places have come surprisingly close to it. One Spanish town closed its historic centre to private vehicles in the late 1990s, at a time (2)____ such a decision was regarded as political suicide. Shopkeepers predicted ruin. Instead, footfall rose, air pollution fell by two thirds and, contrary (3)____ every expectation, the town became a model that delegations from abroad now come to study.<br><br>The reasoning behind such schemes is simple. Cars take up an astonishing amount of space for the number of people they move, so much (4)____ that in some cities up to eighty percent of urban land is given over to roads and parking. Reclaim even a fraction, and daily life is transformed. Streets that once served as corridors turn (5)____ places where people linger, and struggling shops suddenly find customers in no hurry to be elsewhere.<br><br>The objections, (6)____, are not trivial. Deliveries must still be made, the elderly cannot always cycle, and residents of the suburbs, for (7)____ a car is often the only realistic option, may feel that a privilege is being withdrawn for the benefit of people wealthy enough to live centrally. Any council that ignores these concerns does so (8)____ its peril. What the successful examples have in common is that they moved gradually, one street at a time, and that public transport (9)____ been improved before the first barrier went up.<br><br>No sooner had the first fully car-free district opened in a large European city (10)____ property prices there began to climb. Whether that is a sign of success or a warning about who really benefits is a question planners are still debating.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["yet","but"],"answer":"yet","explanation":"The second clause contradicts the first, so a contrastive conjunction is needed: <b>yet</b> or <i>but</i>. <i>So</i> would present the near-success as a result of its being a fantasy, and <i>and</i> loses the contrast entirely."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["when"],"answer":"when","explanation":"After <i>a time</i> the relative adverb is <b>when</b>. <i>Which</i> would need the preposition, as in <i>at which</i>, and <i>where</i> refers to places, not periods."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>contrary to</b> expectation. <i>Contrary with</i> and <i>contrary of</i> do not exist; the noun form would be <i>on the contrary</i>, which does not fit here."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["so"],"answer":"so","explanation":"<b>So much so that</b> intensifies a previous statement before giving its consequence. <i>So much that</i> alone would need a verb to modify, and <i>so much as that</i> is not a linking expression."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"A change of state is <b>turn into</b>: corridors become places to linger. <i>Turn to</i> means to seek help from someone, and <i>turn in</i> means to hand over or go to bed."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["however","though","nevertheless","nonetheless","admittedly"],"answer":"however","explanation":"A contrast with the previous paragraph, set between commas inside the sentence, calls for <b>however</b> (or <i>though</i>, <i>nevertheless</i>, <i>nonetheless</i>; the concessive <i>admittedly</i> also fits). <i>But</i> and <i>although</i> are conjunctions and cannot sit in this parenthetical slot."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["whom"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"Directly after a preposition the relative pronoun must be the object form <b>whom</b>: <i>for whom a car is the only option</i>. <i>Who</i> is not used after <i>for</i>, and <i>which</i> cannot refer to people."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"The idiom is <b>at one's peril</b>, meaning with a risk of harm to oneself. <i>In peril</i> describes a state of danger and cannot take the possessive <i>its</i> in this warning sense."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"The improvement was completed before a past event, <i>went up</i>, so the past perfect <b>had been improved</b> is required. <i>Has been</i> would clash with the past tense of the following clause, and <i>was been</i> is not a form."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The inverted pattern <i>No sooner had X happened</i> is completed with <b>than</b>, because <i>sooner</i> is a comparative. <i>When</i> belongs with <i>hardly</i> or <i>scarcely</i>, and <i>then</i> is a common misspelling that breaks the structure."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-06-4","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"The Office Comes Home","topic":"the rise of remote work","passage":"Until quite recently, the idea that a large share of office work could be done from a kitchen table was dismissed as wishful thinking. Managers took it (1)____ granted that productivity would collapse the moment staff were out of sight. Few companies, therefore, were willing to put the theory (2)____ the test. Then circumstances forced their hand. Within weeks, millions of employees who had never worked a day from home were doing so full-time, and the results, (3)____ patchy, were far better than the pessimists had predicted.<br><br>Several years (4)____, the picture is more complicated. Some firms have called their staff back, arguing that innovation depends on the chance conversations that only happen when people share a corridor. Others have (5)____ office space altogether and now recruit from anywhere with a reliable connection. (6)____, the majority lie somewhere in between, with a hybrid arrangement that satisfies nobody completely but that few are prepared to abandon.<br><br>What is (7)____ dispute is that the change has altered the balance of power. Workers who once accepted a two-hour commute as an unavoidable fact of life now regard it as a cost to be weighed against the salary on offer, and employers who insist on five days in the office find their pool of candidates (8)____ accordingly. The effects reach well beyond the workplace: city-centre cafés have lost their lunchtime trade, while small towns within commuting distance have gained residents who would never (9)____ considered living there.<br><br>(10)____ remains to be seen is whether the arrangement will survive the next downturn, when the bargaining power that made it possible may shift back the other way.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","by","for","as"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The idiom is <b>take it for granted that</b>, meaning to assume without question. <i>Take as</i> needs a noun such as <i>a compliment</i>, and <i>on</i> and <i>by</i> never follow <i>take it</i> before <i>granted</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","under","in","to"],"correct":3,"explanation":"You <b>put something to the test</b> when you try it out for real. <i>Under test</i> would need <i>is</i> rather than <i>put</i>, and <i>on</i> and <i>in</i> do not form this expression."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["albeit","despite","even","whereas"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Albeit</b> can be followed directly by an adjective to concede a weakness: <i>the results, albeit patchy, were better</i>. <i>Despite</i> needs a noun or <i>being</i>, <i>even</i> would need <i>if</i>, and <i>whereas</i> requires a full clause."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["since","later","ago","before"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The sentence describes the present situation, so <b>several years later</b> moves the narrative forward to now. <i>Ago</i> and <i>before</i> point backwards, and <i>since</i> cannot stand alone after a period of time in this position."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the words that fit.","options":["dispensed from","dispensed for","dispensed with","dispensed of"],"correct":2,"explanation":"To manage without something is to <b>dispense with</b> it. <i>Dispense</i> takes no other preposition in this sense; <i>dispose of</i> is a different verb, and <i>from</i> and <i>for</i> form no phrasal verb with <i>dispense</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["Whereas","Despite","Although","Meanwhile"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Meanwhile</b> is an adverb that opens a complete sentence and sets the majority alongside the two extremes just described. <i>Whereas</i> and <i>although</i> would leave the sentence as a fragment with no main clause, and <i>despite</i> cannot be followed by a subject and verb."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["beyond","over","against","across"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Something nobody can argue with is <b>beyond dispute</b>. <i>Over</i> and <i>against</i> describe the position of the parties in an argument, not the certainty of a fact, and <i>across dispute</i> is not English."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["to shrink","shrinking","shrinks","shrank"],"correct":1,"explanation":"After <i>find</i> + object, a present participle describes what the object is doing: <b>find their pool shrinking</b>. A finite verb such as <i>shrinks</i> or <i>shrank</i> would need its own subject, and <i>find X to shrink</i> is not idiomatic."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["having","has","have","had"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A past unreal idea uses modal + <b>have</b> + past participle: <i>would never have considered</i>. <i>Had</i> and <i>has</i> cannot follow a modal, and <i>having</i> would need <i>be</i> before it."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["That","It","What","Which"],"correct":2,"explanation":"A cleft sentence begins with <b>What</b> as the subject of its own clause: <i>What remains to be seen is whether</i>. <i>It remains to be seen</i> would need no <i>is</i>, <i>that</i> cannot open a subject clause without a verb after it, and <i>which</i> requires a preceding noun."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-07-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Rainforests of the Sea","topic":"coral reefs","passage":"Few ecosystems on Earth are (1)____ once so productive and so fragile as a coral reef. Although reefs cover less than one per cent of the ocean floor, they are home (2)____ roughly a quarter of all marine species, a density of life that has earned them the nickname 'rainforests of the sea'. The reef itself is built by tiny animals called polyps, which secrete a limestone skeleton and live in partnership with microscopic algae. It is these algae, rather (3)____ the polyps themselves, that give a healthy reef its colour. When the surrounding water grows too warm, the polyps expel their algae and the reef turns a ghostly white, a process (4)____ as bleaching. Not (5)____ the temperature drops again can the partnership be restored, and if the heat persists for more than a few weeks the polyps simply starve. Scientists had long assumed that bleaching was a rare event; (6)____ the past two decades, however, it has struck the world's largest reefs repeatedly. Had governments acted on the earliest warnings, some of that damage (7)____ have been avoided. Even so, not all the news is bleak. Certain reefs in the Red Sea appear to tolerate heat far (8)____ than their Pacific cousins, and researchers are now trying to work (9)____ whether the genes responsible could be encouraged elsewhere. Whether such efforts succeed will depend largely (10)____ how quickly the oceans keep warming.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>At once ... and ...</b> means 'simultaneously both': at once so productive and so fragile. <b>All</b> or <b>for</b> cannot combine with <i>once</i> to carry that meaning."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"A place is <b>home to</b> the species that live there. <b>Of</b> and <b>for</b> do not follow <i>home</i> in this sense."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"<b>Rather than</b> contrasts the algae with the polyps inside a cleft sentence. <b>Then</b> is a time adverb and <b>not</b> would need <i>and not</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["known","described"],"answer":"known","explanation":"A reduced relative clause: a process (which is) <b>known as</b> bleaching; <b>described as</b> also works. <b>Called</b> and <b>named</b> fail because they take a bare noun, never <i>as</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["until","till","before"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Not until</b> (or <i>till</i>, <i>before</i>) at the front of a sentence triggers the inversion <i>can the partnership be restored</i>. <b>When</b> would give the reverse meaning: that recovery never happens at the moment the water cools."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["over","in","during","throughout","within"],"answer":"over","explanation":"A period measured up to now takes <b>over</b>, <b>in</b>, <b>during</b>, <b>throughout</b> or <b>within</b> with the present perfect <i>has struck</i>. <b>Since</b> fails because it needs a starting point, not a length of time; <b>for</b> fails before <i>the past two decades</i> with this verb pattern in formal prose."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["might","could","would","may"],"answer":"might","explanation":"An inverted third conditional (<i>Had governments acted ...</i>) needs a past modal: <b>might</b>, <b>could</b> or <b>would</b> have been avoided. <b>Should</b> fails because it turns a hypothetical result into advice about the past."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["better","more"],"answer":"better","explanation":"<i>Tolerate heat far <b>better</b> than</i> compares how well they cope; <b>more</b> is also acceptable. <b>Best</b> is a superlative and cannot precede <i>than</i>; <b>well</b> is not comparative."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>work out</b> means discover by reasoning. <b>Work on</b> means continue improving something, and <b>work at</b> means make an effort, so neither can take a <i>whether</i> clause."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["on","upon"],"answer":"on","explanation":"<b>Depend on</b> (or <i>upon</i>) is the only preposition the verb takes, even with an adverb like <i>largely</i> in between. <b>Of</b> and <b>from</b> are common learner errors influenced by <i>independent of</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-07-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Letters Made of Lead","topic":"the printing press","passage":"It is hard to overstate how thoroughly the printing press transformed the way (1)____ which knowledge moved around Europe in the fifteenth century. Before movable type, a single manuscript might take a monk the better (2)____ of a year to copy, and every copy carried the errors of the one before it. No sooner (3)____ Johannes Gutenberg's workshop begun producing Bibles than printers in other cities started to imitate his methods, and within fifty years presses were operating in more than two hundred towns. What made the invention so powerful was not the press (4)____ but the combination of cheap paper, oil-based ink and metal letters that could be rearranged endlessly. Scholars who had once relied (5)____ memory could now compare identical texts hundreds of miles apart, which in (6)____ made it possible to spot mistakes and build on one another's work. Not everyone welcomed the change. Some rulers, fearing the spread of ideas they could not control, (7)____ about licensing every printer in their territory; others simply banned books outright. Yet (8)____ hard they tried, the flow of pamphlets proved impossible to stop. Had the press never been invented, the religious and scientific upheavals of the following century would almost certainly have (9)____ a very different course. Historians still argue over how much credit one man deserves, but few would deny that the world he left behind bore little (10)____ to the one he had been born into.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"The formal relative structure is <b>the way in which</b> something happens. <b>By</b> or <b>on</b> do not collocate with <i>way</i> here; <b>that</b> would need to replace <i>which</i>, not sit beside it."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["part"],"answer":"part","explanation":"<b>The better part of</b> a period means most of it. <b>Half</b> gives the wrong quantity and does not take <i>better</i>; <b>portion</b> is not the idiom."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"<b>No sooner had</b> + subject + past participle ... <i>than</i> is a fixed inversion with the past perfect. <b>Did</b> fails because the participle <i>begun</i> follows, and <b>was</b> would need <i>begun</i> to be passive, which makes no sense."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["itself","alone"],"answer":"itself","explanation":"<i>Not the press <b>itself</b> but the combination</i> uses the emphatic reflexive to isolate the machine from its context; <b>alone</b> works the same way. <b>Only</b> fails because it must precede the noun, and <b>self</b> is not a free-standing word."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["on","upon"],"answer":"on","explanation":"You <b>rely on</b> or <b>upon</b> something. <b>In</b> and <b>to</b> are common errors modelled on <i>trust in</i> and <i>refer to</i>, but <i>rely</i> never takes them."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["turn"],"answer":"turn","explanation":"<b>In turn</b> means 'as a further consequence' and links one result to the next. <b>Return</b> gives <i>in return</i>, an exchange, which is not the meaning; <b>fact</b> would merely add emphasis."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["set","went","thought"],"answer":"set","explanation":"<b>Set about</b> (or <i>went about</i>) doing something means started to tackle it; <b>thought about</b> licensing (merely considered it) is also grammatical and fits the contrast with rulers who <i>simply banned</i> books. <b>Started</b> or <b>began</b> fail because they take <i>to</i> or a bare gerund, never <i>about</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["however"],"answer":"however","explanation":"<b>However hard they tried</b> means 'no matter how hard'. <b>Whatever</b> must be followed by a noun, and <b>although</b> cannot precede a bare adverb like <i>hard</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["taken","followed","run","pursued"],"answer":"taken","explanation":"Events <b>take</b> (or <i>follow</i>, <i>run</i>, <i>pursue</i>) <b>a course</b>; the past participle is needed after <i>would have</i>. <b>Had</b> or <b>gone</b> do not form this collocation, and the base form <i>take</i> cannot follow <i>have</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["resemblance","relation","similarity"],"answer":"resemblance","explanation":"<b>Bear little resemblance to</b> is the standard collocation; <i>bore little relation to</i> and <i>bore little similarity to</i> are also accepted. <b>Difference</b> reverses the logic, and <b>likeness</b> is not used with <i>bear ... to</i> in this way."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-07-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"What the Brain Does at Night","topic":"sleep and memory","passage":"For most of the twentieth century, sleep was regarded as little (1)____ than a period of rest, a nightly shutdown during which the brain did nothing of importance. That view has been overturned so completely that many neuroscientists now describe sleep as the point (2)____ which learning actually happens. During the day, new experiences are stored temporarily in a region called the hippocampus, but the record is fragile and easily overwritten. It is only at night, (3)____ the brain is cut off from fresh input, that these traces are replayed and gradually transferred to the cortex for long-term storage. Researchers have demonstrated this in a series of elegant experiments. Volunteers who learned a list of word pairs in the evening and then slept remembered far more the next morning than those who had stayed awake, (4)____ though both groups had been given the same amount of time. Nor does the benefit stop at facts. Musicians who practise a difficult passage and then sleep on it often play it more fluently the following day, (5)____ having touched the instrument in between. What is less clear is (6)____ the different stages of sleep contribute. Deep, slow-wave sleep appears to strengthen factual memories, (7)____ the dreaming stage seems to link new information to what we already know. There is a warning here for students. Staying up all night before an exam may feel productive, but (8)____ it is followed by sleep, much of the effort is wasted. Far from being time lost, then, sleep turns (9)____ to be the very process by (10)____ knowledge is made permanent.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["more"],"answer":"more","explanation":"<b>Little more than</b> means 'hardly anything beyond'. <b>Less</b> reverses the sense, and <b>other</b> would need <i>little other than</i>, which is not idiomatic English."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>The point at which</b> is the formal equivalent of <i>the point when</i>. <b>In</b> and <b>on</b> do not collocate with <i>point</i> in a time sense, and <b>of</b> would make the sentence meaningless."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["when","once","while","after","as"],"answer":"when","explanation":"A time conjunction introduces the clause: <b>when</b>, <b>once</b>, <b>while</b>, <b>after</b> or <b>as</b> the brain is cut off. <b>Which</b> fails because the clause already has its own subject, and <b>that</b> would wrongly close the cleft sentence early."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["even"],"answer":"even","explanation":"<b>Even though</b> concedes the fact that both groups had equal time. <b>As</b> would produce <i>as though</i>, which means 'as if' and turns the fact into an appearance."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"<b>Without having touched</b> uses the preposition plus perfect gerund to say the practice did not happen. <b>Despite</b> is impossible because the meaning is not concessive, and <b>not</b> would need <i>not having touched</i> in a different position."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["how","what","whether"],"answer":"how","explanation":"A noun clause is needed after <i>is</i>: <b>how</b>, <b>what</b> or <b>whether</b> the stages contribute. <b>That</b> fails because the sentence says the matter is unclear, so a fact cannot simply be stated."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["while","whereas","and","but"],"answer":"while","explanation":"Two stages are contrasted, so <b>while</b> or <b>whereas</b> (or simply <b>and</b> / <b>but</b>) joins the clauses. <b>Although</b> fails because the two facts are not in tension, and <b>however</b> cannot join clauses with just a comma."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["unless"],"answer":"unless","explanation":"<b>Unless it is followed by sleep</b> means 'if it is not'. <b>If</b> says the opposite, that sleep causes the waste, and <b>until</b> would make the wasted effort a temporary state."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>Turn out to be</b> means prove to be, after investigation. <b>Turn up</b> means appear, and <b>turn in</b> means go to bed, so neither takes <i>to be</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"After a preposition the relative pronoun must be <b>which</b>: <i>the process by which</i>. <b>That</b> can never follow a preposition, and <b>what</b> would leave <i>process</i> without a relative clause."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-07-4","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"Born of Fire, Claimed by the Sea","topic":"volcanic islands","passage":"Volcanic islands are among the youngest pieces of land on the planet, and their life story follows a pattern that geologists can now (1)____ with remarkable precision. Most begin far below the surface, where a plume of molten rock forces its way (2)____ the ocean crust. Eruption after eruption piles lava on the sea floor until, often after hundreds of thousands of years, a cone finally breaks the surface. At this stage the new island is little more (3)____ bare black rock, utterly hostile to life. Yet colonisation begins almost immediately. Seeds arrive on the wind or in the stomachs of birds; insects are blown in on storms; lichens take (4)____ on the cooling lava and slowly grind it into soil. Within a few centuries, what was once a sterile heap of cinders may support a forest. The island's (5)____, however, is already sealed. As the tectonic plate carries it away from the plume that created it, the volcano falls silent and erosion sets (6)____. Waves eat at the coastline, rain carves valleys, and the whole mass gradually sinks under its own weight. In warm seas, coral reefs grow up around the shrinking peak, so that (7)____ the island has disappeared entirely, a ring of reef remains to mark where it stood. The Hawaiian chain illustrates this cycle perfectly: the youngest island, still erupting, sits at one end, (8)____ a line of ever older, ever lower islands stretches away to the north-west. Thanks (9)____ this sequence, scientists can estimate how fast the plate is moving. Few natural laboratories offer (10)____ a clear picture of creation and decay unfolding side by side.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["recover","reconstruct","rebuild","restore"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Scientists <b>reconstruct</b> a sequence of past events from evidence. <i>Rebuild</i> and <i>restore</i> apply to physical objects, and <i>recover</i> means get back something lost."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["through","across","over","along"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Magma <b>forces its way through</b> a layer, passing from one side to the other. <i>Across</i>, <i>over</i> and <i>along</i> describe movement on a surface, not penetration."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["as","then","that","than"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Little more than</b> is a comparative and needs <b>than</b>. <i>As</i> pairs with <i>as much</i>, <i>then</i> is a time adverb, and <i>that</i> would need a full clause to follow."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["place","shape","hold","grip"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Take hold</b> means become established. <i>Take place</i> means happen, <i>take shape</i> means develop a form, and <i>take grip</i> is not an English expression."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["chance","fate","luck","fortune"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Only a <b>fate</b> can be <i>sealed</i>, meaning fixed beyond change. <i>Luck</i>, <i>fortune</i> and <i>chance</i> refer to random outcomes and do not take this verb."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["in","up","off","out"],"correct":0,"explanation":"An unwelcome process such as erosion, rot or bad weather <b>sets in</b>, meaning begins and continues. <i>Set up</i> means establish, <i>set off</i> means depart or trigger, and <i>set out</i> means begin a journey or explain."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["while","unless","until","once"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Once the island has disappeared</b> means 'after that point', which is when the ring of reef is all that remains. <i>While</i> would place the two events at the same time, and <i>unless</i> and <i>until</i> would say the reef survives only if the island does not vanish."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["unless","until","while","although"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>While</b> here contrasts the two ends of the chain, like <i>whereas</i>. <i>Although</i> signals an unexpected exception, and <i>unless</i> and <i>until</i> express condition and time, neither of which fits a description."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","to","for","by"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Thanks to</b> is a fixed prepositional phrase meaning 'because of'. <i>Thanks for</i> expresses gratitude and needs a person, and <i>by</i> and <i>of</i> never follow <i>thanks</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","too","very","such"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Such a clear picture</b> is the order before an article plus adjective plus noun. <i>So</i> and <i>too</i> would need <i>so clear a picture</i>, and <i>very</i> cannot precede the article at all."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-08-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Who Pays for a Cup of Coffee?","topic":"the economics of coffee","passage":"Few commodities travel as far, or change hands as often, as the coffee bean. By the time a cup reaches a customer in a city café, its contents have typically passed (1)____ the hands of a grower, a local collector, an exporter, a roaster and a retailer, each of (2)____ takes a share of the final price. What is striking is how unevenly that price is divided. Farmers in the highlands of East Africa or Central America commonly receive less (3)____ ten per cent of what the drinker pays, and in a poor year the figure can fall further still.<br><br>Part of the explanation lies in the structure of the market. Coffee is traded on international exchanges, (4)____ means that its price responds to weather forecasts, currency movements and speculation rather than to the cost of growing it. A frost in Brazil can double prices overnight; a bumper harvest can halve them. Growers, (5)____ contrast, cannot adjust their output quickly, because a coffee tree takes several years to bear fruit. Had they known in advance what the market would do, they might have planted differently — but nobody does.<br><br>Various schemes have been set (6)____ to address the imbalance. Certification programmes promise a guaranteed minimum price, and some roasters now buy directly from cooperatives, cutting (7)____ the middlemen altogether. Critics point out, however, that such arrangements reach only a tiny minority of the world's growers, and that the premium paid is often swallowed (8)____ by the cost of meeting the standards. It is not so (9)____ demand that is lacking as bargaining power. Until growers are able to hold stocks back when prices dip, (10)____ than sell at whatever is offered, the economics of coffee will continue to favour those furthest from the tree.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["through"],"answer":"through","explanation":"Something that <b>passes through the hands of</b> several people is handled by each in turn. <b>Between</b> would need only two parties and does not collocate with <b>hands</b> here."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["whom"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"After the preposition <b>of</b>, a relative pronoun for people takes the object form: <b>each of whom</b>. <b>Which</b> is for things, and <b>who</b> cannot follow a preposition directly in formal writing."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The comparative <b>less</b> is completed by <b>than</b>: <b>less than ten per cent</b>. <b>Then</b> is a common misspelling here but is an adverb of time, not a comparative marker."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"The clause comments on the whole preceding statement, so a sentential relative <b>which means that</b> is needed. <b>That</b> cannot follow a comma to refer back to a whole clause, and <b>it</b> would leave two main clauses joined by nothing."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["by","in"],"answer":"by","explanation":"<b>By contrast</b> (or <b>in contrast</b>) sets growers against the fast-moving market just described. <b>On</b> and <b>for</b> do not form a contrast linker with this noun."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"To <b>set up</b> a scheme is to establish it. <b>Set out</b> means to begin a journey or to lay out a plan in detail, and <b>set off</b> means to depart or trigger something."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"To <b>cut out</b> the middlemen is to remove them from the chain entirely, which <b>altogether</b> confirms. <b>Cut off</b> means to isolate or interrupt, and <b>cut down</b> means to reduce."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"A premium that is <b>swallowed up by</b> costs disappears into them completely; the particle strengthens the sense of being fully absorbed. <b>Swallowed down</b> is about literally eating, and <b>swallowed off</b> does not exist."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["much"],"answer":"much","explanation":"The frame <b>not so much X as Y</b> corrects one explanation with a better one: what is missing is bargaining power, not demand. <b>Not so many</b> would need a plural countable noun, and <b>demand</b> is uncountable here."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["rather"],"answer":"rather","explanation":"<b>Rather than sell</b> presents holding stock back as the preferred alternative to selling cheaply. <b>Other than</b> means 'except', and <b>more than</b> would compare quantities rather than choices."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-08-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Race to the Ends of the Earth","topic":"polar exploration","passage":"No sooner (1)____ the last blank spaces on the map been filled than explorers turned their attention to the poles. The attraction was not gold or trade routes, for there were none; it was the challenge of reaching a place (2)____ no human being had ever stood. Between 1890 and 1914, a succession of expeditions set out from Europe, and a good many of their members never came back.<br><br>The conditions defied preparation. Temperatures dropped so low that metal stuck to bare skin, and the wind could pin a party inside their tents for days on (3)____. Yet what proved most dangerous was often something far more ordinary: food. Expeditions carried tinned supplies, and men who lived on them for months began to suffer (4)____ scurvy, a disease whose cause was still poorly understood. Had the link with fresh food been better known, several lives would have been (5)____.<br><br>The most famous rivalry of the era was that between a Norwegian and a British party racing for the South Pole in 1911. The Norwegians, (6)____ had grown up on skis and understood dogs, arrived first and returned in good health. The British reached the Pole a month later, only to find their rivals' flag already flying, and died on the way back. Historians have argued ever (7)____ over whether the British leader was heroic or reckless. What is beyond dispute is that his diaries, recovered months afterwards, turned a failure (8)____ a legend.<br><br>Today the poles are visited by scientists and even tourists, and it is easy to forget how recently they were beyond (9)____. The men who went there had no radios and no hope of rescue. That any of them returned at (10)____ is remarkable.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"After <b>No sooner</b> at the start of a sentence, the auxiliary comes before the subject, and the earlier of the two past events takes the past perfect: <b>No sooner had … been filled than</b>. <b>Were</b> is impossible before <b>been</b>, and <b>have</b> would give the wrong time."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["where"],"answer":"where","explanation":"<b>Place</b> is followed by a relative adverb of location: <b>a place where nobody had stood</b>. <b>Which</b> would need a preposition (<b>on which</b>), and <b>that</b> cannot replace a place adverb."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["end"],"answer":"end","explanation":"<b>For days on end</b> means for many days without a break. <b>For days on</b> plus <b>and</b> or <b>after</b> is not a fixed expression, and <b>on time</b> would mean punctually."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["from"],"answer":"from","explanation":"The verb collocates with the disease as <b>suffer from scurvy</b>. <b>Suffer with</b> is a colloquial variant that examiners do not accept, and <b>suffer of</b> is not English."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["saved","spared"],"answer":"saved","explanation":"The inverted third conditional <b>Had … been known, lives would have been saved</b> describes an unreal past result; <b>spared</b> works equally well. <b>Lost</b> reverses the logic, since better knowledge would not have cost lives."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause about people takes <b>who</b>: <b>The Norwegians, who had grown up on skis</b>. <b>That</b> is not used after a comma in a non-defining clause, and <b>which</b> is for things."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["since"],"answer":"since","explanation":"<b>Ever since</b> means continuously from that time until now, which matches the present perfect <b>have argued</b>. <b>Ever after</b> belongs to fairy tales, and <b>ever then</b> does not exist."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"The pattern <b>turn X into Y</b> expresses transformation: the diaries <b>turned a failure into a legend</b>. <b>Turn to</b> means to seek help or move towards, and <b>turn in</b> means to hand over or go to bed."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["reach"],"answer":"reach","explanation":"<b>Beyond reach</b> means impossible to get to, which the following sentence about having no rescue confirms. <b>Beyond belief</b> would mean unbelievable, and <b>beyond access</b> is not a collocation."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["all"],"answer":"all","explanation":"<b>At all</b> strengthens the surprise in a clause with <b>any</b>: <b>that any of them returned at all</b>. <b>At last</b> would mean finally, and <b>at least</b> would set a minimum, neither of which fits the sense of survival against the odds."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-08-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Slow Death of a Language","topic":"how languages die","passage":"Of the roughly seven thousand languages spoken today, linguists estimate that as (1)____ as half may have vanished by the end of the century. A language does not die suddenly. It fades, generation by generation, and nearly always in the same way. First, speakers of a small language come into contact with a larger one that offers access to jobs, schooling or trade. Parents, keen for their children to get (2)____ in the wider world, begin to speak the dominant language at home. The children grow up understanding their grandparents but answering them in the new tongue. By the third generation, the old language survives only in songs, prayers and a few expressions (3)____ nobody can quite translate.<br><br>It is tempting to see this as a natural process, no more to be regretted (4)____ the extinction of a dialect of Latin. Linguists disagree, and (5)____ good reason. Every language divides up experience in its own way: some have a dozen words for kinds of snow, others mark whether a speaker witnessed an event or merely heard about it. (6)____ time such a language goes, an irreplaceable record of how human beings have made sense of their surroundings goes with it.<br><br>Efforts to reverse the decline have had mixed results. Schools that teach in a minority language can slow its loss, but they cannot on their (7)____ restore it, because a language lives in kitchens and markets, not in classrooms. Success stories exist — one Pacific island language has been brought (8)____ from a handful of elderly speakers to several thousand — but they depend on something no government can legislate: a community deciding that its language is (9)____ keeping. Without that decision, even careful documentation amounts to (10)____ more than a record of a voice already fallen silent.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["many","much"],"answer":"many","explanation":"<b>As many as half</b> stresses how large the number of languages is; <b>as much as half</b> is also accepted because <b>half</b> can be read as a proportion. <b>As far as</b> or <b>as long as</b> would introduce a limit or condition, not a quantity."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["on","ahead","by","along"],"answer":"on","explanation":"To <b>get on</b> (or <b>get ahead</b>) in the world is to succeed and make progress; <b>get by</b> or <b>get along</b>, meaning to manage, are weaker but defensible. <b>Get up</b> or <b>get out</b> would lose the idea of advancement that motivates the parents."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"A defining relative clause with <b>expressions</b> as the object of <b>translate</b> takes <b>that</b> or <b>which</b>. <b>What</b> cannot follow a noun it describes, and <b>who</b> is reserved for people."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"<b>No more … than</b> makes the comparison: the loss is supposedly no sadder <b>than</b> the loss of a Latin dialect. <b>As</b> would need <b>as much</b> before it, and <b>like</b> cannot complete a <b>more</b> comparison."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["with","for"],"answer":"with","explanation":"<b>With good reason</b> and <b>for good reason</b> both mean that the disagreement is justified. <b>In good reason</b> and <b>by good reason</b> are not English expressions."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["each","every"],"answer":"each","explanation":"<b>Each time</b> or <b>every time</b> a language disappears, a record goes with it; the singular noun <b>time</b> requires a singular distributive determiner. <b>All time</b> is not a linker, and <b>any time</b> would suggest choice rather than repetition."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["own"],"answer":"own","explanation":"<b>On their own</b> means without help from anywhere else, which the contrast with kitchens and markets makes clear. <b>On their part</b> would attribute an action to them, and <b>by their own</b> is not idiomatic."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["back"],"answer":"back","explanation":"A language <b>brought back from</b> a handful of speakers has been recovered from near extinction, in the pattern <b>bring back from the brink</b>. <b>Brought up</b> means raised, and <b>brought down</b> means reduced or defeated."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["worth"],"answer":"worth","explanation":"<b>Worth</b> is followed directly by a gerund: <b>worth keeping</b>. <b>Worthy</b> would need <b>of being kept</b>, and <b>deserving</b> is not a natural collocation with a gerund here."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["little","nothing","no"],"answer":"little","explanation":"<b>Amounts to little more than</b> (or <b>nothing / no more than</b>) belittles the documentation as a mere recording. <b>Much more than</b> would reverse the meaning, and <b>a little more than</b> would need two words."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-08-4","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"Two Wheels and a Long Road","topic":"the invention of the bicycle","passage":"The bicycle is (1)____ a simple machine that it is easy to assume it must always have existed. In fact, it is younger than the railway. The first recognisable ancestor appeared in Germany in 1817, when a baron built a wooden frame with two wheels (2)____ the rider pushed along with his feet. It had no pedals, no chain and no brakes, and it was ridiculed in the newspapers, (3)____ it sold in surprising numbers for a couple of summers before the novelty wore off.<br><br>Pedals were not added (4)____ the 1860s, and even then they were fixed to the front wheel, so that one turn of the legs meant one turn of the wheel. To go faster, builders simply made the front wheel bigger, (5)____ in the famous high-wheeled machine that stood taller than its rider. It was fast, elegant and dangerous: a stone in the road could send the rider flying (6)____ the handlebars, and injuries were so common that the accident had its own name, a 'header'.<br><br>The breakthrough (7)____ about in 1885 with the so-called safety bicycle. Its two wheels were the same size, its seat was low, and a chain (8)____ the pedals to the rear wheel, allowing gearing to replace sheer wheel size. Three years later an inflatable rubber tyre, invented to make a child's tricycle more comfortable, (9)____ the jolting ride into a smooth one. Within a decade, millions were cycling, women (10)____ men, and the shape settled on then has hardly changed since.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the best option.","options":["such","so","too","very"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Before <b>a</b> plus adjective plus noun, the result structure uses <b>such … that</b>: <b>such a simple machine that</b>. <b>So</b> would need the order <b>so simple a machine</b>, and <b>too</b> or <b>very</b> cannot introduce a <b>that</b> clause of result."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the best option.","options":["whose","which","what","where"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Wheels</b> is the object of <b>pushed</b>, so a defining relative pronoun for things is required: <b>wheels which the rider pushed</b>. <b>What</b> cannot follow a noun, <b>where</b> needs a place, and <b>whose</b> would need a following noun."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the best option.","options":["for","nor","yet","so"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Ridicule in the press and strong sales are in contrast, and <b>yet</b> joins two clauses with that meaning. <b>So</b> would make the sales a result of the ridicule, <b>for</b> would make them the reason, and <b>nor</b> needs a preceding negative and inversion."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the best option.","options":["since","by","from","until"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Not … until</b> marks the moment something finally happened, which <b>even then</b> picks up. <b>Not added since</b> would mean nothing has been added from that time onwards, <b>by</b> would leave open whether pedals were ever added, and <b>from</b> does not combine with a negative this way."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the best option.","options":["resulting","leading","causing","producing"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Only <b>result</b> takes the preposition <b>in</b> for a consequence: <b>resulting in the high-wheeled machine</b>. <b>Leading</b> would need <b>to</b>, and <b>causing</b> or <b>producing</b> take a direct object with no preposition."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the best option.","options":["onto","over","above","across"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A rider thrown forward goes <b>over the handlebars</b>, passing from one side to the other, which is why the accident was called a header. <b>Above</b> describes a position rather than a movement, <b>across</b> suggests a sideways path, and <b>onto</b> would land the rider on the bars."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the best option.","options":["went","got","came","brought"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Come about</b> means to happen, so <b>the breakthrough came about in 1885</b>. <b>Bring about</b> needs an object (someone brings a change about), <b>go about</b> means to tackle a task, and <b>get about</b> means to move around."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the best option.","options":["fixed","tied","stuck","connected"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A chain <b>connects</b> the pedals <b>to</b> the rear wheel, transmitting motion between separate parts, which is what makes gearing possible. <b>Fixed</b> would repeat the rigid arrangement of the earlier design, and <b>tied</b> or <b>stuck</b> describe fastening with rope or glue."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the best option.","options":["turned","became","got","grew"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The tyre changed one thing into another, and only <b>turn</b> takes an object followed by <b>into</b>: <b>turned the jolting ride into a smooth one</b>. <b>Became</b>, <b>got</b> and <b>grew</b> describe a subject changing itself, not changing something else."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the best option.","options":["alongside","besides","together","along"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Women alongside men</b> means both groups took part side by side. <b>Together</b> and <b>along</b> would need <b>with</b>, and <b>besides men</b> would mean 'in addition to men' in a way that does not describe shared activity."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-09-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Compasses in the Sky","topic":"migration of birds","passage":"Few sights in nature rival thousands of geese lifting off a marsh at dawn, yet the mechanics of how they find their way are (1)____ to be fully understood. What is certain is that no single sense accounts for the feat. Birds appear to draw (2)____ a whole toolkit: the setting sun, the rotating stars, the smell of the coast and, most remarkably, the Earth's magnetic field, which they seem to perceive through specialised proteins in the eye. (3)____ sooner had researchers demonstrated this magnetic sense in the laboratory than a new puzzle arose, for a compass tells a traveller which way is north but not where it currently is. Somehow the birds manage (4)____ only to hold a heading but also to correct for being blown hundreds of kilometres off course. Young birds on their first journey seem to inherit a rough programme, an instruction to fly in a given direction for a given number of weeks, (5)____ older birds refine this with a memory of landmarks built up over previous seasons. This would explain why juveniles displaced by storms tend to end (6)____ in the wrong place while adults usually recover. The costs are severe: a small warbler may lose nearly half its body weight crossing the Sahara, and every year countless birds fail to complete the trip. (7)____ the risks, the strategy persists because the rewards, chiefly the brief but enormous abundance of insects in the northern summer, outweigh them. Nowadays, however, timing that evolution fine-tuned over millennia is being thrown (8)____ of step with a warming climate. Insects are emerging earlier, and birds (9)____ arrive on the old schedule find the feast already over. Whether they can adjust quickly enough is a question (10)____ which the answer, for many species, will decide their survival.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["yet","still"],"answer":"yet","explanation":"<b>Be yet to</b> (or <i>be still to</i>) means something has not happened up to now: the mechanics are <b>yet to be</b> fully understood. <i>Far</i> would need <i>from</i>, not <i>to be</i>, and <i>not</i> cannot stand alone before <i>to be</i> here."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["on","upon"],"answer":"on","explanation":"To <b>draw on</b> (or <i>draw upon</i>) resources means to make use of them, which is what the birds do with their toolkit of senses. <i>Draw from</i> is used for taking something out of a source, and <i>draw in</i> means attract."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["No","no"],"answer":"No","explanation":"The inverted structure <b>No sooner had</b> … <b>than</b> … means one event followed immediately after another. <i>Hardly</i> or <i>scarcely</i> would need <i>when</i>, not <i>than</i>, and neither combines with <i>sooner</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["not"],"answer":"not","explanation":"The correlative pair <b>not only … but also</b> links the two infinitives <i>to hold</i> and <i>to correct</i>. <i>Never</i> or <i>no</i> would break the pairing with <i>but also</i>, and <i>just</i> cannot be followed by <i>only</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["while","whereas","and","but","although","though"],"answer":"while","explanation":"The sentence contrasts what young birds do with what older birds do, so a contrast linker such as <b>while</b> or <i>whereas</i> fits; a plain <i>and</i> or <i>but</i> is also acceptable. <i>Because</i> or <i>so</i> would wrongly make one behaviour the cause or result of the other."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>end up</b> (somewhere) means finish a journey in that place, often unintentionally. <i>End in</i> describes the outcome of an event, not a location, and <i>end at</i> is not idiomatic with a wrong place."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["Despite","despite","Notwithstanding","notwithstanding","Whatever","whatever"],"answer":"Despite","explanation":"A concessive word is needed before the noun phrase <i>the risks</i>: <b>Despite</b> (or <i>Notwithstanding</i>, or <i>Whatever</i>) the risks, the strategy persists. <i>Although</i> is a conjunction and would need a clause with a verb after it, and <i>For</i> on its own does not work here: the idiom is <i>For all the risks</i>, with <i>all</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"To be <b>out of step with</b> something is to no longer match it, and <i>thrown out of step</i> is the natural collocation. <i>Off</i> would need a different noun (<i>thrown off course</i>), and <i>away</i> does not combine with <i>of step</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"A defining relative pronoun is needed to identify which birds: <b>birds that</b> (or <i>which</i>) <b>arrive on the old schedule</b>. <i>Who</i> is normally reserved for people, and <i>whose</i> would need a following noun."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The formal relative structure <b>a question to which the answer</b> … keeps the preposition in front of <i>which</i>: the answer <i>to</i> a question. <i>Of</i> or <i>for</i> do not collocate with <i>answer</i> in this sense."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-09-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Stone Statements","topic":"medieval castles","passage":"To the modern visitor a castle is a romantic ruin, but to the people who built one it was (1)____ more than a fortress: it was a statement. The earliest examples in Britain were little more than earth mounds topped with wooden towers, thrown (2)____ in weeks by conquerors who needed to hold territory before the local population could organise resistance. Only later, once the land was secure, (3)____ stone replace timber, and with stone came a new scale of ambition. A great keep might take twenty years to complete and consume the income of an entire region, which is (4)____ so few lords could afford one. The design was anything but haphazard. Walls were angled so that attackers approaching the gate would be exposed to arrows from two sides at (5)____; staircases spiralled clockwise so that a defender retreating upwards could swing his sword freely while an attacker below could not. Even the latrines, which emptied into the moat, played a part (6)____ discouraging anyone from swimming across. Yet for all its military ingenuity, a castle was rarely taken by assault. Far more often the besiegers simply waited, cutting (7)____ supplies until hunger did the work that catapults could not. Had gunpowder never reached Europe, the castle might have survived for centuries; (8)____ it was, the cannon made high walls a liability rather than a strength within a few generations. By the sixteenth century the nobility had abandoned the keep for the comfortable country house, and the fortresses they left behind were quarried for their stone. What remains today is, in most cases, (9)____ a fraction of the original, and it takes a considerable act of imagination to picture the noise, smoke and crowding of a castle in (10)____ prime.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["far","much","rather"],"answer":"far","explanation":"<b>Far more than</b> (or <i>much more than</i>) intensifies the comparison: the castle was not merely a fortress. <i>Very</i> cannot modify a comparative, and <i>even</i> would suggest an unexpected addition rather than an intensified one."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"To <b>throw up</b> a structure is to build it hastily, exactly what conquerors in a hurry did. <i>Throw out</i> means discard, and <i>throw down</i> means hurl to the ground; neither describes rapid building."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"A sentence beginning with restrictive <b>Only later</b> triggers inversion, so the auxiliary <b>did</b> comes before the subject <i>stone</i>: <i>only later did stone replace timber</i>. <i>Was</i> would need a passive participle, and no inversion at all would be ungrammatical after <i>Only later</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["why"],"answer":"why","explanation":"<b>Which is why</b> introduces the consequence of the enormous cost: few lords could afford a keep. <i>Because</i> would reverse the direction of cause and effect, and <i>how</i> would describe a method rather than a reason."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["once"],"answer":"once","explanation":"<b>At once</b> here means <i>at the same time</i>: arrows came from two sides simultaneously. <i>At last</i> would mean finally, and <i>at all</i> only works in negative or question contexts."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"The collocation is <b>play a part in</b> doing something, so <b>in</b> is followed by the -ing form <i>discouraging</i>. <i>Of</i> and <i>for</i> do not combine with <i>play a part</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["off"],"answer":"off","explanation":"To <b>cut off</b> supplies is to prevent them from reaching their destination, which is what a besieging army did. <i>Cut down</i> means reduce, and <i>cut out</i> means remove or stop entirely, neither of which describes a blockade."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["as","As"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The idiom <b>as it was</b> means <i>in reality, as things actually turned out</i>, and it answers the preceding unreal conditional. <i>How it was</i> or <i>so it was</i> do not carry this contrast between the imagined and the real."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["only","just","merely","but"],"answer":"only","explanation":"A limiting adverb such as <b>only</b> (or <i>just</i>, <i>merely</i>, formal <i>but</i>) stresses how little survives: <i>only a fraction of the original</i>. <i>Even</i> would imply surprise that so much remains, the opposite of the intended sense."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["its"],"answer":"its","explanation":"The idiom <b>in its prime</b> means at the height of its strength; the possessive refers back to <i>a castle</i>. <i>It's</i> is a contraction of <i>it is</i>, and <i>the prime</i> is not idiomatic with a subject."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-09-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Seeing Red, Feeling Blue","topic":"the psychology of colour","passage":"Ask a group of people to name a calming colour and most (1)____ them will say blue; ask for an alarming one and red will win by a wide margin. Such agreement is striking, and it has led many to conclude that colour acts on the mind directly, (2)____ of culture or experience. The evidence, however, is far from straightforward. It is true that red has been shown to raise the pulse slightly and that athletes wearing it win marginally more often, an effect (3)____ to the association between red and dominance in many species. Yet the same colour signals luck in one country and danger in another, and a bride dressed in white would be a cause for grief in cultures where white is the colour of mourning. What seems innate, in other words, may be (4)____ more than a habit learnt so early that we no longer notice having learnt it. Marketing departments have not waited for the argument to be settled. Fast-food chains favour warm colours on the (5)____ that they stimulate appetite and encourage customers to eat quickly and leave; banks lean towards navy and grey, shades that are supposed to suggest stability, (6)____ the balance sheet actually says. Whether these choices work is another matter: a company (7)____ logo is the wrong shade of orange will not go bankrupt on that account. The most robust finding is perhaps the least dramatic. Colour affects us, but only in context, only briefly, and only at the margins. Anyone who claims that painting a classroom green will make children behave has, (8)____ all likelihood, mistaken a fashion for a law of nature, and it is worth remembering that such claims (9)____ been made, and quietly abandoned, for well (10)____ a century.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"Before a pronoun, a quantifier needs <b>of</b>: <b>most of them</b>. <i>Most them</i> is ungrammatical, and <i>most people</i> would not need <i>of</i> but here the word after the blank is <i>them</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["regardless","irrespective","independently","independent"],"answer":"regardless","explanation":"<b>Regardless of</b> (or <i>irrespective of</i>, <i>independently of</i>) means without being affected by, which fits the claim that colour works on everyone the same way. <i>Despite</i> and <i>instead</i> cannot be followed by <i>of</i> with this meaning."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["attributed","attributable","due","owing","linked","traced","related","ascribed"],"answer":"attributed","explanation":"<b>An effect attributed to</b> a cause (or <i>attributable</i>, <i>due</i>, <i>owing</i>, <i>linked</i>, <i>related to</i>) says where the effect comes from. <i>Caused</i> is impossible because it would need <i>by</i>, not <i>to</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["little","nothing","no"],"answer":"little","explanation":"<b>Little more than</b> (or <i>nothing more than</i>, <i>no more than</i>) plays down the supposed instinct as merely a habit. <i>Much more than</i> would say the opposite, and <i>far</i> would also intensify rather than minimise."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["grounds","assumption","theory","basis","principle","premise","understanding","belief","notion"],"answer":"grounds","explanation":"<b>On the grounds that</b> (or <i>on the assumption</i>, <i>theory</i>, <i>basis</i>, <i>premise</i>, <i>understanding</i>, <i>belief</i> <i>that</i>) gives the reason for a decision. <i>Reason</i> would need <i>for the reason that</i>, not <i>on the</i>, and <i>ground</i> in the singular is not the idiom."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["whatever"],"answer":"whatever","explanation":"<b>Whatever the balance sheet actually says</b> means the colours are meant to suggest stability no matter what the real figures show. <i>Despite</i> is a preposition and cannot be followed directly by a clause, and <i>however</i> would need an adjective or adverb after it."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"The possessive relative <b>whose</b> links the company to its logo: <i>a company whose logo is the wrong shade</i>. <i>Which</i> or <i>that</i> would leave <i>logo</i> without a determiner, and <i>its</i> cannot introduce a relative clause."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in all likelihood</b>, meaning very probably. <i>With</i> or <i>by</i> are not used with <i>likelihood</i> in this idiom."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["have"],"answer":"have","explanation":"The time phrase <i>for well over a century</i> reaches up to the present, so the present perfect passive is needed: such claims <b>have been made</b>. <i>Had</i> would place the claims before some other past point, and <i>were</i> would need a finished period."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["over","above"],"answer":"over","explanation":"<b>Well over a century</b> means considerably more than a hundred years; <i>well above</i> is a rarer but defensible alternative. <i>Well more than</i> is not idiomatic, and <i>beyond</i> does not combine with <i>well</i> before a period of time in this way."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-09-4","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"Harvest from the Sand","topic":"desert farming","passage":"Growing food where rain falls only a few times a year sounds futile, yet farmers have done precisely that for thousands of years, and their methods are attracting fresh attention as the climate warms. The oldest trick is simply to (1)____ the most of what little water there is. In the Negev, ancient farmers channelled the run-off from bare hillsides into small walled fields, (2)____ a single storm could deliver the equivalent of a season's rain. Modern versions, from drip irrigation to buried clay pots that release moisture slowly, (3)____ on the same principle: deliver water to the root and nowhere else. Choice of crop matters just as much. Date palms and millet can (4)____ levels of heat and salt that would kill wheat within days, and breeders are now combing wild relatives of common crops for drought-tolerance genes. Perhaps the most ambitious projects, however, are those that set (5)____ to reverse the desert's advance altogether. Along the Sahara's edge, rows of trees are being planted to hold back the dunes and shade the soil, keeping evaporation (6)____ a minimum and slowly restoring the organic matter that overgrazing stripped away. Critics note that such schemes have a poor record; too many have been (7)____ with great fanfare and then quietly forgotten once the funding dried up. There is also the question of who owns the water. Pumping fossil aquifers to grow fodder for export may pay in the (8)____ term, but the water took ten thousand years to accumulate and will not be replaced. (9)____ desert farming can feed the growing populations of the world's dry regions depends less on technology than on whether governments will value water as the scarce resource it is. Without that shift, even the cleverest irrigation will do little more than put (10)____ the inevitable.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["get","do","take","make"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The idiom is <b>make the most of</b> something, meaning use it as fully as possible. <i>Take</i> and <i>get</i> combine with <i>the most</i> only as quantities, and <i>do the most</i> is not a set phrase."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["whereupon","whereas","whereby","wherein"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Whereby</b> means <i>by means of which</i>: the fields were the arrangement through which one storm delivered a season's rain. <i>Whereas</i> contrasts, <i>whereupon</i> means <i>immediately after which</i>, and <i>wherein</i> would refer to a place or document, not a method."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["sit","rest","lie","stand"],"correct":1,"explanation":"An idea or method <b>rests on</b> a principle, meaning it is based on it. <i>Stand on principle</i> is a different idiom (refuse to compromise), and <i>lie on</i> or <i>sit on</i> describe physical position."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["tolerate","accept","allow","permit"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A plant <b>tolerates</b> heat and salt when it can survive them. <i>Accept</i>, <i>allow</i> and <i>permit</i> all involve agreeing to something, which a crop cannot do; they are near misses because <i>tolerate</i> can also mean <i>put up with</i> in human contexts."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["up","about","off","out"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Set out to do</b> something means begin with that as your aim. <i>Set about</i> must be followed by an -ing form, <i>set off</i> means depart on a journey, and <i>set up</i> means establish."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","at","to","in"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>keep something to a minimum</b>, meaning make it as small as possible. <i>At a minimum</i> means <i>at the very least</i>, a different idea, and <i>in</i> or <i>on</i> do not combine with <i>minimum</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["delivered","launched","released","issued"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A scheme or campaign is <b>launched</b>, and <i>launched with great fanfare</i> is the standard collocation. Films and products are <i>released</i>, statements are <i>issued</i>, and goods or speeches are <i>delivered</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["short","brief","small","quick"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in the short term</b>, contrasting with <i>the long term</i>. <i>Brief</i>, <i>small</i> and <i>quick</i> share the idea of limited size or time but do not collocate with <i>term</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["Unless","Although","If","Whether"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A clause acting as the subject of <i>depends</i> must begin with <b>Whether</b>. <i>If</i> cannot introduce a subject clause, and <i>Unless</i> or <i>Although</i> would leave the main verb <i>depends</i> without a subject."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["down","off","out","away"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To <b>put off</b> something is to postpone it, so an irrigation system can only <i>put off the inevitable</i>. <i>Put away</i> means store, <i>put down</i> means place or suppress, and <i>put out</i> means extinguish or publish."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-10-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Lanterns in the Dark","topic":"deep-sea creatures","passage":"Nowhere on Earth is life stranger than in the deep ocean, where sunlight has never reached and the pressure would crush any submarine (1)____ was not built for it. Only in the last few decades (2)____ scientists begun to map this world in any detail. Every expedition, meanwhile, seems to turn (3)____ a creature that nobody had imagined. Some fish carry their own lanterns, tiny organs (4)____ glow produces just enough light to lure prey within reach. Others have done away (5)____ eyes altogether, relying instead on smell and on the faint vibrations of the water. Food is so scarce (6)____ a single dead whale sinking to the seabed can feed a whole community for years. Far (7)____ being a wasteland, then, the deep sea turns out to be one of the richest habitats on the planet. What makes these discoveries urgent, (8)____, is that mining companies are now looking at the same seabed for metals. Had the deep ocean been easier to reach, it (9)____ almost certainly have been damaged long ago. As things stand, we may be on the (10)____ of destroying a world we have barely begun to understand.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"A defining relative clause with the subject missing needs a relative pronoun: <b>that</b> or <b>which</b>. <i>Who</i> is for people, and <i>it</i> would leave two finite clauses with nothing joining them."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["have"],"answer":"have","explanation":"After a fronted <i>Only in…</i> phrase the auxiliary comes before the subject, and <i>begun</i> is a past participle, so the auxiliary must be <b>have</b>. <i>Did</i> would need the base form <i>begin</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"<b>Turn up</b> means to find or produce something unexpectedly. <i>Turn out</i> would mean to prove to be, and cannot take a creature as its object."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"The glow belongs to the organs, so the possessive relative <b>whose</b> is needed. <i>Which</i> would need a verb straight after it and leaves <i>glow produces</i> without a subject."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"The phrasal verb is <b>do away with</b> something, meaning to get rid of it. <i>Do away from</i> does not exist, and <i>without</i> would turn the sentence into nonsense."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<i>So scarce</i> sets up a result clause, and the result is introduced by <b>that</b>. <i>Because</i> or <i>as</i> would give a reason, which is the wrong logic."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["from"],"answer":"from","explanation":"<b>Far from</b> being X means not at all X. <i>Far of</i> is not English, and <i>far beyond</i> would suggest the deep sea is more than a wasteland rather than not one."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["however","though","nevertheless","nonetheless"],"answer":"however","explanation":"The sentence contrasts a hopeful discovery with a threat, so a contrastive adverb between commas is needed: <b>however</b> or <b>though</b>. <i>But</i> cannot sit inside commas in mid-sentence, and <i>therefore</i> makes no logical sense."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["would"],"answer":"would","explanation":"This is an inverted third conditional (<i>Had it been…</i>), whose main clause takes <b>would</b> + have + past participle. <i>Might</i> or <i>could</i> clash with <i>almost certainly</i>, which states a firm result."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["brink","verge","point","edge","cusp"],"answer":"brink","explanation":"<b>On the brink of</b> (or <i>verge</i>, <i>point</i>, <i>edge</i>) doing something means about to do it. <i>Way</i> or <i>road</i> collocate with <i>on the way to</i>, not with <i>of</i> + -ing."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-10-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"More Than Silk","topic":"the Silk Road","passage":"The Silk Road was never a single road, (1)____ a shifting network of tracks that linked China with the Mediterranean for well over a thousand years. Silk itself accounted for only a fraction (2)____ what travelled along it; paper, glass, horses and spices changed hands just as often, and so, less happily, did disease. No merchant ever made the whole journey. Goods were passed from one caravan to (3)____ at oasis towns, each middleman adding his own mark-up, (4)____ is why a bolt of Chinese silk could cost its weight in gold by the time it reached Rome. It was not (5)____ the fifteenth century, when sea routes around Africa opened up, that the overland trade began to decline. Ideas, however, proved more durable (6)____ any cargo. Buddhism reached China along these paths, as did techniques for making paper, and the mathematics that Europe later took (7)____ granted. Had it not been for this exchange, the Renaissance (8)____ well have looked very different. Today the name has been revived by governments keen to cash (9)____ on its romance, though the trains and pipelines of the new routes bear little resemblance to a camel train. The history is a reminder that trade, (10)____ its faults, has always carried more than goods.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["but","rather"],"answer":"but","explanation":"<i>Never X, <b>but</b> Y</i> (or <i>rather</i> Y) corrects a false idea with the true one. <i>And</i> would make the two descriptions both true, which contradicts <i>never</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"A quantity word such as <i>fraction</i>, <i>share</i> or <i>part</i> is linked to its whole by <b>of</b>. <i>From</i> would suggest silk was taken out of the goods, not that it formed part of them."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["another"],"answer":"another","explanation":"The fixed pair is <i>from one … to <b>another</b></i>. <i>Other</i> cannot stand alone as a singular pronoun, and <i>the next</i> would be two words."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A comma followed by <b>which</b> refers back to the whole previous idea (the mark-ups). <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma in this way, and <i>it</i> would create a run-on sentence."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["until","till"],"answer":"until","explanation":"This is the cleft pattern <i>It was not <b>until</b> … that …</i>, stressing when something finally happened. <i>Before</i> or <i>in</i> do not work with <i>not … that</i> in this frame."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The comparative <i>more durable</i> must be completed with <b>than</b>. <i>As</i> belongs to <i>as durable as</i>, and <i>to</i> follows only comparatives such as <i>superior</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"The idiom is <b>take something for granted</b>. <i>As</i> is tempting after <i>take</i>, but <i>take as granted</i> is not an English expression."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["might","may","could"],"answer":"might","explanation":"<b>Might</b> (or <i>may</i>, <i>could</i>) <i>well have</i> + past participle expresses a strong past possibility in a conditional. <i>Would well have</i> is not idiomatic, and <i>should</i> changes the meaning to duty."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"The three-part verb is <b>cash in on</b> something, meaning to profit from it. <i>Cash on</i> or <i>cash out on</i> do not carry that meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["despite","whatever","notwithstanding"],"answer":"despite","explanation":"<b>Despite</b> (or <i>whatever</i>) <i>its faults</i> concedes the drawbacks before making a positive claim. <i>For</i> only works in the longer phrase <i>for all its faults</i>, and <i>although</i> needs a clause with a verb."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-10-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Foxes on the High Street","topic":"urban wildlife","passage":"Cities were never designed (1)____ animals in mind, yet a surprising number have moved in regardless. Foxes now breed in the suburbs of London at a greater density than in the countryside, and peregrine falcons, once close to extinction, nest on the ledges of office towers, (2)____ pigeons provide an endless supply of food. What sets these species (3)____ is their adaptability: a raccoon will learn to open a bin within days, and the crows of one Japanese city have been seen dropping walnuts onto pedestrian crossings so that passing cars crack them (4)____. Not everyone welcomes the newcomers. Residents complain of overturned rubbish, dug-up lawns and, (5)____ the case of wild boar in some European cities, damage to cars. Councils are therefore torn (6)____ two demands: to protect wildlife and to keep the streets tidy. Nor is the traffic harmless to the animals themselves, (7)____ have to cross roads that were never meant for them. Even so, ecologists argue that the city may (8)____ out to be a refuge rather than a threat. Rarely (9)____ a habitat expanded so quickly, and the animals that succeed in it may be the ones best placed to survive whatever the next century brings. The question is not whether we can keep them (10)____, but whether we should even try.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>with</b> somebody or something <i>in mind</i>, meaning while thinking of them. <i>For</i> is tempting after <i>designed</i>, but <i>designed for animals in mind</i> mixes two different patterns."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["where","as","since","because"],"answer":"where","explanation":"The clause says what happens at the office towers, a place, so the relative adverb <b>where</b> fits best; a causal linker (<i>as</i>, <i>since</i>, <i>because</i>) also works, explaining why the falcons nest there. <i>Which</i> would need a preposition (<i>on which</i>) to make sense."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["apart"],"answer":"apart","explanation":"<b>Set something apart</b> means to make it different from the rest. <i>Set out</i> and <i>set up</i> exist but mean to start a journey or to establish something."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["open"],"answer":"open","explanation":"You <b>crack</b> a nut <b>open</b>: the adjective describes the result of the cracking. <i>Up</i> would suggest breaking into small pieces rather than getting at the inside."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in the case of</b> something. <i>On</i> and <i>for</i> do not combine with <i>the case of</i> in this meaning."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["between"],"answer":"between","explanation":"To be <b>torn between</b> two things is to be pulled in two directions. <i>Among</i> is for more than two, and <i>by</i> would mean the demands were doing the tearing."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"After a comma, a non-defining relative clause about animals takes <b>which</b>. <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma here, and <i>who</i> is reserved for people."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["turn"],"answer":"turn","explanation":"<b>Turn out to be</b> means to prove to be in the end. <i>Work out</i> is not followed by <i>to be</i>, and <i>come out</i> gives the wrong meaning."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["has"],"answer":"has","explanation":"A negative adverb such as <i>rarely</i> at the front forces inversion, and with the singular subject <i>a habitat</i> and the participle <i>expanded</i> the auxiliary is <b>has</b>. <i>Did</i> would need <i>expand</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["out","away"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>Keep somebody out</b> (or <i>away</i>) means to prevent them from entering. <i>Off</i> would need a following object such as <i>the streets</i>, and <i>in</i> reverses the meaning."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-10-4","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"Prison or Ark?","topic":"the ethics of zoos","passage":"Few institutions divide opinion as sharply as the zoo. To its defenders, a modern zoo is (1)____ a prison than an ark: a place where species that would otherwise have vanished are kept alive until their habitats can be restored. The Arabian oryx, hunted to extinction in the wild by the 1970s, was bred in captivity and (2)____ reintroduced to the desert, and it is hard to (3)____ that this would have happened without zoos. Critics, (4)____, point out that such success stories are rare. The great majority of animals on display belong to species that are not endangered at all, and are there, (5)____ put it bluntly, because visitors like looking at them. Nor does a large enclosure (6)____ for a life spent on the move; an elephant that would walk thirty kilometres a day in Africa cannot do so behind a fence, however well it is fed. It is this gap between what an animal needs and what a zoo can (7)____ that troubles many biologists. There is, (8)____, a middle path. Some zoos have already (9)____ up their elephants and big cats, keeping only species that adapt well to confinement and spending the money saved on conservation abroad. Whether the public will keep coming to see frogs and beetles remains to be seen; but if zoos are to (10)____ their claim to be on the animals' side, they may have no choice.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["more","less","rather","fewer"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Less</b> X <i>than</i> Y means it is better described as Y than as X, which matches the defenders' view. <i>More</i> reverses the meaning, <i>rather</i> needs <i>rather than</i>, and <i>fewer</i> goes only with plural nouns."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["subsequently","formerly","previously","consequently"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The reintroduction came after the breeding, so the adverb of sequence is <b>subsequently</b>. <i>Previously</i> and <i>formerly</i> point backwards in time, and <i>consequently</i> claims a cause the sentence does not state."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["deny","accept","doubt","imagine"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The writer means the rescue could not have happened without zoos, so it is <i>hard to <b>imagine</b></i> the alternative. <i>Hard to deny</i> or <i>hard to doubt</i> would assert the opposite, and <i>accept</i> makes the sentence contradict itself."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["moreover","otherwise","however","therefore"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Critics are being contrasted with defenders, so the linker is <b>however</b>. <i>Therefore</i> and <i>moreover</i> would continue the defenders' argument, and <i>otherwise</i> needs a condition."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["as","to","for","in"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The comment phrase is <b>to put it bluntly</b>, an infinitive of purpose. <i>As</i> would need a finite verb (<i>as I put it</i>), and <i>for</i> and <i>in</i> cannot introduce a bare infinitive."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["compensate","substitute","replace","account"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Compensate for</b> means to make up for a loss, which is the idea here. <i>Replace</i> takes no preposition, <i>account for</i> means to explain, and <i>substitute for</i> would mean the enclosure is used instead of the walking, not that it makes up for its loss."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["perform","attain","fulfil","provide"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A zoo <b>provides</b> conditions, so <i>what a zoo can provide</i> pairs with <i>what an animal needs</i>. <i>Fulfil</i> takes a need as its object, not <i>what</i>, and <i>perform</i> and <i>attain</i> do not describe supplying living conditions."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["meanwhile","instead","nevertheless","likewise"],"correct":2,"explanation":"After the troubling gap, the writer offers hope despite it, which calls for the concessive <b>nevertheless</b>. <i>Likewise</i> adds a similar point, <i>meanwhile</i> marks time, and <i>instead</i> needs a rejected alternative."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["set","given","taken","put"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Give up</b> means to stop keeping or doing something, so the zoos have <i>given up</i> their elephants. <i>Taken up</i> means to start, <i>put up</i> means to build or tolerate, and <i>set up</i> means to establish."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["certify","approve","admit","justify"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To <b>justify a claim</b> is to show it is deserved. <i>Approve</i> and <i>certify</i> are done by an outside authority, not by the claimant, and <i>admit</i> would mean confessing to it."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-19-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"From Bitter Drink to Penny Bar","topic":"the history of chocolate","passage":"Long before chocolate became a bar wrapped in foil, it was a bitter drink. The peoples of ancient Mesoamerica roasted cacao seeds, ground them into a paste and whisked the result (1)____ water until it foamed. So highly (2)____ the seeds prized that they doubled as currency: a handful could buy a rabbit, and a sack, so the story goes, a slave. When Spanish sailors first tasted the drink in the sixteenth century, they found it revolting; only (3)____ sugar and cinnamon had been stirred in did it win favour at court. For the next two hundred years chocolate remained a luxury, (4)____ to the wealthy few who could afford the imported beans and the servants needed to prepare them. (5)____ changed everything was a Dutch chemist's press, which in 1828 squeezed the fat out of the beans and left a dry powder that dissolved easily. Not long (6)____, an English firm discovered that adding the fat back to sweetened powder produced a paste that could be moulded, and the solid bar was born. Had that press never been invented, chocolate might (7)____ have stayed a drink for the rich. Instead, factories sprang (8)____ across Europe, each competing to produce a smoother, cheaper product, and within a generation a treat once reserved for kings was being sold to children for a penny. It is worth remembering, (9)____, that the cacao tree itself still grows only in a narrow belt near the equator, and that the farmers who tend it have seldom shared in the fortunes made (10)____ their beans.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["with","in"],"answer":"with","explanation":"You whisk one thing <b>with</b> another to combine them; cooks also say <i>whisk the paste in water</i>, so <b>in</b> is accepted. <i>By</i> would name the tool, not the liquid, and <i>to</i> is not used after <i>whisk</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["were"],"answer":"were","explanation":"A fronted <i>So + adverb</i> forces inversion: <b>So highly were the seeds prized that</b>. The plural subject <i>seeds</i> and the past narrative rule out <i>was</i> and <i>are</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["when","after","once"],"answer":"when","explanation":"<b>Only when/after/once</b> + clause, fronted, triggers the inversion <i>did it win</i>. <i>Only if</i> would state a condition rather than the moment it happened, and <i>until</i> would contradict <i>only</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["confined","restricted","limited"],"answer":"confined","explanation":"A reduced passive clause: a luxury <b>confined/restricted/limited to</b> a small group. <i>Reserved</i> would need <i>for</i>, and <i>only</i> cannot take <i>to</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["What"],"answer":"What","explanation":"A wh-cleft sentence: <b>What changed everything was</b> a press. <i>That</i> and <i>Which</i> cannot open a sentence as a free relative meaning 'the thing that'."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["afterwards","afterward","after","later"],"answer":"afterwards","explanation":"<b>Not long afterwards/after/later</b> is the fixed time phrase for 'soon after that'. <i>Then</i> or <i>next</i> cannot follow <i>not long</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["well","otherwise","easily"],"answer":"well","explanation":"<b>Might well have</b> strengthens a past possibility; <i>otherwise</i> and <i>easily</i> also fit the unreal past after <i>Had that press never been invented</i>. <i>Never</i> would reverse the meaning, and <i>not</i> would make the bar the surprising outcome."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>spring up</b> means to appear suddenly in large numbers. <i>Spring out</i> describes leaping from a hiding place, not new factories opening."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["however","though","nevertheless","nonetheless"],"answer":"however","explanation":"Set off by commas mid-sentence, the contrast linker must be an adverb: <b>however/though/nevertheless</b>. <i>But</i> and <i>although</i> are conjunctions and cannot sit between commas after the verb."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["from","on"],"answer":"from","explanation":"A fortune is <b>made from</b> a product, marking its source; <i>fortunes made on</i> a commodity is also idiomatic. <i>Made of</i> describes the material something consists of, and <i>made by</i> would turn the beans into the earners."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-19-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Pull of the Moon","topic":"how tides work","passage":"Anyone who has spent a day at the beach will have noticed the sea creeping up the sand and then, some hours later, drawing back again. Few people, (1)____, could explain why. The answer lies not in the wind or the weather but in the moon, (2)____ gravity tugs at the oceans as it circles the Earth. The water on the side nearest the moon is pulled towards it and bulges outward; (3)____ surprisingly, a second bulge forms on the far side, where the Earth itself is drawn away from the water left behind. As the planet turns beneath these two bulges, (4)____ point on the coast passes through both of them once a day, which is why most shores see two high tides roughly twelve hours (5)____. The sun exerts a pull of its own, though a weaker one, and when the sun and moon line up at full and new moon their forces combine to produce the exceptionally high 'spring' tides, a name that has nothing to do (6)____ the season. Local geography complicates the picture (7)____ further: a funnel-shaped bay can amplify the rise to more than fifteen metres, whereas an enclosed sea may barely register a change (8)____ all. Sailors have relied on tide tables for centuries, and rightly (9)____, for a harbour that is deep at noon can be a mudflat by dusk. Not until the seventeenth century, however, (10)____ anyone able to set out the mathematics behind a rhythm that fishermen had trusted since the dawn of navigation.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["however","though"],"answer":"however","explanation":"A contrast adverb between commas after the subject: <b>Few people, however/though, could explain</b>. <i>But</i> is a conjunction and cannot be parenthetical here; <i>despite</i> needs a noun."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"The relative pronoun shows possession: the moon, <b>whose gravity</b> tugs at the oceans. <i>Which</i> would need <i>the gravity of which</i>, and <i>its</i> cannot introduce a relative clause."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["more","perhaps","rather"],"answer":"more","explanation":"<b>More surprisingly</b> introduces the less expected of two facts; <i>perhaps</i> and <i>rather</i> also modify the sentence adverb naturally. <i>Most</i> would need <i>the</i> and a set of three, and <i>very</i> does not sit before <i>surprisingly</i> as a comment adverb."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["every","each","any"],"answer":"every","explanation":"A singular determiner before <i>point</i> with the singular verb <i>passes</i>: <b>every/each/any point</b>. <i>All</i> would need <i>points pass</i>, and <i>the</i> would refer to one specific point."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["apart"],"answer":"apart","explanation":"An interval between two events is expressed as <b>twelve hours apart</b>. <i>Away</i> describes distance from a place, and <i>later</i> would need <i>than</i> to compare the two tides."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"The fixed expression is <b>have nothing to do with</b> something. <i>About</i> and <i>for</i> never complete this idiom."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["still","even","yet"],"answer":"still","explanation":"<b>Still/even/yet further</b> intensifies a comparative adverb. <i>More</i> cannot precede <i>further</i>, which is already comparative, and <i>much</i> would need <i>much further</i> without the verb object between."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>Barely ... at all</b> is the emphatic negative phrase. <i>In all</i> means 'in total' and <i>of all</i> follows a superlative, so neither fits after <i>a change</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["so"],"answer":"so","explanation":"<b>And rightly so</b> is a set phrase meaning 'and they were right to'. <i>Too</i> or <i>then</i> would not stand in for the whole previous clause the way <i>so</i> does."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["was"],"answer":"was","explanation":"A fronted <i>Not until</i> phrase inverts subject and auxiliary: <b>Not until the seventeenth century was anyone able</b>. <i>Did</i> would need a bare verb, but the sentence continues with <i>able</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-19-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Hobby Horses and Boneshakers","topic":"the first bicycles","passage":"The bicycle as we know it did not spring fully formed from a single inventor's workshop; (1)____ the contrary, it took the best part of a century to evolve. The earliest ancestor, built in Germany in 1817, had no pedals (2)____: the rider sat astride a wooden beam and pushed himself along with his feet, rather (3)____ a child does on a balance bike today. It was nicknamed the 'hobby horse', and for a few summers fashionable young men raced along city pavements, much to the annoyance of pedestrians. Pedals were not attached to the front wheel (4)____ the 1860s, when a French mechanic hit (5)____ the idea while repairing one of the old machines. His 'boneshaker', so called (6)____ of the punishing ride its iron tyres delivered over cobbles, sold in the thousands. Then designers reasoned that a bigger front wheel would cover more ground per turn of the pedals, and wheels grew until they stood taller (7)____ the rider. These 'high-wheelers' were fast but perilous: a stone in the road could pitch a man (8)____ over the handlebars. Had it not been (9)____ the invention of the chain drive in the 1880s, which allowed two wheels of equal size, cycling might have remained a sport for daring athletes. As it was, the 'safety bicycle', fitted with inflatable rubber tyres, brought cheap, independent travel within (10)____ of ordinary workers and, notably, of women, whose freedom to roam it enlarged more than any law had done.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The linking phrase is <b>on the contrary</b>, used to reject the idea just stated. <i>In contrast</i> exists, but <i>in the contrary</i> does not."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["whatsoever"],"answer":"whatsoever","explanation":"After a negative noun phrase, <b>no pedals whatsoever</b> adds emphasis in a single word. The familiar <i>at all</i> means the same but is two words, so it cannot fill one blank."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["as","like"],"answer":"as","explanation":"A comparison followed by a full clause (<i>a child does</i>) takes the conjunction <b>as</b>; <i>like</i> before a clause is informal but widely accepted. <i>Than</i> needs a comparative, and <i>so</i> makes no sense after <i>rather</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["until","till","before"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Not ... until/before the 1860s</b> places the first occurrence at that decade. <i>Since</i> would need a present perfect verb, and <i>in</i> would clash with the negative <i>were not attached</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["upon","on"],"answer":"upon","explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>hit upon/on an idea</b> means to think of it suddenly. <i>Hit at</i> or <i>hit with</i> would describe striking something physically."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["because"],"answer":"because","explanation":"Before a noun phrase the reason is given by <b>because of</b>. <i>Due</i> would need <i>to</i>, not <i>of</i>, and <i>as</i> or <i>since</i> must introduce a full clause."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The comparative <i>taller</i> is completed by <b>than</b>. <i>Over</i> or <i>above</i> would describe position, not a comparison of height."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["headlong","straight","right","clean"],"answer":"headlong","explanation":"An adverb intensifying the direction of a fall: <b>pitch someone headlong/straight/right/clean over</b>. <i>Down</i> would contradict <i>over</i>, and <i>forward</i> does not combine with <i>over the handlebars</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"The inverted conditional is <b>Had it not been for</b> + noun, meaning 'without'. <i>Of</i> and <i>by</i> do not form this idiom."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["reach"],"answer":"reach","explanation":"<b>Within reach of</b> someone means affordable or obtainable for them. <i>Within range of</i> refers to distance or weapons, and <i>within grasp</i> takes a possessive, not <i>of</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-19-4","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"A City That Ought Not to Exist","topic":"a city built on water","passage":"Venice ought not to exist. (1)____ sensible planner would choose a marsh of shifting mud, flooded twice a day, as the site for a great city, yet that is precisely where refugees fleeing invaders in the fifth century chose to settle. Safety, not comfort, was what they were (2)____; the lagoon's shallow channels, known only to locals, kept enemy fleets (3)____ bay. To build on such ground the settlers drove millions of wooden piles into the mud, packed so tightly (4)____ they formed a raft on which stone could rest. Deprived of oxygen beneath the silt, the timber never rotted; (5)____ anything, it hardened over the centuries into something resembling stone. On this improbable foundation rose palaces, churches and a trading empire that at its height (6)____ stretch from the Adriatic to the Black Sea. The city's very success, however, has come at a (7)____. Wells sunk in the twentieth century drew water from beneath the lagoon and the ground sank; meanwhile the sea has been rising. High water, once a nuisance a few times a winter, now floods the main square dozens of times a year, and (8)____ recently as 2019 it reached a level not seen for half a century. A vast barrier of movable gates has been built at the lagoon's mouth to hold the sea (9)____, but engineers concede that it buys time rather than a permanent solution. Whether the city that defied the sea for fifteen hundred years can go (10)____ doing so is a question Venetians would rather not dwell on.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["No"],"answer":"No","explanation":"The negative determiner <b>No sensible planner would</b> makes the whole statement emphatic without a separate <i>not</i>. <i>Any</i> would need a negative verb, and <i>None</i> cannot directly precede a noun."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["after","seeking","pursuing"],"answer":"after","explanation":"<b>Be after something</b> is the idiom for wanting or seeking it; <i>seeking</i> and <i>pursuing</i> express the same idea as verbs. <i>Looking</i> would need <i>for</i>, and <i>wanting</i> is not used this way in a cleft."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"The collocation is <b>keep something at bay</b>, meaning prevent it from coming close. <i>In</i> or <i>to bay</i> would break the idiom."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<b>So tightly that</b> introduces the result of the degree. <i>As</i> after <i>so</i> forms a comparison, not a result, and <i>which</i> would need a noun to refer back to."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["if"],"answer":"if","explanation":"<b>If anything</b> means 'on the contrary, to a small degree', correcting the expectation that the wood weakened. <i>Of anything</i> and <i>for anything</i> are not linking phrases."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["would","could"],"answer":"would","explanation":"A modal describing a past state of affairs: the empire <b>would/could stretch</b> so far at its peak. <i>Had</i> would need <i>stretched</i>, and <i>did</i> is not used for an unstressed statement of fact here."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["price","cost"],"answer":"price","explanation":"<b>Come at a price/cost</b> means to bring disadvantages with it. <i>Fee</i> and <i>charge</i> refer to sums actually paid, not to consequences."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"<b>As recently as 2019</b> stresses how short a time ago something happened. <i>So recently as</i> is not used in the affirmative, and <i>more recently</i> cannot pair with <i>as</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["back","off","out"],"answer":"back","explanation":"<b>Hold back/off/out</b> the sea means stop it advancing. <i>Hold up</i> means delay or support, and <i>hold down</i> means keep under control, neither of which fits a barrier."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"<b>Go on doing something</b> means continue. <i>Go through</i> and <i>go over</i> mean experience or review, and <i>keep</i> is not available after <i>can go</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-19-5","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Rumble on the Sofa","topic":"why cats purr","passage":"Anyone who has shared a sofa with a cat knows the sound: a low, steady rumble that seems to come from nowhere in particular. What is far (1)____ obvious is how cats produce it, and (2)____ they do so at all. For much of the last century scientists assumed the noise came from blood rushing through a vein in the chest; only in the 1990s was it shown that the muscles of the larynx twitch around twenty-five times a second, opening and closing the airway (3)____ the cat breathes in and out. As for the purpose, the obvious answer, contentment, turns (4)____ to be only part of the story. Cats purr when they are injured, when they are frightened at the vet's and even when they are dying, which hardly suggests pleasure. One theory (5)____ that the frequency of a purr lies within the range known to stimulate bone growth and healing in mammals, so a cat may, in effect, be treating itself. Another points out that kittens purr while feeding, (6)____ signalling to their mother that all is well and keeping her calm enough to lie still. Whichever explanation proves correct, (7)____ is certain that cats have learned to exploit the sound. Researchers have found that a cat wanting food embeds a high-pitched cry, not unlike a baby's, within its purr, and that owners respond to it (8)____ realising why. Far from being a simple expression of happiness, (9)____, the purr appears to be a tool, refined over thousands of years of living (10)____ us, for getting exactly what a cat wants.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["less"],"answer":"less","explanation":"The contrast is between the familiar sound and its unexplained cause, so <b>far less obvious</b> is needed. <i>More</i> would claim the mechanism is easier to see than the sound itself, the opposite of the point."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["why"],"answer":"why","explanation":"The second embedded question asks for the reason, which the rest of the passage then explores: <b>why they do so at all</b>. <i>Whether</i> would question whether purring happens, which nobody doubts."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["as","while","when","whenever"],"answer":"as","explanation":"A time conjunction linking two simultaneous actions: the airway opens <b>as/while/when the cat breathes</b>. <i>During</i> is a preposition and cannot take a clause, and <i>because</i> would state a cause instead of a time."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>Turn out to be</b> means prove to be in the end. <i>Turn up</i> means arrive, and <i>turn into</i> would need a noun rather than <i>to be</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["holds","suggests","proposes","argues","claims","maintains","says","is"],"answer":"holds","explanation":"A theory <b>holds/suggests/proposes that</b> something is the case; the plain <i>is that</i> also works. The verb must be singular present after <i>One theory</i>, so <i>hold</i> or <i>held</i> would break agreement or tense."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["thereby","thus","perhaps","possibly","apparently"],"answer":"thereby","explanation":"<b>Thereby/thus signalling</b> introduces the result of the action just described; <i>perhaps</i> or <i>possibly</i> softens it. <i>Therefore</i> is not used before an -ing participle in this position."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["it"],"answer":"it","explanation":"Anticipatory <b>it</b> stands for the that-clause: <i>it is certain that cats have learned</i>. <i>This</i> or <i>there</i> cannot introduce a delayed that-clause subject this way."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"<b>Without realising</b> is the preposition plus -ing pattern meaning 'not knowing'. <i>Before</i> would claim they later work it out, and <i>not</i> cannot stand alone before a participle here."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["then","therefore","thus"],"answer":"then","explanation":"A concluding adverb between commas draws the passage together: <b>then/therefore/thus</b>. <i>However</i> would signal a contrast with the previous sentence, which actually supports this one."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["with","alongside","among","amongst","beside"],"answer":"with","explanation":"<b>Living with/alongside/among</b> people describes sharing their homes. <i>Living by us</i> would mean simply nearby, and <i>living on us</i> would make cats parasites."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-19-6","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"Rags, Pulp and the Printed Page","topic":"the story of paper","passage":"Few inventions have been taken so thoroughly for (1)____ as paper. The credit for it is usually given to a Chinese court official in the second century, though scraps found in tombs suggest that people had been making something similar for two hundred years before he described the method (2)____ writing. The recipe was simple enough: rags, bark and old fishing nets were soaked, beaten to a pulp and spread thinly over a screen to dry. What was not simple was keeping the method secret, and for six centuries China (3)____ in doing so. The knowledge is said to have leaked out only when Chinese papermakers were taken prisoner after a battle in Central Asia in 751 and agreed to teach their captors (4)____ exchange for their freedom. From there the craft moved slowly westward, reaching Spain in the twelfth century and England not (5)____ the fifteenth. Europeans were at first suspicious of the new material, which seemed flimsy (6)____ with parchment made from animal skin; some officials even banned its use for legal documents. Printing put (7)____ to that. Once a press could turn out hundreds of pages an hour, no amount of sheep could keep (8)____ with demand, and paper mills spread along every river fast enough to drive a waterwheel. The shift to wood pulp in the nineteenth century (9)____ prices down further still, and with cheap paper came cheap newspapers, cheap novels and, (10)____ the first time, the expectation that an ordinary person could read.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["granted","sure","given","known"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Take something for granted</b> means to stop noticing its value. <i>Given</i>, <i>known</i> and <i>sure</i> all express certainty but none forms this idiom with <i>take for</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","in","on","at"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Something recorded on paper is described <b>in writing</b>. <i>By writing</i> would describe the means of doing some other action, and <i>on</i> or <i>at writing</i> are not used."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["achieved","accomplished","succeeded","managed"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Only <b>succeed</b> takes <i>in + -ing</i>. <i>Managed</i> needs a to-infinitive, while <i>achieved</i> and <i>accomplished</i> take a direct object."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","at","for","in"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>in exchange for</b>. <i>On</i>, <i>at</i> and <i>for exchange</i> do not form the idiom, although <i>for</i> is tempting because it appears later in the phrase."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["until","from","by","since"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Not until the fifteenth</b> century means it arrived only then. <i>Not by</i> would set a deadline that was missed, and <i>since</i> or <i>from</i> mark a starting point rather than a delayed arrival."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["contrasted","compared","opposed","measured"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Compared with</b> introduces the standard against which paper seemed flimsy. <i>Contrasted with</i> stresses difference rather than a scale, and <i>opposed</i> or <i>measured with</i> are not used for judging quality."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["end","rest","paid","stop"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Put paid to something</b> is an idiom meaning to finish it off completely. <i>Put a stop to</i> and <i>put an end to</i> exist but each needs an article, and <i>put to rest</i> has a different word order."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","in","out","up"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Keep up with demand</b> means supply as fast as it is needed. <i>Keep on with</i> means continue an activity, <i>keep in with</i> means stay friendly with someone, and <i>keep out</i> means exclude."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["drove","dropped","lowered","fell"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>drive prices down</b> means force them lower. <i>Fell</i> is intransitive and cannot take an object, <i>dropped</i> does not combine with <i>down</i>, and <i>lowered ... down</i> repeats the idea."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","on","at","in"],"correct":0,"explanation":"The set phrase is <b>for the first time</b>. <i>At first</i> exists without <i>the ... time</i>, and <i>in</i> or <i>on the first time</i> are not English idioms."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-20-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"When the Reef Turns White","topic":"coral bleaching","passage":"Not until the summer of 1998 (1)____ marine biologists fully grasp how quickly a reef can die. That year an unusually strong El Niño pushed sea temperatures a degree or two (2)____ their normal maximum across much of the tropics, and reefs from East Africa to the Maldives lost their colour within weeks. The colour, it turns out, was never the coral's own. It belongs to microscopic algae living inside each polyp, (3)____ supply their host with most of its food in exchange for shelter. Under heat stress the polyp expels them, and the bare white skeleton shows through the tissue. A bleached coral is not yet a dead one; provided (4)____ the water cools soon enough, the algae can return and the partnership resumes. What worries researchers is the frequency (5)____ which these events now occur. A reef that once had a decade to recover between heatwaves may now have three or four years, hardly enough for slow-growing species to rebuild. (6)____ it not for a handful of unusually tolerant colonies, some reefs would already have vanished altogether. Scientists have begun cross-breeding these survivors in the hope of producing corals able to cope (7)____ warmer seas, though nobody pretends this will work at the scale required. Reef tourism, on (8)____ many island economies depend, is watching nervously. (9)____ the scale of the damage, one might expect bleaching to dominate environmental news; in fact it receives far less attention than deforestation. Only when a reef has gone entirely (10)____ people tend to notice what they have lost.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"<b>Not until</b> at the start of a sentence forces inversion with an auxiliary: <i>Not until 1998 did biologists grasp</i>. <b>Have</b> or <b>could</b> would need a different verb form and would not match the past-tense narrative."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["above","over","beyond","past"],"answer":"above","explanation":"Temperatures rise <b>above</b> (or over, beyond, past) a maximum. <b>Than</b> is tempting after 'a degree or two' but needs a comparative such as <i>higher</i>, which is absent."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause after a comma takes <b>which</b> for the algae. <b>That</b> cannot follow a comma in a non-defining clause, and <b>who</b> is reserved for people."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<b>Provided that</b> introduces a condition: on condition that the water cools. <b>If</b> or <b>when</b> cannot follow <i>provided</i>, which already carries the conditional meaning."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["with","at"],"answer":"with","explanation":"Events occur <b>with</b> (or at) a certain frequency, so the relative clause is <i>the frequency with which they occur</i>. <b>In</b> and <b>by</b> do not collocate with <i>frequency</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["Were","were"],"answer":"Were","explanation":"<b>Were it not for</b> is the inverted form of <i>if it were not for</i>, an unreal condition whose result is <i>would already have vanished</i>. <b>Was</b> is not used in this inverted subjunctive, and <b>Had</b> would need <i>had it not been for</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"The phrasal verb is <b>cope with</b> a difficulty. <b>In</b> would describe where the corals live, not what they can withstand, and <i>cope</i> never takes <b>to</b>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"The preposition is fronted before the relative pronoun: <i>tourism, on <b>which</b> economies depend</i>. <b>That</b> can never follow a preposition, and <b>whom</b> is only for people."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["Given","given","Considering","considering"],"answer":"Given","explanation":"<b>Given</b> (or considering) the scale of the damage means 'taking it into account', which is what leads one to expect heavy coverage. <b>Despite</b> would reverse the logic: the expectation follows from the damage rather than in spite of it."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["do"],"answer":"do","explanation":"A fronted <b>Only when</b> clause triggers inversion in the main clause: <i>only when it has gone do people notice</i>. <b>Then</b> is a tempting filler but leaves the sentence without the required auxiliary before <i>people</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-20-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Penny Universities","topic":"the origin of coffee houses","passage":"Legend has it that coffee was discovered by an Ethiopian goatherd (1)____ animals grew unusually lively after chewing red berries from a certain bush. Whatever the truth of the tale, the drink had reached the ports of Yemen by the fifteenth century, where Sufi monks used it to stay awake (2)____ their night-time devotions. From there it travelled north to Cairo, Damascus and Istanbul, and it was in these cities that the coffee house (3)____ such was born. Unlike the tavern, it sold nothing that clouded the mind; unlike the mosque, it demanded no particular conduct. Men of every rank could sit for hours over a single cup, playing chess, listening to storytellers or, (4)____ the authorities feared most, discussing politics. Several sultans tried to (5)____ the coffee houses down, and each time they reopened within months. When the habit reached London in the 1650s, it (6)____ hold with astonishing speed: by 1700 the city is thought to have had several hundred such establishments, each one attracting its own clientele. Merchants gathered at one, poets at another, and it was out of a coffee house near the Thames (7)____ the insurance market Lloyd's eventually grew. A penny bought admission and a cup, which is (8)____ the coffee houses came to be called 'penny universities'. Their decline in the eighteenth century, historians agree, had (9)____ to do with the quality of the coffee, which had if anything improved, than with the rise of private clubs and the growing popularity of tea. (10)____, the idea they embodied — a public room where strangers could talk as equals — has never quite gone away.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"<b>Whose</b> links the goatherd to his animals as a possessive relative: <i>a goatherd whose animals grew lively</i>. <b>Who</b> would leave <i>animals</i> without a possessor and produce two subjects in a row."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["during","through","throughout","for"],"answer":"during","explanation":"The monks stayed awake <b>during</b> (or through, throughout, for) their devotions, a period of time. <b>While</b> is a conjunction and needs a clause with a verb, not a noun phrase."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"<b>As such</b> means 'in the proper sense of the word': the coffee house as such, as opposed to earlier informal gatherings. <b>Like</b> or <b>for</b> cannot combine with <i>such</i> here."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["what"],"answer":"what","explanation":"<b>What the authorities feared most</b> is a nominal relative clause standing for 'the thing they feared most'. <b>Which</b> would need a noun before it to refer back to, and there is none."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["close","shut"],"answer":"close","explanation":"The phrasal verb <b>close down</b> (or shut down) means to stop a business operating. <b>Put down</b> or <b>break down</b> do not apply to an establishment, and <b>ban</b> does not take <i>down</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["took"],"answer":"took","explanation":"A habit or idea <b>takes hold</b> when it becomes established. <b>Got</b> or <b>caught</b> hold would mean physically grasping something, which is not the sense here."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"This is a cleft sentence: <i>it was out of a coffee house <b>that</b> Lloyd's grew</i>. <b>Which</b> is wrong because the emphasised element is a prepositional phrase, not a noun that a relative clause could modify, and <b>where</b> would double the place meaning already given by <i>out of</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["why","how"],"answer":"why","explanation":"<b>Which is why</b> (or how) introduces the consequence of the penny price: it explains the nickname. <b>Because</b> cannot follow <i>which is</i>, and <b>that</b> would not express the reason."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["less"],"answer":"less","explanation":"<b>Less to do with X than with Y</b> ranks the two causes, and the aside 'which had if anything improved' rules out the coffee as the reason, so <b>less</b> is the only logical choice. <b>More</b> would make the improved coffee the main cause of the decline."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["Nevertheless","nevertheless","However","however","Nonetheless","nonetheless","Still","still","Yet","yet"],"answer":"Nevertheless","explanation":"The final sentence contrasts the decline of the coffee house with the survival of its idea, so a concessive linker such as <b>Nevertheless</b>, however or yet is needed. <b>Therefore</b> or <b>Moreover</b> would present the survival as a consequence or an addition, not a contrast."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-20-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Rebuilding the Past","topic":"how memory works","passage":"Most of us imagine memory (1)____ a kind of recording, stored somewhere in the brain and played back on demand. Neuroscientists have spent decades demonstrating that nothing could be (2)____ from the truth. A memory is not retrieved so much as rebuilt, assembled afresh each time from fragments scattered across different regions, and every rebuilding introduces small changes. This explains why two people (3)____ witnessed the same event can give sincere but contradictory accounts of it years later. It also explains why memories can be planted: tell someone often (4)____ that they once got lost in a shopping centre as a child, and a surprising number will eventually 'remember' the episode in vivid detail. Sleep plays a role that was underestimated (5)____ recently. During the deeper stages of the night, the hippocampus appears to replay the day's experiences to the cortex, gradually transferring (6)____ has been judged important into long-term storage. Deprive a student of sleep after a day of revision and (7)____ of what was learned will be lost by morning. Forgetting, meanwhile, is not simply a failure of the system (8)____ part of its design. A brain that kept everything would drown in irrelevant detail, unable to tell a lasting principle from a passing coincidence. Had our ancestors remembered every leaf on every tree, they might never (9)____ recognised the one that was poisonous. The art of memory, in (10)____ words, lies less in holding on than in knowing what to let go.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"<b>Imagine X as Y</b> means to picture X in the form of Y. <b>Like</b> is tempting but after <i>imagine</i> it would compare memory to a recording rather than define what people take it to be, and <b>to be</b> is two words."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["further","farther"],"answer":"further","explanation":"<b>Nothing could be further from the truth</b> is a fixed expression of strong denial, and British usage also allows <i>farther</i>. <b>Far</b> is not comparative, and <b>away</b> would need <i>further</i> in front of it."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["who","that"],"answer":"who","explanation":"A defining relative clause about people takes <b>who</b> (or that): <i>two people who witnessed the same event</i>. <b>Which</b> is not used for people, and <b>whom</b> is wrong because the pronoun is the subject of <i>witnessed</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["enough"],"answer":"enough","explanation":"<b>Often enough</b> means 'sufficiently often', with <i>enough</i> following the adverb it modifies. <b>Too</b> would have to come before <i>often</i> and would give the wrong meaning, and <b>very</b> cannot follow the adverb."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["until","till"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Until recently</b> marks the end point of the period during which the role was underestimated. <b>Since</b> would mean the opposite, that the underestimation began recently, and <b>before</b> is not used with <i>recently</i> in this way."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["what","whatever"],"answer":"what","explanation":"<b>What has been judged important</b> is a nominal clause meaning 'the things that have been judged important', and <i>whatever</i> is also possible. <b>Which</b> or <b>that</b> would need a noun before them to refer to."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["much","most","some","part","half"],"answer":"much","explanation":"A quantifier is needed before <b>of what was learned</b>: much, most, some, part or half. <b>Many</b> is wrong because <i>what was learned</i> is uncountable, and <b>all</b> overstates a claim the passage presents as a partial loss."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["but"],"answer":"but","explanation":"<b>Not simply X but Y</b> corrects one description with another: not a failure but part of the design. <b>And</b> would make forgetting both a failure and a design feature, and <b>rather</b> would need <i>but rather</i> here."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["have"],"answer":"have","explanation":"The inverted third conditional <i>Had our ancestors remembered</i> requires a perfect modal in the result: <i>might never <b>have</b> recognised</i>. <b>Had</b> after <i>might</i> is impossible, and <b>be</b> would make the sentence passive without an agent."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["other"],"answer":"other","explanation":"<b>In other words</b> signals that the writer is restating the argument more simply. <b>Another</b> is singular and cannot precede the plural <i>words</i>, and <b>short</b> would be <i>in short</i> without <i>words</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-20-4","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"A Light with No One to Keep It","topic":"the lighthouse keepers","passage":"For the best part of two centuries, the lights (1)____ guarded Britain's coast were tended by men who lived beside them. The job was passed from father to son more often than (2)____, and a keeper's children grew up knowing the rhythm of the lamp better than the rhythm of the school bell. On the rock stations — towers built straight out of the sea, miles from the nearest land — three men would share a space no larger than a modest flat for weeks (3)____ end. Boredom was the enemy quite (4)____ much as the weather. Keepers carved, knitted, built ships in bottles, and kept diaries in which the arrival of the relief boat is recorded with an enthusiasm normally reserved (5)____ great victories. No sooner had a storm blown itself out (6)____ the men would be scrubbing salt from the glass, since a dirty lens could dim the beam by a third. The relationship between keepers and the ships they protected was oddly one-sided: sailors depended on the light absolutely, yet few of them ever gave a thought to (7)____ was up there keeping it lit. Automation began in the 1960s and was complete by the end of the century, (8)____ which point the last keepers were quietly pensioned off. Some welcomed retirement; others, (9)____ to the solitude, found the noise of ordinary life almost unbearable. Today the towers still stand and the lamps still turn, but nobody watches them do it. (10)____ many of the old keepers, that is the saddest part: a light with no one to keep it.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"A defining relative clause identifies which lights are meant: <i>the lights <b>that</b> guarded the coast</i>, and <i>which</i> is equally correct without a comma. <b>Who</b> is impossible because lights are not people, and <b>what</b> cannot introduce a relative clause after a noun."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["not"],"answer":"not","explanation":"<b>More often than not</b> is a fixed phrase meaning 'usually'. <b>Ever</b> would give <i>more often than ever</i>, a comparison with the past that makes no sense for an inherited job, and <b>never</b> is not used in this idiom."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"<b>For weeks on end</b> means for many weeks without a break. <b>At</b> or <b>in</b> do not form this idiom, and <i>without end</i> would need a different structure."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The comparison of equality is <b>as much as</b>, here strengthened by <i>quite</i>: boredom was quite as much of an enemy as the weather. <b>So</b> is used only in negative comparisons (<i>not so much as</i>), and <b>too</b> would need <i>too much</i> without the following <i>as</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"Something is <b>reserved for</b> a purpose or occasion: enthusiasm reserved for great victories. <b>To</b> would suggest handing the enthusiasm over to the victories, and <b>on</b> does not collocate with <i>reserved</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"<b>No sooner had ... than</b> is the inverted pattern for two events in quick succession. <b>When</b> belongs with <i>hardly had ... when</i>, and <b>then</b> is a spelling trap that turns the sentence into nonsense."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["who","whoever"],"answer":"who","explanation":"After the preposition <i>to</i>, the clause <i>who was up there</i> is a nominal clause acting as its object, so <b>who</b> (or whoever) is the subject of <i>was</i>. <b>Whom</b> is tempting after a preposition but is wrong because the pronoun is a subject, not an object, and <b>which</b> is not used for people."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>At which point</b> means 'and at that moment', a relative phrase with a fronted preposition. <b>By</b> or <b>from</b> would imply a deadline or a starting point rather than the moment the pensioning happened."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["accustomed","used","attached"],"answer":"accustomed","explanation":"<b>Accustomed to</b> (or used to, attached to) the solitude explains why ordinary noise became unbearable. <b>Adapted</b> to the solitude is possible in isolation, but the contrast with those who welcomed retirement points to habit, and <b>keen</b> or <b>fond</b> take <i>on</i> and <i>of</i>, not <i>to</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["For","for","To","to"],"answer":"For","explanation":"<b>For</b> (or to) many of the old keepers introduces the people in whose view something is true. <b>With</b> would describe accompaniment, and <b>Among</b> would need a different verb, since <i>that is the saddest part among them</i> does not work."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-20-5","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Roads That Held an Empire","topic":"ancient road builders","passage":"Long before the Romans laid their first paved mile, other civilisations had learned that (1)____ a state could move its soldiers and grain, it could hold its territory. The Persians built a royal road stretching some 2,500 kilometres from Susa to the Aegean, along (2)____ relays of mounted messengers could carry a message in a week that would have taken a traveller on foot three months. The Inca, (3)____ had neither the wheel nor iron tools, nevertheless strung more than 30,000 kilometres of roads across the Andes. Their routes came complete with rope bridges swaying above gorges and storehouses spaced a day's walk (4)____. It is the Romans, however, (5)____ roads have left the deepest mark on the modern map. Their engineers dug a trench, packed it with layers of stone, gravel and sand, and finished the surface with a slight curve so that rain would run (6)____ rather than pool. So well were these roads made (7)____ several remain in use two thousand years later, and the routes of countless European motorways still follow them. What is often forgotten is the cost (8)____ which they were built. Much of the labour was carried out by soldiers between campaigns, and much of the rest by slaves and conquered peoples who had little choice (9)____ the matter. A road, in other words, was never simply a convenience; it was a statement that the land it crossed now belonged to (10)____ had built it.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["if","once","when","where","provided","providing"],"answer":"if","explanation":"The clause states a condition: <b>if</b> (or once, when, where, provided) a state could move its soldiers, it could hold its territory. <b>Unless</b> would reverse the logic, and <b>that</b> is already used before the blank and cannot be repeated."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"The preposition <i>along</i> is fronted before the relative pronoun for the road: <i>along <b>which</b> messengers could carry</i>. <b>That</b> can never follow a preposition, and <b>where</b> would clash with <i>along</i>, which already gives the place meaning."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["who"],"answer":"who","explanation":"A non-defining relative clause after a comma about a people takes <b>who</b>. <b>That</b> is impossible after a comma in this type of clause, and <b>which</b> is not used for people."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["apart"],"answer":"apart","explanation":"Things placed at regular intervals are <b>spaced</b> a certain distance <b>apart</b>. <b>Away</b> would need a reference point (<i>away from something</i>), and <b>between</b> cannot end the phrase without an object."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"In this cleft sentence the emphasised noun <i>the Romans</i> is linked to <i>roads</i> by the possessive relative <b>whose</b>. <b>Who</b> would leave <i>roads</i> as a second subject with no connection, and <b>that</b> would make the Romans, not their roads, leave the mark."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["off","away"],"answer":"off","explanation":"Water <b>runs off</b> (or away from) a curved surface instead of collecting on it, which is the contrast with <i>pool</i>. <b>Out</b> would mean the rain was used up, and <b>down</b> alone leaves unclear where it goes."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<b>So well were these roads made that</b> is the inverted form of <i>so ... that</i>, expressing result. <b>Which</b> cannot introduce the result clause, and <b>as</b> would turn the sentence into a comparison with no second term."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"Something is built <b>at</b> a cost, so the relative phrase is <i>the cost at which they were built</i>. <b>For</b> is tempting because we pay for things, but the idiom for a price or sacrifice is <i>at a cost</i>, not <i>for a cost</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>to have little choice in the matter</b>. <b>About</b> or <b>over</b> can follow <i>choice</i> in other contexts, but the idiom with <i>the matter</i> takes <i>in</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["whoever"],"answer":"whoever","explanation":"<b>Whoever had built it</b> is a nominal clause meaning 'the people who had built it', and <i>whoever</i> is the subject of <i>had built</i>. <b>Whomever</b> is a common trap after the preposition <i>to</i>, but the case is decided inside the clause, where the pronoun is a subject, and <b>who</b> alone cannot follow a preposition in this sense."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-20-6","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"The Dance in the Dark Hive","topic":"the language of bees","passage":"In the early twentieth century an Austrian zoologist named Karl von Frisch (1)____ out to discover how a honeybee returning from a rich patch of flowers manages to tell her sisters where it is. He painted tiny dots on the backs of individual bees and watched them for hours through the glass wall of an observation hive. (2)____ he saw was a dance. A forager that had found food close to the hive ran in a tight circle, turning first one way and then the other; one that had flown further performed a figure of eight, waggling her abdomen (3)____ time she crossed the middle line. The length of the waggle, von Frisch worked out, corresponds to the distance of the food, and the angle of the run relative to the vertical comb tells the others the direction (4)____ the sun. The system is (5)____ precise that bees can be sent to a dish of sugar water several hundred metres away that none of them has ever seen. Not everyone was convinced. Critics argued that the bees were simply following a scent picked (6)____ from the dancer's body. It took decades of increasingly ingenious experiments, including a robot bee that danced without any scent at all, to (7)____ the matter beyond reasonable doubt. Von Frisch was eventually awarded a Nobel Prize (8)____ the work, sharing it in 1973 with two other pioneers of animal behaviour. (9)____ the dance can properly be called a language remains a matter of definition, since it has no grammar and refers only to the here and now. Even so, it is the only known case (10)____ a creature other than a human passes on abstract information about something that is out of sight.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["set","took","put","made"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Set out to do</b> something means to begin with a definite aim. <i>Put out</i> means to extinguish or publish, <i>made out</i> means to manage to see, and <i>took out</i> means to remove."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["Which","What","That","Whatever"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>What he saw</b> is a nominal clause meaning 'the thing he saw' and acts as the subject of <i>was</i>. <i>Which</i> and <i>that</i> need a noun to refer back to, and <i>whatever</i> would wrongly suggest that anything at all he saw counted as a dance."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["whole","same","each","all"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Each time she crossed</b> means on every crossing. <i>All the time</i> would need <i>the</i> and means continuously, <i>whole</i> needs <i>the whole time</i>, and <i>same</i> would need <i>the same time as</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["to","at","for","of"],"correct":3,"explanation":"We speak of <b>the direction of</b> a landmark: the direction of the sun. <i>To</i> is used after verbs of movement (<i>fly to</i>), <i>at</i> after verbs of pointing, and <i>for</i> would mean 'on behalf of'."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","very","such","too"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>So precise that</b> is the result pattern with an adjective. <i>Such</i> needs a noun (<i>such a precise system</i>), <i>too</i> would need <i>to</i> and would give the wrong meaning, and <i>very</i> cannot be followed by a <i>that</i> clause."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["out","up","off","over"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A scent is <b>picked up</b> from a surface, meaning detected or acquired. <i>Picked out</i> means chosen, <i>picked off</i> means removed one by one, and <i>picked over</i> means sorted through."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["lay","bring","put","set"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The collocation is <b>put a matter beyond doubt</b>. <i>Set</i> and <i>lay</i> are tempting because they also mean 'place', but neither forms this idiom, and <i>bring</i> would be <i>bring into doubt</i>, the opposite meaning."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","on","about","for"],"correct":3,"explanation":"A prize is awarded <b>for</b> an achievement. <i>Of</i> would express possession, while <i>on</i> and <i>about</i> would describe the subject of a talk or book, not the reason for an award."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["Whether","Although","Unless","If"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A clause acting as the subject of <i>remains</i> must begin with <b>whether</b>. <i>If</i> can replace <i>whether</i> only after a verb, never as the subject of a sentence, and <i>although</i> and <i>unless</i> would leave the main verb without a subject."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["where","what","which","that"],"correct":0,"explanation":"After <i>case</i> a relative clause describing a situation takes <b>where</b>, equivalent to <i>in which</i>. <i>Which</i> and <i>that</i> would make the creature's action into a description of the case itself with no preposition, and <i>what</i> cannot follow a noun."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-21-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"From Counter to Trolley","topic":"the rise of the supermarket","passage":"For most of the nineteenth century, buying food was a slow conversation. A shopper stood at a counter, asked for flour or sugar by the pound, and waited while the grocer weighed and wrapped it. Not (1)____ the early twentieth century did the idea of letting customers help themselves take hold, and even then many shopkeepers dismissed it as an invitation to theft. What changed their minds was arithmetic rather (2)____ trust. A self-service store needed fewer clerks, so its prices could undercut those of the corner shop by a margin (3)____ no loyal customer could ignore for long. Once refrigerated cabinets and the private car became commonplace, the supermarket was able to move to the edge of town, (4)____ land was cheap and parking was plentiful. Had the motor car remained a luxury, the format (5)____ never have spread beyond the wealthiest suburbs. The trolley, patented in the 1930s, was itself a quiet revolution: (6)____ than carrying a basket that grew heavier with every aisle, shoppers could now buy as much as they were willing to push. Critics have long pointed (7)____ that the layout of a modern store is a piece of applied psychology. Milk sits at the back so that customers must walk (8)____ everything else to reach it. Sweets, meanwhile, wait beside the till, (9)____ patience is thinnest and children are loudest. Whether we admire the efficiency or resent the manipulation, few of us can honestly claim to be immune (10)____ it.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["until","till"],"answer":"until","explanation":"<b>Not until</b> + time, followed by inversion (<i>did the idea ... take hold</i>), means the thing happened only at that point. <i>Before</i> or <i>in</i> would not license the inverted <i>did</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"<b>Rather than</b> contrasts the real reason (arithmetic) with the rejected one (trust). <i>Rather from</i> or <i>rather to</i> does not exist."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"The relative pronoun is the object of <i>ignore</i>: a margin <b>that/which</b> no customer could ignore. <i>What</i> cannot follow a noun it would modify, and <i>where</i> needs a place."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["where"],"answer":"where","explanation":"<b>Where</b> introduces a relative clause about a place (<i>the edge of town</i>). <i>Which</i> would need a preposition (<i>in which</i>), and <i>when</i> refers to time."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["would","might","could"],"answer":"would","explanation":"<i>Had the motor car remained</i> is an inverted third conditional, so the result clause needs a modal + <i>have</i>: <b>would/might/could</b> never have spread. <i>Will</i> or <i>had</i> breaks the pattern."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["rather"],"answer":"rather","explanation":"<b>Rather than</b> + -ing (<i>rather than carrying</i>) sets one action against another. <i>Other than</i> means 'apart from', and <i>more than</i> would make no sense with <i>carrying</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>Point out that</b> means draw attention to a fact. <i>Point to</i> takes a noun, not a <i>that</i>-clause, and <i>point at</i> is a physical gesture."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["past","through","by"],"answer":"past","explanation":"Customers walk <b>past</b> (or <b>through</b>, or <b>by</b>) the other goods to reach the milk; all three mean passing the shelves on the way. <i>Over</i> or <i>across</i> does not fit a row of shelves, and <i>round</i> would suggest avoiding them."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["where","when"],"answer":"where","explanation":"The clause describes the till as the place (<b>where</b>) or the moment (<b>when</b>) patience runs out; both are defensible. <i>Which</i> would need a preposition, and <i>that</i> cannot introduce this kind of adverbial relative clause."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["to","from"],"answer":"to","explanation":"You are <b>immune to</b> an influence (the usual choice); <b>immune from</b> is also accepted, chiefly in the sense of being exempt. <i>Immune of</i> or <i>immune against</i> is not standard."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-21-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"A Very Slow Argument","topic":"how mountains form","passage":"Mountains look like the most permanent things on Earth, yet geologically they are among the youngest. The Himalayas, (1)____ instance, began to rise some fifty million years ago, when the landmass that is now India collided with Asia. Neither plate was willing to sink beneath the other, (2)____ the crust between them had nowhere to go but up. The range is still growing by roughly a centimetre a year, though erosion strips away almost as (3)____ material as the collision adds. Not all mountains are born (4)____ this way. Where one plate slides under another, molten rock forces its way to the surface and builds volcanic cones, some of (5)____ reach their full height within a few thousand years. Elsewhere the crust is pulled (6)____ rather than squeezed together, and huge blocks tilt and drop, leaving their neighbours standing as ranges. (7)____ less striking is the fact that the summit of Everest is made of marine limestone, complete with fossils of creatures that once lived on a sea floor. Only by measuring the rock's age and composition (8)____ geologists able to work out how far it had travelled. The lesson is a humbling one. However solid the ground beneath our feet may appear, it is (9)____ the move, and what we call a mountain is simply a moment in a very slow argument (10)____ two pieces of the planet's crust.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"<b>For instance</b> introduces an example. <i>For example</i> is the same idiom, but <i>as instance</i> or <i>in instance</i> is not English."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["so","and"],"answer":"so","explanation":"<b>So</b> links cause (neither plate would sink) to result (the crust went up); plain <b>and</b> is also grammatical, though it states the consequence less clearly. <i>Because</i> would reverse the logic, and <i>but</i> would set up a contrast that is not there."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["much"],"answer":"much","explanation":"<i>Material</i> is uncountable, so the comparison is <b>as much ... as</b>. <i>Many</i> needs a plural, and <i>fast</i> would require the noun to be dropped."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"Something happens <b>in</b> this way or in that way. <i>By this way</i> is a common learner error; English uses <i>by</i> only with <i>by means of</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"<b>Some of which</b> refers back to the cones inside a non-defining relative clause. <i>Them</i> would need a new sentence, and <i>that</i> cannot follow a preposition."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["apart"],"answer":"apart","explanation":"The crust is pulled <b>apart</b>, the opposite of <i>squeezed together</i>. <i>Away</i> or <i>out</i> does not express separation into two pieces."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["no"],"answer":"no","explanation":"<b>No less striking</b> means 'just as striking', and the fronted phrase forces the inversion <i>is the fact</i>. <i>Not less striking</i> is not idiomatic in this position, and <i>more</i> would need <i>than</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["were"],"answer":"were","explanation":"<b>Only by ...</b> at the start of a sentence triggers inversion, so the auxiliary comes before the subject: <i>were geologists able</i>. <i>Are</i> clashes with the past tense of the passage, and <i>had</i> cannot go with <i>able</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"<b>On the move</b> means constantly moving. <i>In the move</i> or <i>at the move</i> is not a fixed phrase."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["between"],"answer":"between","explanation":"An argument takes place <b>between</b> two parties. <i>Among</i> requires three or more, and <i>of</i> would make the crust the owner of the argument rather than a party to it."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-21-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"When Copying Became Cheap","topic":"the printing revolution","passage":"Before the middle of the fifteenth century, every book in Europe was written (1)____ hand, and a single copy of a long work could occupy a scribe for the better part of a year. Books were therefore rare, expensive and, for most people, entirely (2)____ of reach. The press that changed this did not appear from nowhere. Presses for squeezing grapes had existed for centuries, and so (3)____ ink, paper and metal casting; what was new was the idea of combining them with movable type. No sooner (4)____ the first printed Bibles gone on sale than imitators set up workshops in every trading city. Within fifty years, more books had been produced than in the whole of the previous thousand. The consequences were not at all (5)____ the inventor had intended. He had hoped to make a comfortable living; instead he died in debt, and it fell (6)____ others to profit from his method. Ideas that would once have circulated among a handful of scholars could now be read, argued (7)____ and rejected by thousands, and rulers found it far harder to control what their subjects knew. It is tempting to draw a parallel with the internet, and the comparison is (8)____ without merit. In both cases the cost of copying collapsed almost overnight, and in both cases those in authority were the last to grasp what (9)____ happening. Whether the printing press did more good (10)____ harm is a question historians still argue about; that it did a great deal of both is beyond dispute.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["by"],"answer":"by","explanation":"Something is written or made <b>by hand</b>. <i>With hand</i> would need an article (<i>with the hand</i>) and is not the idiom; <i>in hand</i> means 'under control'."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>Out of reach</b> means impossible to obtain. <i>Beyond reach</i> exists but takes no <i>of</i>, so <i>beyond</i> cannot fill this blank."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"<b>And so had ink, paper and metal casting</b> repeats the auxiliary of <i>had existed</i> with inversion. <i>Did</i> would suit a simple past, but the verb here is past perfect."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"<b>No sooner had ... than</b> is the inverted past perfect: <i>No sooner had the Bibles gone on sale than</i>. <i>Were</i> cannot combine with <i>gone on sale</i> in this structure, and <i>did</i> would need a bare infinitive."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["what"],"answer":"what","explanation":"<b>What the inventor had intended</b> is a nominal relative clause meaning 'the thing he intended'. <i>That</i> and <i>which</i> would need a noun in front of them, and <i>as</i> would require <i>such as</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"<b>It falls to somebody to do something</b> means the task or opportunity passes to them. <i>Fell on</i> suggests a burden landing on someone, and <i>fell for</i> means to be deceived."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["over","about"],"answer":"over","explanation":"Ideas are <b>argued over</b> or <b>argued about</b>, both meaning debated. <i>Argued with</i> takes a person, and <i>argued for</i> would mean supported, which clashes with <i>rejected</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["not"],"answer":"not","explanation":"<b>Not without merit</b> is a deliberate double negative meaning 'has some merit'. <i>Never</i> would be too absolute for a hedged claim, and <i>hardly without</i> is not idiomatic."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["was"],"answer":"was","explanation":"<i>Grasp what was happening</i> uses a past continuous inside indirect speech, matching <i>were the last</i>. <i>Is</i> ignores the tense of the reporting verb, and <i>had</i> would need a past participle."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"<b>More good than harm</b> completes the comparative <i>more</i>. <i>Or</i> would turn the phrase into a plain choice and leave <i>more</i> unfinished."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-21-4","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Contagious Reflex","topic":"why we yawn","passage":"Everybody yawns, and almost nobody knows why. The oldest explanation, that a yawn draws extra oxygen into the blood, sounds sensible but does not stand (1)____ to testing: volunteers who breathe pure oxygen yawn just as often as those who do not. A more recent theory holds that yawning cools the brain, which works best (2)____ a narrow range of temperature. Supporters point out that people yawn more in warm rooms and hardly (3)____ all when a cold pack is pressed against their forehead. Yet even this does not explain the strangest feature of yawning: (4)____ easily it spreads. Read the word, watch a stranger yawn, or merely think about yawning, and the (5)____ are that you will yawn yourself within a minute or two. Dogs catch yawns from their owners, and chimpanzees from one another, (6)____ suggests that the reflex is tied to empathy in some way. Children under four rarely catch a yawn, and (7)____ do adults who score low on tests of social sensitivity. Had yawning no social function at all, it (8)____ be hard to see why it should be so contagious. Some researchers now suspect that a shared yawn once helped a group keep its members alert at the same time, so that nobody was (9)____ their guard when danger came. It is, admittedly, a difficult idea to prove. What is certain is that the more you try (10)____ to yawn, the more likely you are to do exactly that.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"<b>Stand up to testing</b> means survive it. <i>Stand out</i> means be noticeable, and <i>stand in</i> means substitute."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["within","in","inside"],"answer":"within","explanation":"The brain works best <b>within</b> (or <b>in/inside</b>) a narrow range. <i>Between</i> would need two named limits, and <i>at</i> suits a single point, not a range."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>Hardly at all</b> means almost never. No other preposition combines with <i>all</i> to make this intensifier; <i>hardly in all</i> is meaningless."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["how"],"answer":"how","explanation":"After the colon the writer names the feature: <b>how easily</b> it spreads, an exclamation-style clause. <i>Which</i> or <i>that</i> would need a verb and subject before <i>easily</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["chances","odds"],"answer":"chances","explanation":"<b>The chances are that</b> and <b>the odds are that</b> both mean 'it is probable that'. <i>Likelihood</i> is singular and would need <i>is</i>, so it cannot go with <i>are</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"<b>Which</b> refers back to the whole preceding statement (the fact that animals catch yawns). <i>That</i> cannot introduce a non-defining clause, and <i>it</i> would need a new sentence."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["nor","neither"],"answer":"nor","explanation":"After a negative (<i>rarely catch</i>), <b>and nor do / and neither do</b> adds another subject with inversion. <i>So do</i> would wrongly make the adults agree with a positive statement, and <i>also</i> cannot invert."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["would","might","could"],"answer":"would","explanation":"<i>Had yawning no social function</i> is an inverted conditional, so the result needs a past-form modal: it <b>would</b> (or <b>might/could</b>) be hard to see. <i>Will</i> breaks the hypothetical sense, and <i>had</i> cannot precede <i>be</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["off"],"answer":"off","explanation":"To be caught <b>off</b> one's guard means unprepared. <i>On</i> one's guard means the opposite, and would contradict <i>when danger came</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["not"],"answer":"not","explanation":"The blank negates the infinitive: <i>try <b>not</b> to yawn</i>. <i>Never</i> would sit before <i>try</i>, and <i>hard</i> would make the sentence say the opposite of what the ending needs."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-21-5","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Worth Its Weight in Gold","topic":"the silk trade","passage":"For centuries the secret of silk was guarded (1)____ jealously as any state secret has ever been. Chinese law made it a capital offence to carry silkworm eggs beyond the frontier, and foreign merchants were kept (2)____ the dark about how the thread was produced; some believed it was combed from the leaves of trees. What they did know was that the cloth fetched extraordinary prices at the far end of a route that ran (3)____ thousands of miles of desert and mountain. Few traders travelled the whole way. Instead the bales changed hands at oasis towns, each middleman adding his margin, so that (4)____ the time a length of silk reached Rome it was worth its weight in gold. The Romans, for their (5)____, worried that the trade was draining the empire of silver, and moralists complained that the fabric was too revealing to be decent. (6)____ was the demand, however, that neither cost nor disapproval made any difference. The monopoly finally broke in the sixth century, when, (7)____ to a well-known story, two monks smuggled eggs to Constantinople hidden inside hollow walking sticks. Whether or not the tale is true, silk was soon being woven in the eastern Mediterranean, and prices, (8)____ had once rivalled gold, fell steadily. The overland route declined too, not because the goods lost their appeal but because ships proved cheaper and safer. It is worth remembering that what we now call the Silk Road was never a single road (9)____ a shifting web of tracks, and that silk was only one of many things, among them paper, glass, religions and diseases, that passed (10)____ it.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The comparison is <b>as jealously as</b> any secret has been guarded. <i>So jealously as</i> is possible only after a negative, and <i>more</i> would need <i>than</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"To be kept <b>in the dark</b> means to be left uninformed. <i>At the dark</i> or <i>on the dark</i> is not an idiom."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["across","through","over"],"answer":"across","explanation":"A route runs <b>across</b>, <b>through</b> or <b>over</b> thousands of miles of terrain. <i>Along</i> would need a line such as a river or coast, and <i>for</i> does not fit with <i>of desert and mountain</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["by"],"answer":"by","explanation":"<b>By the time</b> means 'at the point when'. <i>At the time</i> refers to a specific moment already known, and <i>in the time</i> would need a duration."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["part"],"answer":"part","explanation":"<b>For their part</b> means 'as far as they were concerned'. <i>For their side</i> or <i>for their turn</i> is not idiomatic; <i>in turn</i> exists but takes <i>in</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["such"],"answer":"such","explanation":"<b>Such was the demand that</b> is a fronted, inverted result clause meaning the demand was so great that. <i>So</i> would need an adjective (<i>so great was</i>), and <i>great</i> alone cannot start the sentence without <i>so</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["according"],"answer":"according","explanation":"<b>According to a story</b> attributes the claim to its source. <i>Referring to</i> would make the monks do the referring, and <i>due to</i> gives a cause, not a source."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"The non-defining clause <i>which had once rivalled gold</i> adds information about <i>prices</i>. <i>That</i> cannot follow a comma in a non-defining clause, and <i>what</i> would need no antecedent."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["but"],"answer":"but","explanation":"<b>Never a single road but a shifting web</b>: after a negative, <i>but</i> means 'rather'. <i>And</i> would list two things that are supposedly both true, and <i>or</i> offers a choice the writer is not making."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["along","down","through"],"answer":"along","explanation":"Goods, ideas and diseases passed <b>along</b>, <b>down</b> or <b>through</b> the route. <i>Across</i> would mean crossing it from side to side, and <i>by</i> would mean going past it."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-21-6","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"Neighbours After Dark","topic":"urban foxes","passage":"Anyone who walks through a British city after dark is likely, sooner or later, to (1)____ across a fox. The animals began moving into towns in the 1930s, when new suburbs with large gardens spread into the countryside they had always (2)____. Far (3)____ being driven out, the foxes simply stayed put and found that their new neighbours were generous. A city fox rarely needs to hunt: bins, bird tables and food left out for cats provide most of what it eats, and the (4)____ is made up of worms, beetles and fallen fruit. Their numbers have risen steadily ever (5)____, and some cities now support several dozen adults per square kilometre. Opinion about them is sharply (6)____. Some residents leave out scraps and give the visitors names; others complain of torn rubbish bags, dug-up lawns and the unearthly screaming heard on winter nights during the mating season. Attempts to reduce the population (7)____ trapping have repeatedly failed, because a vacant territory is claimed by a newcomer within weeks. Wildlife officers now (8)____ that the sensible response is to make homes less inviting rather than to remove the animals. Foxes, they point out, pose (9)____ threat to people, and cases of aggression are almost unknown. Whether we regard them (10)____ pests or as a welcome touch of wildness in the city, they are clearly here to stay.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["fall","walk","bump","come"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Come across</b> means meet by chance. <i>Bump into</i> has the same meaning but takes <i>into</i>, not <i>across</i>; <i>fall</i> and <i>walk</i> do not form this phrasal verb."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["lived","dwelt","inhabited","stayed"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Only <b>inhabited</b> is transitive, so it can take <i>the countryside</i> as a direct object. <i>Lived</i>, <i>dwelt</i> and <i>stayed</i> would all need <i>in</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["away","from","beyond","off"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Far from being driven out</b> means 'not at all driven out; on the contrary'. <i>Far off</i>, <i>far away</i> and <i>far beyond</i> describe distance and cannot introduce this contrast."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["rest","others","remaining","left"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>The rest</b> is the fixed noun for the remaining portion. <i>The others</i> would need a plural verb, and <i>remaining</i> and <i>left</i> are not nouns."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["then","before","after","since"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Ever since</b> means from that time to the present, which suits the present perfect <i>have risen</i>. <i>Ever after</i> belongs to fairy tales, and <i>then</i> or <i>before</i> does not pair with <i>ever</i> here."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["separated","parted","divided","broken"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Opinion is <b>sharply divided</b> when people disagree strongly. <i>Separated</i>, <i>parted</i> and <i>broken</i> apply to physical things or relationships, not to public opinion."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","by","with","from"],"correct":1,"explanation":"A goal is achieved <b>by</b> + method (<i>by trapping</i>). <i>With</i> takes a tool, not an activity; <i>from</i> and <i>of</i> do not express means."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["insist","consist","resist","persist"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Insist that</b> introduces a firmly held view. <i>Persist</i> takes <i>in</i>, <i>consist</i> takes <i>of</i>, and <i>resist</i> takes a noun; none of them is followed by a <i>that</i>-clause."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["few","small","less","little"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<i>Threat</i> here is uncountable, so <b>little threat</b> means almost none. <i>Few</i> needs a plural, <i>small</i> would need an article, and <i>less</i> requires a comparison."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","as","to","like"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The pattern is <b>regard something as</b>, echoed later by <i>or as a welcome touch</i>. <i>Like</i> compares, <i>for</i> suits <i>mistake ... for</i>, and <i>to</i> does not follow <i>regard</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-22-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Behind the Bars","topic":"the ethics of zoos","passage":"Whether a zoo can ever be justified is a question that has divided animal lovers for well over a century. Those who defend zoos point (1)____ the species that would by now have vanished altogether had captive breeding programmes not intervened. Critics, (2)____, argue that no enclosure, however spacious, can make (3)____ for the loss of a territory that may once have stretched across hundreds of kilometres. It is the large mammals, rather than the reptiles or the birds, (4)____ appear to suffer most: elephants pacing the same strip of concrete, or a solitary polar bear swimming in endless circles, are sights (5)____ few visitors forget. Not until the 1970s (6)____ the major zoos begin to redesign their enclosures around the needs of the animals rather than the convenience of the public. Since then, a great (7)____ of money has been spent on naturalistic habitats, and the best institutions now devote a large share of their budgets to conservation in the wild. Even so, the accusation that zoos exist chiefly (8)____ entertainment has never entirely gone away. Were every zoo to close tomorrow, the animals already living in them could hardly be released, having never learnt to hunt or to fear predators. The more honest defenders concede that the real question is not (9)____ zoos are ideal but whether they do more good than harm. On (10)____, they insist, the answer is still yes.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"To <b>point to</b> something is to cite it as evidence for your case. <b>At</b> would mean physically indicating with a finger, and <b>out</b> would need an object placed after it (<i>point out the species</i>)."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["however","meanwhile","though","nevertheless","nonetheless","conversely"],"answer":"however","explanation":"The sentence contrasts the critics with the defenders in the sentence before, so a contrastive adverb set off by commas is needed: <b>however</b>. <b>But</b> is a conjunction and cannot sit between commas after the subject."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"<b>Make up for</b> a loss means to compensate for it. <b>Make for</b> means to head towards or to contribute to, and <b>make out</b> means to discern, so neither fits with <i>for the loss</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["that","which","who"],"answer":"that","explanation":"This is a cleft sentence, <i>It is the large mammals ... that appear to suffer most</i>, and <b>that</b> is the pronoun that completes it. <b>What</b> cannot introduce the second half of a cleft."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<i>Sights</i> is the object of <i>forget</i> in the relative clause, so a relative pronoun for things is needed: <b>that</b> or <b>which</b>. <b>What</b> would make the clause a noun clause and leave <i>sights</i> without a link."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"A sentence opening with <b>Not until</b> inverts subject and auxiliary, and the bare verb <i>begin</i> plus the 1970s time frame call for the past auxiliary <b>did</b>. <b>Had</b> would require <i>begun</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["deal"],"answer":"deal","explanation":"The quantifier for an uncountable noun like <i>money</i> is <b>a great deal of</b>. <b>Number</b> pairs only with plural countable nouns, and <b>amount</b> takes <i>large</i> rather than <i>great</i> in idiomatic use."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"Something exists <b>for</b> a purpose: <i>exist chiefly for entertainment</i>. <b>As</b> would identify the zoo with entertainment itself, and <b>of</b> does not follow <i>exist</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["whether"],"answer":"whether","explanation":"The structure <i>not ... but whether</i> demands a parallel clause, and only <b>whether</b> can introduce an alternative question after <i>the question is</i>. <b>If</b> is not used to complete <i>the question is</i> and breaks the parallel with the second <i>whether</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["balance"],"answer":"balance","explanation":"<b>On balance</b> means after weighing the arguments on both sides, which is exactly what the sentence describes. <b>On the whole</b> would carry a similar meaning but needs two words, and <i>on average</i> concerns numbers, not arguments."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-22-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Making the Picture Stay","topic":"the first photographs","passage":"Long before anyone managed to fix an image permanently, people knew that light could draw. A darkened room with a pinhole in one wall throws an upside-down picture of the street onto the opposite wall, and artists had used the trick for centuries (1)____ an aid to sketching. What nobody could do was make the picture stay. The breakthrough, when it finally came in the 1820s, owed as (2)____ to chemistry as to optics: a French inventor discovered that a certain kind of bitumen hardened (3)____ exposed to light, so that the unexposed parts could later be washed away. His first successful view, taken from an upstairs window, required an exposure of several hours, (4)____ is why the sun appears to shine on both sides of the courtyard at once. Within a decade the process had been refined to the point (5)____ a portrait could be taken in a few minutes, though sitters still had to be clamped into position (6)____ they blur. Hardly (7)____ the technique been announced in Paris when studios sprang up in every capital in Europe. Painters were divided: some declared that their art was dead, (8)____ others seized on the camera as a tool. What strikes a modern viewer most about those earliest pictures is not their technical crudeness (9)____ their stillness, for the streets appear emptied of everything that moved. It was not until faster plates arrived that photography (10)____ capture the crowd rather than the buildings it walked past.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"To use something <b>as</b> an aid means to use it in that role. <b>For</b> would need a purpose (<i>for sketching</i>), and <b>like</b> would only compare the trick to an aid instead of saying it was one."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["much"],"answer":"much","explanation":"The equal comparison <b>as much to X as to Y</b> means X and Y were equally responsible. <b>Many</b> cannot stand alone without a plural noun, and <i>as well</i> would not be followed by a second <i>as to</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["when","once","if","whenever","while"],"answer":"when","explanation":"A reduced clause <i>when (it was) exposed to light</i> drops the subject and <i>be</i>, leaving the conjunction before the participle. <b>After</b> and <b>by</b> would need a full clause or a gerund (<i>after being exposed</i>)."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"Only <b>which</b> can refer back to a whole preceding clause (the long exposure) and act as subject of <i>is why</i>. <b>That</b> cannot open a non-defining clause after a comma, and <b>this</b> would need a full stop before it."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["where","that"],"answer":"where","explanation":"The collocation is <b>to the point where</b> (or <i>that</i>) something becomes possible. <b>Which</b> would make <i>point</i> the subject or object of the next clause, which it is not."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["lest"],"answer":"lest","explanation":"<b>Lest</b> means 'for fear that' and is followed by a bare subjunctive, which explains <i>they blur</i> rather than <i>they blurred</i>. <b>Unless</b> reverses the logic, and <i>in case</i> would be two words."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"<b>Hardly had</b> + subject + past participle ... <i>when</i> is the inverted past perfect pattern for one event following immediately after another. <b>Was</b> cannot pair with <i>been announced</i>, and <b>did</b> would need a bare verb."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["while","whereas","whilst","but","and"],"answer":"while","explanation":"The two groups of painters are set against each other, so a contrastive linker such as <b>while</b> or <b>whereas</b> joins the clauses. <b>However</b> is an adverb and would need a semicolon or a full stop before it."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["but"],"answer":"but","explanation":"The pattern <b>not X but Y</b> corrects one idea with another. <b>Rather</b> would need <i>but rather</i> or a preceding comma, and <b>than</b> only works after a comparative such as <i>less</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["could","would"],"answer":"could","explanation":"The cleft <i>It was not until ... that</i> puts the main verb in the past, and ability in the past is expressed by <b>could</b>. <b>Can</b> clashes with the past frame, and <b>began</b> would require <i>to capture</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-22-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Where the Grass Gives Way","topic":"how deserts spread","passage":"Deserts do not advance across the land like an army; they seep. The process (1)____ which fertile ground turns to dust, known as desertification, rarely begins with a shortage of rain. Far more often it begins with too many animals grazing on too little grass, so that the roots which once held the soil (2)____ place are eaten away before they can recover. Once the surface is bare, wind and the occasional cloudburst finish the job, stripping (3)____ the thin layer of topsoil that took centuries to form. What makes the damage so hard to reverse is (4)____ each stage feeds the next: less vegetation means less moisture returned to the air, which in (5)____ means less rain, which means still less vegetation. Farmers on the margins are seldom (6)____ blame; driven by debt or drought, they have little choice but to push their herds onto land that cannot support them. Governments, (7)____ their part, have tended to answer with grand schemes, such as walls of trees planted across entire continents, whose survival rates have often been dismal. Had the same money been spent on fencing, wells and simple crop rotation, the results (8)____ well have been better. There is, (9)____, reason for cautious optimism. Satellite images show that where villagers have been given secure rights to their land, the green has crept back within a generation. The desert, it turns (10)____, can retreat as quietly as it came.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["by","through","in"],"answer":"by","explanation":"A process is the means <b>by which</b> something happens, so the preposition of means goes before the relative pronoun. <b>Of which</b> would express possession, which makes no sense with <i>process</i> here."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"To hold something <b>in place</b> is to stop it moving. <b>On place</b> is not English, and <i>into place</i> describes movement towards a position rather than keeping it there."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["off","away"],"answer":"off","explanation":"To <b>strip off</b> (or <i>strip away</i>) a layer is to remove it from a surface. <b>Strip out</b> is used for removing contents from inside something, and <b>strip down</b> means to dismantle."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"After a pseudo-cleft (<i>What makes ... is</i>), a full clause must be introduced by <b>that</b>. <b>Because</b> is possible in speech but not in formal writing, and <b>how</b> would change the meaning to manner."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["turn"],"answer":"turn","explanation":"<b>In turn</b> signals one consequence leading to the next in a chain. <b>In return</b> means as a reward or exchange, and <i>in time</i> would mean 'eventually', which loses the sense of sequence."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"Someone is <b>to blame</b> for a problem; the infinitive here carries a passive meaning. <b>At</b> and <b>for</b> do not follow <i>are seldom</i> with <i>blame</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["for","on"],"answer":"for","explanation":"<b>For their part</b> means 'as far as they are concerned' and introduces what one party did in contrast to another. <b>In their part</b> is not idiomatic, and <b>by</b> would suggest agency rather than viewpoint."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["might","could","may"],"answer":"might","explanation":"The inverted conditional <i>Had the same money been spent</i> needs a past modal, and <b>might well have</b> expresses a likely but uncertain outcome. <b>Would</b> does not collocate with <i>well</i> in this sense, and <b>should</b> would sound like an obligation."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["nevertheless","however","nonetheless","though","still"],"answer":"nevertheless","explanation":"The gloomy paragraph is followed by good news, so a concessive adverb between commas is required: <b>nevertheless</b>. <b>Despite</b> is a preposition and needs a noun after it, and <b>although</b> introduces a clause."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>It turns out</b> means 'it proves to be the case', an aside inserted between commas. <b>Turns up</b> means to appear unexpectedly, and <b>turns over</b> means to flip, so neither carries the meaning of discovery."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-22-4","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Money on the Table","topic":"the economics of tipping","passage":"Economists have long been puzzled by tipping. A customer who leaves money on the table after a meal in a city she will never visit again stands to gain nothing (1)____ doing so, and yet millions of people do exactly that every day. The obvious explanation, that we tip in order to reward good service, does not hold (2)____ under examination, since studies have repeatedly shown only a weak link between the quality of service and the size of the tip. What seems to matter far more is habit, together (3)____ a vague sense of what other people would think of us if we walked out without paying it. From the restaurant's point of view, the custom is convenient (4)____ that it shifts part of the wage bill from the owner to the customer. In some countries, indeed, staff are paid a base rate so low that (5)____ it not for tips they could not live on it at all. Critics argue that this arrangement leaves workers at the mercy (6)____ whoever happens to be sitting at their tables, and there is evidence that tips reflect the customer's mood, and even the server's appearance, quite as much as anything the server actually does. Nor (7)____ the system easy to abolish. Several restaurants that tried replacing tips with higher prices found their customers drifting away, apparently because a menu that looked more expensive put them (8)____ even when the final bill was no larger. It is a curious situation: a practice (9)____ almost nobody defends in theory, yet which almost everybody keeps up in practice. Until customers learn to judge a bill by its total rather than by its parts, tipping is here to (10)____.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["from","by"],"answer":"from","explanation":"You gain something <b>from</b> (or <i>by</i>) an action, so <i>gain nothing from doing so</i>. <b>Of</b> does not follow <i>gain</i>, and <b>for</b> would express purpose rather than result."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"An argument that <b>holds up</b> survives being tested. <b>Hold on</b> means to wait or grip, and <b>hold out</b> means to resist or to last, so neither describes an explanation surviving scrutiny."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"<b>Together with</b> adds a second item to the first, like <i>along with</i>. <b>And</b> cannot follow <i>together</i>, and <i>together to</i> is not an English phrase."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"<b>In that</b> means 'for the reason that' and explains why the custom is convenient. <b>So that</b> would express purpose, and <b>because</b> cannot be followed directly by <i>that</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["were"],"answer":"were","explanation":"<b>Were it not for</b> is the inverted form of <i>if it were not for</i>, an unreal conditional meaning 'without'. <b>Was</b> is not used in this inverted structure, and <b>if</b> would need <i>it were</i> after it."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"To be <b>at the mercy of</b> someone is to be entirely in their power. <b>To</b> and <b>from</b> do not complete this fixed phrase."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["is","was"],"answer":"is","explanation":"A sentence beginning with negative <b>Nor</b> inverts subject and verb, and the passage's present-tense discussion calls for <b>is</b>. <b>Does</b> would need a main verb rather than the adjective <i>easy</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["off"],"answer":"off","explanation":"To <b>put someone off</b> is to discourage them; the expensive-looking menu discouraged the customers. <b>Put out</b> means to inconvenience or extinguish, and <b>put down</b> means to insult or to set something down."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"<i>Practice</i> is the object of <i>defends</i> in the relative clause, so <b>that</b> or <b>which</b> links it. <b>Who</b> is reserved for people, and <b>what</b> cannot follow a noun as a relative pronoun."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["stay"],"answer":"stay","explanation":"The idiom <b>here to stay</b> means permanently established. <b>Remain</b> has the right meaning but is not part of the fixed phrase, and <b>last</b> would need a different structure."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-22-5","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"When the Bells Learnt to Count","topic":"the invention of the clock","passage":"For most of human history, time was something you looked up to see. The sun told you roughly where you were in the day, and the stars did the same at night; for anything finer, people (1)____ to make do with the drip of water through a hole or the slow burning of a marked candle. None of these devices was accurate, and (2)____ did they need to be, since nobody's life depended on the quarter hour. The mechanical clock changed that, though not at (3)____ in the way one might expect. The earliest examples, built for monasteries in the late Middle Ages, had no face and no hands (4)____ all; their only job was to strike a bell so that the monks would know when to pray. It was only later that dials were added, and later (5)____ that the minute hand appeared. What drove the improvement was less curiosity (6)____ commerce: merchants needed to agree on when a market opened, and employers wanted to know how long their workers had actually worked. By the eighteenth century a clock accurate to a few seconds a day (7)____ be bought by anyone with money to spare, and towns began to set their bells by it rather than by the sun. (8)____ earlier generations had fitted their work to the light, their descendants fitted it to the dial. Some historians go so (9)____ as to claim that the clock, not the steam engine, was the true machine of the industrial age. Whether or not that is an exaggeration, it is hard to imagine a factory, a railway or a school timetable without (10)____.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"<b>Had to</b> expresses past necessity and is followed by the bare infinitive <i>make</i>. <b>Must</b> has no past form and cannot be followed by <i>to</i>, and <b>were</b> would need <i>forced</i> or <i>obliged</i> before <i>to</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["nor","neither"],"answer":"nor","explanation":"After a negative statement, <b>nor</b> (or <i>neither</i>) opens an inverted clause: <i>nor did they need to be</i>. <b>Not</b> cannot trigger inversion here, and <b>so</b> is used only after a positive statement."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["first","all"],"answer":"first","explanation":"<b>At first</b> means 'in the early stages', which fits a change that arrived only gradually; <b>not at all in the way one might expect</b> (= 'not in the least') also reads naturally, so both are accepted. <b>At last</b> would mean 'finally', the opposite of what the following sentences describe, and <i>at once</i> means immediately."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>At all</b> strengthens a negative: <i>no hands at all</i>. <b>In all</b> means 'in total', and <b>of</b> would need <i>any</i> or <i>none</i> before it."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["still","yet"],"answer":"still","explanation":"<b>Later still</b> means 'even later', adding a third step to the sequence begun by <i>only later</i>. <b>Again</b> would suggest repetition, and <b>more</b> cannot follow a comparative adverb this way."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The comparison <b>less X than Y</b> says that Y mattered more than X. <b>But</b> would need <i>not</i> rather than <i>less</i>, and <b>then</b> is a time adverb that has no comparative sense."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["could","might"],"answer":"could","explanation":"The clause states what was possible in the past, so the past ability modal <b>could</b> fits the passive <i>be bought</i>. <b>Can</b> clashes with the eighteenth-century setting, and <b>had</b> would require <i>to be</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["Whereas","While","Whilst","Where"],"answer":"Whereas","explanation":"The two halves contrast the old habit with the new, so <b>Whereas</b> (or <i>While</i>) introduces the subordinate clause. <b>Although</b> would imply a concession rather than a plain contrast, and <b>Instead</b> cannot introduce a clause."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["far"],"answer":"far","explanation":"<b>Go so far as to</b> + verb means to take an argument to an extreme. <b>Much</b> does not combine with <i>go</i> in this idiom, and <i>so long as</i> means 'provided that'."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["one","it"],"answer":"one","explanation":"<b>One</b> stands in for <i>a clock</i>, the singular countable noun the sentence has been discussing, and <b>it</b> can refer to <i>the clock</i> as an invention. <b>Them</b> would need a plural antecedent, and <b>this</b> would point to the historians' claim instead."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-22-6","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"The Sun That Does Not Rise","topic":"polar night","passage":"Above the Arctic Circle there comes a day each autumn (1)____ the sun rises for the last time, skims the horizon and sets, not to return for weeks or even months. This is the polar night, and it is (2)____ no means as uniform as its name suggests. At its southern edge the darkness is really a long twilight, with the sky glowing blue for several hours around noon; only at the pole itself (3)____ the night become truly complete. Those who live through it describe an odd mixture of dread and delight, (4)____ of which they can quite explain. (5)____ from being a season of hibernation, the winter months are busy ones: schools stay open, fishing boats go out, and the towns hang lights along every street to keep the gloom at (6)____. Even so, doctors record a rise in low mood and broken sleep, (7)____ among newcomers who have yet to learn the value of a strict routine. Locals (8)____ by daylight lamps, vitamin pills and, above all, going outside whatever the weather. By the time the sun returns in late January, the community (9)____ without direct sunlight for nearly two months. (10)____ the hardships, few who have watched that first thin sunrise ever describe it as anything but worth the wait.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["where","which","when","what"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<i>A day</i> is a time expression, so the relative adverb <b>when</b> introduces the clause describing what happens on it. <b>Which</b> would need <i>on</i> before it, and <b>where</b> is for places."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","in","on","by"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>By no means</b> is the fixed emphatic negative meaning 'not at all'. The other prepositions never combine with <i>no means</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["does","is","did","has"],"correct":0,"explanation":"A fronted restrictive <b>only at</b> phrase forces inversion, and the bare verb <i>become</i> in a present-tense description takes the auxiliary <b>does</b>. <b>Is</b> would need <i>becoming</i>, and <b>did</b> breaks the present tense."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["none","neither","either","both"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Two feelings were named, so the negative quantifier for two, <b>neither</b>, is required. <b>None</b> is used for three or more, and <b>either</b> needs a negative verb, which <i>can quite explain</i> is not."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["Apart","Away","Far","Aside"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Far from being</b> denies one description before offering its opposite. <i>Apart from</i> and <i>aside from</i> mean 'except for', and <b>away</b> cannot precede <i>from being</i> in this sense."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["hand","length","large","bay"],"correct":3,"explanation":"To <b>keep something at bay</b> is to stop it coming closer. <i>At hand</i> means nearby, <i>at length</i> means in detail or eventually, and <i>at large</i> means free or in general."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["notably","namely","nearly","lately"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Notably</b> singles out the group in which the rise is most marked. <b>Namely</b> would claim that newcomers are the only ones affected, and <b>nearly</b> and <b>lately</b> do not fit before <i>among</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["insist","swear","rely","depend"],"correct":1,"explanation":"To <b>swear by</b> something is to trust it completely as a remedy. <b>Insist</b>, <b>rely</b> and <b>depend</b> all take <i>on</i>, not <i>by</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the phrase that fits.","options":["had gone","would have gone","will have gone","has gone"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>By the time</b> + present tense points to a future moment, and the state completed before it takes the future perfect <b>will have gone</b>. <b>Had gone</b> belongs to a past narrative, and <b>would have gone</b> is a conditional."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["However","Whichever","Whatever","Whenever"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Whatever the hardships</b> is a concessive phrase meaning 'no matter what the hardships are'. <b>However</b> would need an adjective (<i>however hard</i>), and <b>whichever</b> implies a choice between known items."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-23-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"What Happens Inside a Loaf","topic":"the science of bread","passage":"Bread looks like the simplest of foods, yet what goes on inside a loaf is anything but simple. When flour is mixed with water, two proteins that had been lying dormant in the grain begin to link together, forming a stretchy network known (1)____ gluten. Knead the dough and the network tightens; leave it alone and it relaxes. Bakers speak of 'developing' the dough, and it is this network, (2)____ than the yeast, that decides whether the finished loaf will hold its shape. The yeast, for its part, feeds on the sugars in the flour and gives (3)____ carbon dioxide, which the gluten traps in thousands of tiny pockets. Had the network been weak, the gas would simply have escaped, and the loaf would have come out of the oven as flat as a biscuit. It is only when the dough goes into the oven (4)____ the real drama begins. In the first few minutes the trapped gas expands rapidly, a process bakers refer to (5)____ oven spring, and the loaf can grow by a third before the crust sets. Meanwhile the starch absorbs water and stiffens, and the sugars on the surface turn brown, releasing the smell that draws people into a bakery (6)____ the street. Not until the last century (7)____ scientists understand these reactions in detail; before that, the craft was passed on by hand. Even now, a good baker relies (8)____ much on touch as on thermometers. (9)____ the bread has cooled, one final change takes place: the starch slowly recrystallises, which is (10)____ a loaf goes stale even when it is kept perfectly dry.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The passive collocation is <b>known as</b> + a name. <i>Known for</i> would introduce a quality something is famous for, and <i>known by</i> needs a person who knows."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["rather","more"],"answer":"rather","explanation":"<b>X rather than Y</b> sets one candidate against another inside the cleft sentence; <i>more than the yeast</i> (to a greater degree than the yeast) is an equally natural comparison of degree. <i>Other than</i> means <i>apart from</i>, and <i>less than</i> would credit the yeast instead."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["off","out"],"answer":"off","explanation":"To <b>give off</b> a gas or smell is to release it; <i>give out</i> is also used of heat and gas. <i>Give up</i> means to abandon and <i>give away</i> means to donate, neither of which fits."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"The cleft structure <b>It is only when … that …</b> needs <i>that</i> to introduce the main clause. <i>Which</i> or <i>when</i> would leave the sentence without a main clause at all."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"You <b>refer to</b> something <b>as</b> a name. <i>Refer to it by</i> is possible only with <i>by the name of</i>, and <i>refer to it for</i> means consult it for information."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["from","off","across","down"],"answer":"from","explanation":"People are drawn <b>into</b> the bakery <b>from</b> the street, the place they start; <i>off the street</i> is the informal equivalent, and <i>across</i>/<i>down the street</i> also fit by placing the bakery relative to the passer-by. <i>To the street</i> would reverse the direction, and <i>in the street</i> is an awkward description of where a shop is."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"After fronted <b>Not until …</b> the subject and auxiliary invert, so <i>did scientists understand</i> is required. <i>Have</i> would clash with <i>the last century</i>, a finished period, and without inversion the sentence is ungrammatical."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The comparison of equality is <b>as much on X as on Y</b>, so the first <i>as</i> is needed. <i>So much</i> is only used in this pattern after a negative, and <i>too much</i> would mean excessively."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["once","after","when"],"answer":"once","explanation":"<b>Once</b> (or <i>after</i>, <i>when</i>) the bread has cooled introduces the moment the final change starts. <i>Until</i> would make the change stop at that point, and <i>while</i> would place it during the cooling rather than after it."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["why"],"answer":"why","explanation":"<b>Which is why</b> presents the recrystallising as the reason for the loaf going stale. <i>Which is how</i> would describe a method, and <i>which is because</i> reverses cause and effect."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-23-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Farming on the Slopes of Fire","topic":"volcanic soil","passage":"Few landscapes look less promising for farming than the slopes of a volcano, yet some of the most densely settled countryside on earth lies precisely there. The explanation, (1)____ it turns out, is in the ash. When a volcano erupts, it throws out rock that has been ground into fine particles, each of them rich (2)____ potassium, phosphorus and a dozen other minerals that plants crave. Ordinary soil takes centuries to build (3)____ such reserves; a single eruption can deliver them in an afternoon. No sooner (4)____ the ash cooled than rain begins to break it down, releasing the minerals in a form that roots can absorb. The result is land so fertile that farmers on the flanks of certain volcanoes harvest three crops a year, (5)____ their neighbours a valley away manage only one. It is hardly surprising, (6)____, that people keep returning to places that have been destroyed more than once within living memory. The risk is real, but so (7)____ the reward, and for a family with little land the choice is not a difficult one. Governments have tried to move such communities to safer ground, usually (8)____ little success; the villagers drift back within a generation. Geologists like to point (9)____ that the very ash which buried a town in one century may be feeding its orchards in the next. Had the mountain never erupted, the valley (10)____ today be as poor as any other in the region.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The parenthetical comment is <b>as it turns out</b>, meaning <i>as we discover</i>. <i>So it turns out</i> would have to stand as its own clause, and <i>if it turns out</i> makes the explanation uncertain, which the passage does not intend."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["in"],"answer":"in","explanation":"Something is <b>rich in</b> the minerals it contains. <i>Rich with</i> is occasionally seen in literary style but not with a list of chemical elements, and <i>rich of</i> does not exist."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["up"],"answer":"up","explanation":"To <b>build up</b> reserves is to accumulate them gradually. <i>Build on</i> means to use something as a foundation, and <i>build in</i> means to include as a fixed part."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["has","is"],"answer":"has","explanation":"The inverted <b>No sooner has X happened than Y happens</b> describes a general, repeated sequence, so the present perfect <i>has</i> is needed after the fronted adverb; <i>no sooner is the ash cooled</i>, treating <i>cooled</i> as a state, is also possible. <i>Had</i> would shift the whole account into a single past occasion, which the present tense <i>begins</i> rules out."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["while","whereas","whilst","though","although"],"answer":"while","explanation":"<b>While</b> or <b>whereas</b> sets the three harvests against the neighbours' single one; concessive <i>though</i>/<i>although</i> makes the same contrast. <i>But</i> cannot follow a comma inside this sentence without a subject change, and <i>because</i> would make the neighbours' poor harvest the cause of the good one."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["then","therefore","perhaps"],"answer":"then","explanation":"<b>Then</b> (or <i>therefore</i>) between commas draws the conclusion from the fertility just described; a hedging <i>perhaps</i> also fits the slot. <i>However</i> would signal a contrast, and <i>too</i> would add a second surprising thing when none has been mentioned."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["is"],"answer":"is","explanation":"<b>So + auxiliary + subject</b> repeats the previous verb for a new subject: <i>the risk is real, but so is the reward</i>. <i>Does</i> would need a full verb to stand in for, and <i>are</i> disagrees with the singular <i>reward</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["with"],"answer":"with","explanation":"An attempt is made <b>with little success</b>; the preposition describes the result that accompanies the action. <i>In success</i> and <i>at success</i> are not English, and <i>for little success</i> would mean the success was the aim."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"To <b>point out</b> that something is true is to draw attention to it. <i>Point to</i> takes a noun, not a <i>that</i>-clause, and <i>point at</i> is a physical gesture."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["would","might","could"],"answer":"would","explanation":"This is a mixed conditional: a past condition (<i>had the mountain never erupted</i>) with a present result marked by <i>today</i>, so <b>would</b> (or the more tentative <i>might</i>/<i>could</i>) + bare infinitive is needed. <i>Would have been</i> would place the result in the past, which <i>today</i> contradicts, and <i>will</i> cannot follow an unreal condition."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-23-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Business of Birdsong","topic":"why birds sing","passage":"Anyone who has been woken by a blackbird at four in the morning might reasonably ask what the bird thinks it is doing. For a long time the answer seemed obvious: birds sing because they are happy. Only in the last century (1)____ scientists begin to test that assumption, and what they found was far more interesting. Song, it turns out, is mostly a matter of business. A male sings, first of all, to lay (2)____ to a territory; every phrase is a warning to rivals that the trees around him are already taken. He sings, secondly, to attract a mate, and here the quality of the performance matters a great deal. Females of many species prefer males (3)____ repertoires are larger, on the grounds that a bird which has learned many songs has probably lived long enough to do so. The dawn chorus, (4)____, is less a celebration than a daily announcement that the singer has made it through the night. (5)____ does the story end there. Young birds learn their songs much as children learn speech, by listening to adults and then practising, badly (6)____ first, until the phrases come out cleanly. A chick raised in silence will grow up with a thin, incomplete song, (7)____ matter how healthy it is. Some species even develop local dialects, so that a bird from one valley can be told (8)____ from one a few miles away. Whether birds take any pleasure in all this is a question science cannot answer. (9)____ we can say is that the singing is neither idle nor accidental, and that the blackbird outside the window is doing far more (10)____ simply greeting the sun.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"A fronted restrictive phrase such as <b>Only in the last century</b> forces inversion, so the auxiliary <i>did</i> must come before <i>scientists</i>. <i>Have</i> is tempting because of <i>the last century</i>, but the following verb <i>begin</i> is a bare infinitive and the account continues in the past."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["claim"],"answer":"claim","explanation":"To <b>lay claim to</b> something is to assert that it is yours, which is exactly what a territorial song does. <i>Lay hold of</i> means to seize physically, and <i>lay title to</i> is not a fixed expression."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"The relative pronoun shows possession: the repertoires belong to the males, so <b>whose</b> is needed. <i>Which</i> and <i>that</i> would require <i>repertoires</i> to be the subject of the clause without an owner, and <i>of which</i> is two words in the wrong order here."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["then","therefore"],"answer":"then","explanation":"<b>Then</b> (or <i>therefore</i>) between commas signals that this sentence sums up the two reasons just given. <i>However</i> would contradict them, and <i>too</i> would add the chorus as a further example rather than a conclusion."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["nor","neither"],"answer":"nor","explanation":"<b>Nor does the story end there</b> is an inverted negative adding a further point after previous negatives. <i>Not</i> cannot begin a clause with an auxiliary inverted like this, and <i>never</i> would mean the story is endless."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>at first</b>, meaning in the early stages. <i>In first</i> and <i>on first</i> do not exist as adverbials, and <i>from first</i> would need <i>the</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["no"],"answer":"no","explanation":"<b>No matter how</b> + adjective means regardless of how healthy it is. <i>Not matter</i> is ungrammatical, and <i>whatever</i> cannot be followed by <i>matter how</i>."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["apart"],"answer":"apart","explanation":"To <b>tell one thing apart from</b> another is to distinguish them. <i>Tell off</i> means to scold, and <i>tell away</i> is not an English phrasal verb."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["what","all"],"answer":"what","explanation":"<b>What we can say is …</b> is a pseudo-cleft that puts the whole statement in focus; <i>All we can say</i> works the same way with a hint of limitation. <i>That we can say</i> is not a noun clause, and <i>Which</i> needs an antecedent."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"The comparative <b>far more than</b> requires <i>than</i>. <i>Far more as</i> is not a comparison, and <i>far more of</i> would need a noun rather than an <i>-ing</i> form after it."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-23-4","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"A Room for Strangers","topic":"the history of tea houses","passage":"Long before coffee reached Europe, the tea house had already become a fixture of city life across much of Asia. The earliest ones, (1)____ far as records show, were little more than roadside stalls where travellers could rest and refill a flask. Within a few centuries, however, they had grown (2)____ something much grander: rooms with painted screens, musicians in the corner and a proprietor who knew every customer by name. Merchants struck deals there, poets exchanged verses, and matchmakers arranged marriages over a pot that (3)____ be refilled a dozen times before anyone stood up to leave. What made the tea house unusual was that it belonged to nobody in particular. A scholar and a porter might sit at neighbouring tables, (4)____ they would never have shared a room anywhere else. Rulers were not always comfortable with this. More than one emperor tried to close the tea houses (5)____ the grounds that too much talk was going on inside them, and more than one failed, (6)____ a city deprived of its tea houses quickly became restless. Only when the trade routes shifted (7)____ the great tea houses begin to decline; rival drinks arrived, and the young preferred novelty. Yet the institution never quite disappeared. In many cities today the old houses are being restored, (8)____ least in part by people who have never drunk tea from a lidded cup in their lives. Had the tea house merely sold tea, it would have vanished long (9)____. It survived because it sold something rarer: a place (10)____ strangers could sit as equals.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["as","so"],"answer":"as","explanation":"<b>As far as records show</b> limits the claim to what the evidence supports; <i>so far as</i> is an accepted variant. <i>Too far as</i> and <i>how far as</i> are not fixed expressions."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"To <b>grow into</b> something is to develop until you become it. <i>Grow to</i> is used with an infinitive (<i>grow to love</i>) or a size, and <i>grow up</i> is used of children, not institutions."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["might","could","would"],"answer":"might","explanation":"The pot <b>might</b> (or <i>could</i>, <i>would</i>) be refilled: a past habit or possibility described from the narrator's viewpoint. <i>May</i> and <i>can</i> are present forms that clash with the past narrative, and <i>must</i> would turn a custom into an obligation."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["though","although","whereas","while","whilst","when"],"answer":"though","explanation":"The clause contrasts what happened inside the tea house with what would never happen outside it, so a concessive or contrastive linker (<b>though</b>, <i>although</i>, <i>whereas</i>, <i>while</i>, or contrastive <i>when</i>) is needed. <i>Because</i> would make the sharing a result of the refusal, and <i>so</i> gets the logic backwards."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["on","upon"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The justification for an action is given <b>on the grounds that</b>. <i>For the grounds</i> and <i>with the grounds</i> are not English, and <i>in the grounds</i> would refer to the land around a building."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["for","because","since","as"],"answer":"for","explanation":"The clause explains why the emperors failed, so a reason linker is required: literary <b>for</b>, or <i>because</i>, <i>since</i>, <i>as</i>. <i>So</i> would make the restlessness a consequence of the failure rather than its cause, and <i>while</i> would introduce a contrast that is not there."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"After a fronted <b>Only when …</b> clause, the main clause inverts: <i>did the great tea houses begin</i>. <i>Had</i> would need a past participle rather than <i>begin</i>, and the uninverted <i>the tea houses began</i> cannot follow the fronted phrase."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>At least in part</b> means partly, if not more. <i>In least</i> and <i>for least</i> do not exist, and <i>not least</i> would mean especially, which does not fit before <i>in part</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["ago","since","before"],"answer":"ago","explanation":"<b>Long ago</b> (or <i>long since</i>, <i>long before</i>) places the imagined disappearance well in the past, completing the third conditional. <i>Long after</i> would need a reference event, and <i>long already</i> is not English."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["where"],"answer":"where","explanation":"The relative adverb <b>where</b> links <i>a place</i> to the clause that describes what happens in it. <i>Which</i> would need a preposition (<i>in which</i>), and <i>that</i> cannot replace <i>in which</i> after <i>place</i> when no preposition follows."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-23-5","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Men Who Cut Through Hills","topic":"the tunnel builders","passage":"When the first railway tunnels were driven through the hills of northern England, nobody involved had done anything like it before. The engineers had drawings; the men who actually cut the rock had (1)____ but their hands, a few tools and an enormous amount of nerve. Work went on around the clock, gangs (2)____ over from one another every twelve hours, and progress was measured in feet. Had the two teams digging from opposite ends missed each other (3)____ even a few yards, the whole scheme would have been a laughing stock. In the event they met almost exactly (4)____ planned, a feat of surveying that still impresses engineers today. The dangers were constant. Water broke in without warning, roofs collapsed, and the candles the men worked (5)____ frequently went out, leaving them in darkness a mile underground. Casualties were high, and it is only recently that historians have begun to put names to those (6)____ lives the tunnels cost. What the men were paid was, by the standards of the day, generous; what they endured (7)____ earn it was not. Contractors pushed relentlessly, since their own fortunes depended (8)____ speed, and inspectors rarely visited. Yet by all accounts morale among the gangs remained remarkably high, partly out of pride in the work and partly because there was nowhere (9)____ to go. The tunnels are still in use. Trains pass through them at speeds their builders could scarcely have imagined, and hardly (10)____ of the passengers gives a thought to how the passage was made.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type one word.","accept":["nothing","little"],"answer":"nothing","explanation":"<b>Nothing but</b> means only; <i>little but</i> softens it slightly and is also acceptable. <i>Anything but</i> means the opposite of, and <i>everything but</i> would mean the men had all except their hands."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type one word.","accept":["taking"],"answer":"taking","explanation":"To <b>take over from</b> someone is to replace them on duty; the participle <i>taking</i> fits the absolute clause. <i>Handing over</i> describes the gang leaving, not the one arriving, and <i>getting over</i> means recovering."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type one word.","accept":["by"],"answer":"by","explanation":"The margin of an error or difference is expressed with <b>by</b>: <i>missed by a few yards</i>. <i>For a few yards</i> would give a distance travelled, and <i>with a few yards</i> makes no sense of the measurement."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type one word.","accept":["as","where"],"answer":"as","explanation":"<b>As planned</b> is the reduced form of <i>as it had been planned</i>; <i>where planned</i> stresses the location and is also defensible. <i>Like planned</i> is non-standard, and <i>than planned</i> needs a comparative before it."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type one word.","accept":["by"],"answer":"by","explanation":"You work <b>by</b> candlelight, the source of illumination, so the stranded preposition in the reduced relative clause is <i>by</i>. <i>With</i> would suggest the candles were tools, and <i>under</i> would need the candles to be above the men."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type one word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"The lives belonged to the men, so the possessive relative <b>whose</b> is needed: <i>those whose lives the tunnels cost</i>. <i>Which</i> and <i>that</i> cannot show possession, and <i>who</i> would leave <i>lives</i> without a link to <i>those</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type one word.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"The infinitive of purpose <b>to earn it</b> explains why they endured what they did. <i>For earn</i> is ungrammatical before a verb, and <i>and earn</i> would make the earning a separate hardship."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type one word.","accept":["on","upon"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The verb is <b>depend on</b> (or the more formal <i>upon</i>). <i>Depend of</i> is a common error borrowed from other languages, and <i>depend from</i> is not English."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type one word.","accept":["else"],"answer":"else","explanation":"<b>Nowhere else</b> means no other place. <i>Nowhere other</i> requires <i>than</i> and a comparison, and <i>nowhere more</i> would need an adjective."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type one word.","accept":["any"],"answer":"any","explanation":"<b>Hardly any of</b> the passengers means almost none, and the singular verb <i>gives</i> treats the group as a quantity. <i>Hardly some</i> and <i>hardly no</i> are ungrammatical, and <i>hardly one of</i> is not idiomatic."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-23-6","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"A Cloud at Ground Level","topic":"how fog forms","passage":"Fog is simply a cloud that has lost its way, or rather one that has formed at ground level instead of high above it. Air always holds some water vapour, and the warmer the air, the (1)____ vapour it can carry. When that air cools, it eventually reaches the point (2)____ which it can hold no more, and the surplus condenses into droplets so small that they hang in the air rather than falling. Whether this happens on a clear night, after a shower or over the sea depends (3)____ how the cooling takes place. The most familiar kind forms on calm, cloudless nights, when the ground loses heat to the sky and chills the layer of air (4)____ contact with it. By dawn a valley may be filled to the brim, (5)____ the hilltops around it are already in sunshine. Coastal fog behaves differently: warm, moist air drifts over a cold current and cools from below, sometimes for days (6)____ end. Cities once suffered a third kind, in which smoke provided the particles (7)____ which droplets could gather. So thick did this become at times (8)____ buses had to be led through the streets by men on foot. Fog rarely lasts long once the sun is up, (9)____ the same air that produced it warms again and reabsorbs the droplets. Forecasters can now predict it with fair accuracy, (10)____ a night of fog still brings airports to a standstill more often than most travellers would like.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["much","more","many","most"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The double comparative <b>the warmer …, the more …</b> links two changes that rise together. <i>Most</i> is a superlative, and <i>much</i> and <i>many</i> are not comparative forms."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["at","in","on","by"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Something happens <b>at</b> a point, so the relative phrase is <i>the point at which</i>. <i>In which</i> would suit a period or a state, and <i>on</i> and <i>by</i> do not collocate with <i>point</i> in this sense."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["of","in","from","on"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The verb is <b>depend on</b>, and the preposition is kept before an indirect question (<i>depends on how</i>). <i>Depend of</i> is a frequent learner error, and <i>in</i> and <i>from</i> never follow <i>depend</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["by","on","in","at"],"correct":2,"explanation":"Two things touching are <b>in contact with</b> each other. <i>On contact</i> means at the moment of touching, which changes the sense, and <i>at</i> and <i>by</i> do not form the phrase."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["despite","while","meanwhile","during"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>While</b> introduces a clause that contrasts the foggy valley with the sunny hilltops. <i>During</i> and <i>despite</i> are prepositions and cannot take a clause, and <i>meanwhile</i> is an adverb that cannot join two clauses after a comma."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","at","in","to"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>For days on end</b> means for many days without a break. <i>At an end</i> means finished, <i>in the end</i> means finally, and <i>to end</i> is not an idiom of duration."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["over","under","about","around"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Droplets gather <b>around</b> a particle, which acts as a core. <i>About</i> in this sense is archaic, <i>over</i> suggests covering a surface, and <i>under</i> puts the droplets beneath the particle."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["as","so","that","than"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The inverted structure <b>So thick did it become that …</b> introduces the result with <i>that</i>. <i>Than</i> belongs with comparatives, <i>as</i> would need <i>as thick as</i>, and a second <i>so</i> has no role here."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["whereas","since","so","unless"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The clause explains why fog clears, so a reason linker, <b>since</b>, is needed. <i>So</i> would make the warming a result of the clearing, <i>unless</i> makes it a condition that prevents it, and <i>whereas</i> signals a contrast that is not intended."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["for","nor","so","yet"],"correct":3,"explanation":"Good forecasts and airports still stopping are in contrast, which <b>yet</b> expresses after a comma. <i>So</i> and <i>for</i> would present the standstill as a consequence or a reason, and <i>nor</i> needs a preceding negative and inversion."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-24-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Last Steam Trains","topic":"the last steam trains","passage":"For more than a century the steam locomotive was the beating heart of the railway, and (1)____ was it more revered than in the small towns whose prosperity depended on the line. Yet by the late 1950s the writing was already (2)____ the wall. Diesel engines, which needed neither a fireman nor hours of preparation before dawn, could be started at the turn of a key, and railway companies, (3)____ accounts had been sliding into the red for years, were quick to see the appeal. No sooner (4)____ the first diesels been delivered than the older engines began to be withdrawn, sometimes with only a few weeks' notice. Crews who had spent thirty years learning to coax a temperamental boiler into life found themselves retrained in a matter of days, and many never (5)____ to terms with the change.<br><br>It was the enthusiasts, rather (6)____ the companies, who saved what could be saved. Had they not raised the money to buy engines that were bound (7)____ the scrapyard, hardly a single one would survive today. Instead, several hundred were rescued and restored, and every summer weekend they can still be seen climbing the steep valleys of preserved lines. Not one of them earns a penny in freight, (8)____ they draw far larger crowds than any diesel ever did. What (9)____ them apart, the volunteers insist, is not nostalgia but craft: a steam engine has to be understood, not merely operated. Whether those skills can be handed (10)____ to a generation that has never smelt coal smoke is another question, and one the last of the old drivers ask with some anxiety.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["nowhere"],"answer":"nowhere","explanation":"A negative adverb at the front of a clause forces inversion: <b>nowhere was it more revered than</b>. <i>Never</i> would not fit because the comparison is between places, not times, and <i>there</i> would need normal word order."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"The idiom is <b>the writing is on the wall</b>, meaning that an ending is clearly coming. <i>At</i> or <i>against</i> the wall would describe a physical position and lose the idiom."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["whose"],"answer":"whose","explanation":"A possessive relative is needed: the companies' accounts, so <b>whose accounts</b>. <i>Which</i> or <i>that</i> cannot show possession, and <i>their</i> would leave the clause with no relative link."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["had"],"answer":"had","explanation":"<b>No sooner had X happened than Y</b> takes the past perfect with subject-auxiliary inversion. <i>Were</i> would give a passive with the wrong tense, and <i>have</i> breaks the past-time sequence."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["came"],"answer":"came","explanation":"The collocation is <b>come to terms with</b> something, meaning to accept it. <i>Got</i> or <i>went</i> do not combine with <i>to terms</i>, and the narrative past requires <i>came</i>, not <i>come</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"Inside this cleft sentence, <b>rather than</b> contrasts the enthusiasts with the companies. <i>Then</i> is a common misspelling that refers to time, and <i>not</i> cannot follow <i>rather</i> here."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"<b>Bound for</b> a place means heading towards it, here the scrapyard. <i>Bound to</i> is followed by a verb (bound to fail), not a destination."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["yet","but","though","although"],"answer":"yet","explanation":"The two halves contrast, so a concessive linker is needed: <b>yet</b> or <b>but</b> (or <i>though/although</i> introducing the second clause). <i>So</i> would make the crowds a result of earning nothing, which is not the meaning."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["sets"],"answer":"sets","explanation":"<b>Set something apart</b> means to make it different or special. <i>Puts</i> and <i>keeps</i> apart would mean physically separating the engines, and the present simple with <i>what</i> requires the -s form."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["down","on"],"answer":"down","explanation":"Skills are <b>handed down</b> (or <b>handed on</b>) to a later generation. <i>Handed over</i> means surrendering something, and <i>handed out</i> means distributing copies."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-24-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Map Makers","topic":"the map makers","passage":"Before satellites, a map was only (1)____ good as the people who had walked the ground it showed. Surveyors would set (2)____ at first light with a chain, a compass and a heavy brass instrument for measuring angles, and by nightfall they might have fixed the position of a dozen hills. The work was slow, dangerous and, (3)____ the most part, invisible: nobody thanked a surveyor for a border that ran exactly where it should. So exacting were the standards (4)____ a single error in a baseline measurement could throw out every distance calculated from it for a hundred miles.<br><br>It is easy to forget how (5)____ of the world remained blank until quite recently. Well (6)____ the twentieth century, whole river systems appeared on official maps as dotted lines, an honest admission that no one had followed them to their source. The cartographers themselves were rarely the ones who had done the walking; more (7)____ than not, they were drawing up what explorers, traders and soldiers had reported. All too frequently those reports flatly contradicted (8)____ another, and the man at the drawing board had to decide whom to believe. Little (9)____ they know that their careful guesswork would one day be checked from orbit. When that day came, the surprise was not how much they had got wrong (10)____ how much, armed with nothing more than patience and arithmetic, they had got right.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["as"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The equal comparison is <b>only as good as</b> the people. <i>So good as</i> is not used in this positive frame, and <i>too</i> would need a following <i>to</i>-infinitive."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["off","out","forth"],"answer":"off","explanation":"<b>Set off</b> and <b>set out</b> both mean to begin a journey (the older <i>set forth</i> is also possible). <i>Set up</i> means to establish something, and <i>set about</i> would need an object or an -ing form after it."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["for"],"answer":"for","explanation":"The fixed phrase is <b>for the most part</b>, meaning mostly. <i>In</i> or <i>on</i> the most part are not English expressions."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["that"],"answer":"that","explanation":"The inverted structure <b>So exacting were the standards that...</b> needs <i>that</i> to introduce the result. <i>Which</i> would turn the clause into a relative one with no result meaning."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["much"],"answer":"much","explanation":"<i>The world</i> is treated as an uncountable whole, so the quantifier is <b>how much of</b>. <i>Many of</i> requires a plural noun, and <i>most of</i> cannot follow <i>how</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"<b>Well into the twentieth century</b> means a long way past its start. <i>Well in</i> is not a time expression, and <i>until</i> would mean the dotted lines stopped exactly then."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["often"],"answer":"often","explanation":"The idiom <b>more often than not</b> means usually. <i>Likely</i> gives <i>more likely than not</i>, which describes probability, not frequency of practice, and does not describe what they habitually did."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["one"],"answer":"one","explanation":"The reciprocal pronoun is <b>one another</b> (the reports contradicted each other). <i>Each</i> pairs with <i>other</i>, not with <i>another</i>, so <i>each another</i> is impossible."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"<b>Little did they know</b> is a fixed inversion in the past simple. <i>Had</i> would require a past participle (<i>had they known</i>), which changes both tense and meaning."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["but"],"answer":"but","explanation":"The pattern <b>not X but Y</b> replaces one idea with another. <i>As</i> would need <i>not so much X as Y</i>, and <i>than</i> would need a comparative before it."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-24-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Why Rivers Bend","topic":"why rivers bend","passage":"Ask a child to draw a river and the chances (1)____ that it will run straight from the mountains to the sea. Real rivers do nothing (2)____ the kind. Left to themselves, they wander, and the wandering is not random (3)____ all: it obeys rules that engineers have spent a century trying to write down.<br><br>The process begins with the smallest irregularity. (4)____ the current meets a patch of softer bank, it eats into it. The water on the outside of the new curve then speeds up, (5)____ the water on the inside slows down and drops the sand it was carrying. The faster water scours the bank (6)____ further, the slower water builds a beach, and so the bend grows. The larger the bend becomes, (7)____ more sharply the river must turn, until eventually the loop is so tight that the water simply breaks through the narrow neck of land and abandons the curve altogether. What is left (8)____ is a crescent-shaped lake, cut off from the stream that made it.<br><br>Engineers who straighten rivers in the name of flood control have learned, often at great (9)____, that a river deprived of its bends will spend its energy elsewhere, cutting deeper or flooding faster. Far (10)____ being a flaw, the meander is the river's way of spending its energy safely, and the wiser planners now leave it alone.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["are"],"answer":"are","explanation":"The idiom is <b>the chances are that...</b>, meaning it is likely. <i>Chances</i> is plural, so <i>is</i> fails agreement, and <i>were</i> would clash with the present-tense <i>will run</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"<b>Nothing of the kind</b> is a fixed expression meaning not that at all. <i>Like</i> would need <i>nothing like that</i>, and <i>in</i> the kind is not English."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>Not ... at all</b> is the emphatic negative. <i>After all</i> means despite expectations, and <i>in all</i> means in total, neither of which fits here."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["where","when","once","if","wherever","whenever","as"],"answer":"where","explanation":"A conditional or place/time conjunction opens the clause: <b>Where</b> (or <i>when/once/if</i>) the current meets softer bank. <i>Which</i> would need a preceding noun to refer to, and <i>while</i> suggests two simultaneous actions rather than a trigger."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["while","whereas","whilst","as","and"],"answer":"while","explanation":"The clause contrasts the outside of the curve with the inside, so <b>while</b> or <b>whereas</b> (or a plain <i>and</i>) fits. <i>But</i> after a comma with this contrast is possible in speech but weak in formal prose, and <i>because</i> would wrongly make one speed the cause of the other."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["still","even","yet","ever","much"],"answer":"still","explanation":"<b>Still further</b> (or <i>even/yet/ever further</i>) intensifies the comparative and shows the scouring going on beyond its earlier extent; <i>much further</i> is also possible. <i>More further</i> is a double comparative, and <i>very</i> cannot modify a comparative."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["the"],"answer":"the","explanation":"Correlative comparatives take the shape <b>the larger ..., the more sharply ...</b>. Both halves need the definite article; <i>so</i> or <i>then</i> breaks the pattern."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["behind"],"answer":"behind","explanation":"What remains after the river moves away is <b>left behind</b>. <i>Left over</i> refers to an unused remainder of a quantity, such as food, not to a landform abandoned in place."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["cost","expense"],"answer":"cost","explanation":"<b>At great cost</b> or <b>at great expense</b> means with heavy losses. <i>Price</i> is used with <i>pay a high price</i>, not with <i>at great</i>."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["from"],"answer":"from","explanation":"<b>Far from being a flaw</b> denies one description before offering another. <i>Far off</i> and <i>far away</i> describe distance and cannot take an -ing complement in this sense."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-24-4","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Story of the Potato","topic":"the story of the potato","passage":"Few foods have travelled (1)____ far, or changed as many lives, as the humble potato. Cultivated for thousands of years in the high valleys of the Andes, it reached Europe in the sixteenth century and was, (2)____ first, regarded with deep suspicion. It grew underground, it was not mentioned in any holy book, and it belonged to a family of plants several members of (3)____ were poisonous. For nearly two hundred years it was fed to animals rather (4)____ to people.<br><br>Only when famine and war had made grain scarce (5)____ governments begin to promote it in earnest. One king is said to have posted guards around his potato fields by day and quietly withdrawn them by night, (6)____ the assumption that peasants would steal anything worth guarding. (7)____ or not the story is true, the strategy it describes worked: within a generation the crop had taken hold across northern Europe, and a peasant family with a small plot could feed itself through the winter.<br><br>The potato's great virtue, that it yields more food per acre than almost any other crop, turned (8)____ to be its great danger as well. Whole regions came to depend on a single variety, so that when disease struck in the 1840s there was nothing to (9)____ back on, and a million people starved. Had those farmers planted the dozens of varieties their Andean predecessors had grown, the blight (10)____ have been no more than a footnote in history rather than a catastrophe.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type ONE word.","accept":["as","so"],"answer":"as","explanation":"The comparison is completed by <i>as the humble potato</i>, so the first half needs <b>as far</b> (or, after the negative-sense <i>few</i>, <b>so far</b>). <i>Too</i> and <i>very</i> cannot pair with a following <i>as</i>."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type ONE word.","accept":["at"],"answer":"at","explanation":"<b>At first</b> means initially, before attitudes changed. <i>In</i> the first would need a following noun (<i>in the first place</i>), and <i>from</i> first is not idiomatic."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type ONE word.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"After a preposition in a relative clause only <b>which</b> is possible: <i>several members of which</i>. <i>That</i> cannot follow a preposition, and <i>them</i> would leave two clauses with no link."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type ONE word.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"<b>Rather than</b> sets one option against another. <i>Then</i> refers to time and is a spelling trap, and <i>instead</i> would need <i>of</i>, not <i>rather</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type ONE word.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"<b>Only when ... did governments begin</b>: a fronted <i>only</i> phrase forces inversion in the main clause. <i>Had</i> would need a participle, and no inversion at all (<i>governments began</i>) is ungrammatical after <i>Only when</i>."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type ONE word.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"<b>On the assumption that</b> is the standard collocation. <i>In</i> and <i>with</i> the assumption are not idiomatic, and <i>under</i> collocates with <i>the impression</i>, not <i>the assumption</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type ONE word.","accept":["whether"],"answer":"whether","explanation":"<b>Whether or not</b> introduces a concession covering both possibilities. <i>If</i> cannot be followed directly by <i>or not</i> at the start of a sentence, and <i>even</i> would need <i>if</i> after it."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type ONE word.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"<b>Turn out to be</b> means to prove in the end to be. <i>Turn into</i> takes a noun, not a <i>to</i>-infinitive, and <i>turn up</i> means to arrive."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type ONE word.","accept":["fall"],"answer":"fall","explanation":"<b>Fall back on</b> something means to use it as a reserve when the first choice fails. <i>Go back on</i> means to break a promise, and <i>look back on</i> means to remember."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type ONE word.","accept":["might","would","could"],"answer":"might","explanation":"The inverted <i>Had those farmers planted...</i> is a third conditional, so the main clause needs a past modal plus <i>have</i>: <b>might/would/could have been</b>. <i>May</i> is reserved for open possibilities and <i>will</i> cannot look back at an unreal past."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-24-5","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"The First Hospitals","topic":"the first hospitals","passage":"Nobody knows exactly when the first hospital opened its doors, partly because the word itself has meant so many different things. In the ancient world, a temple (1)____ the sick would come to pray was, in a sense, a hospital; so was the roadside shelter where a monastery fed and washed travellers. What these places had in (2)____ was charity rather than medicine. A doctor might visit, but the institution existed to (3)____ for the dying, not to cure them.<br><br>That began to change in the great cities of the medieval Islamic world, (4)____ rulers competed to endow buildings with wards, pharmacies and libraries. Physicians there kept written records of every patient, an idea (5)____ radical at the time that few outside those cities believed it worth the trouble. Patients were separated (6)____ to their illness, and no one, rich or poor, was turned away.<br><br>Europe was slow to catch (7)____. For centuries its hospitals remained crowded, ill-lit places, and a patient who entered one stood a fair chance of leaving it in a coffin. Not (8)____ the nineteenth century, when reformers insisted on clean linen, fresh air and trained nurses, did survival rates begin to climb. Far (9)____ being feared, hospitals came to be trusted, and by the early twentieth century it was in a hospital, not at home, that most people in industrial countries expected to be born.<br><br>It is tempting to think of all this as steady progress. In truth, every stage was (10)____ about by someone deciding that the sick deserved better than they were getting.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["what","where","which","whose"],"correct":1,"explanation":"The clause says the sick came to pray <i>in</i> the temple, so a relative adverb of place is needed: <b>where</b>. <i>Which</i> would need a preposition (<i>to which</i>), and <i>what</i> cannot introduce a relative clause."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["common","general","particular","mind"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>Have something in common</b> means to share a feature. <i>In general</i> and <i>in particular</i> are adverbials that cannot take <i>had</i> as their verb, and <i>had in mind</i> would mean intended."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["look","tend","attend","care"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Care for</b> the dying means to look after them. <i>Look for</i> means to search, <i>tend</i> takes a direct object with no <i>for</i>, and <i>attend</i> pairs with <i>to</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["whom","which","whose","who"],"correct":2,"explanation":"The rulers belonged to the cities, so the possessive relative <b>whose rulers</b> is required. <i>Who</i> and <i>whom</i> refer to people directly, and <i>which rulers</i> would make no grammatical link with <i>cities</i>."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["very","so","such","too"],"correct":1,"explanation":"Before a bare adjective followed by a <i>that</i>-clause, the intensifier is <b>so</b>: <i>so radical that</i>. <i>Such</i> needs a noun phrase, <i>too</i> is followed by an infinitive, and <i>very</i> cannot introduce a result clause."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["according","due","owing","thanks"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<b>According to their illness</b> means classified by it. <i>Due to</i>, <i>owing to</i> and <i>thanks to</i> all express cause, which is not the meaning of sorting patients into wards."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["on","out","in","up"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Catch up</b> means to reach the level others have already reached. <i>Catch on</i> means to become popular or to understand, and <i>catch out</i> means to expose a mistake."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["since","after","until","before"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Not until ... did</b> is the fronted negative that triggers the inversion later in the sentence. <i>Before</i>, <i>since</i> and <i>after</i> do not form this structure with <i>not</i> and would leave the inverted <i>did</i> unexplained."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["apart","from","off","away"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Far from being feared</b> means not feared at all but the opposite. <i>Far off</i>, <i>far away</i> and <i>far apart</i> describe physical distance and cannot govern an -ing clause here."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["carried","come","taken","brought"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Bring about</b> means to cause, and the passive <i>was brought about by</i> fits. <i>Come about</i> has the same meaning but is intransitive, so it has no passive, and <i>carried/taken about</i> are not phrasal verbs of causing."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-25-1","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"The Wires Beneath the Waves","topic":"the deep-sea cables","passage":"Few (1)____ the people who tap a message to a friend on another continent ever pause to wonder how it gets there. Satellites, most of them would guess; in fact, well over ninety per cent of the world's international traffic travels through cables no thicker than a garden hose, lying on the ocean floor. Not (2)____ the first telegraph line was hauled across the Atlantic in the 1850s did anyone believe such a thing could work, and even then the first attempt failed within weeks. Had the engineers of that era given (3)____ after that failure, the modern internet might have taken a very different shape. Instead they tried again, and by the end of the century a web of copper wires had been laid across the seabed, linking the continents (4)____ one another. The cables of today carry light rather than electric pulses, but the way they are installed has changed surprisingly (5)____. A specially built ship pays the cable out slowly from an enormous drum, sometimes travelling for weeks (6)____ ever seeing land. Near the coast, (7)____ fishing nets and anchors pose the greatest danger, the cable is buried in a trench; further out it simply rests on the bottom. Repairs, (8)____ they are needed, are carried out by grappling for the broken end and hauling it to the surface. It is slow, expensive work, (9)____ is why cable companies guard the routes of their lines so jealously. So dependent (10)____ we become on this hidden network that a single cut can leave an entire island nation offline for days.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["of"],"answer":"of","explanation":"<b>Few of the people who…</b> — a quantifier followed by a definite noun phrase needs <i>of</i>. <i>Among</i> would need a different structure (<i>few among them</i>) and <i>from</i> is not used with quantifiers."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["until","till"],"answer":"until","explanation":"The inverted <i>did anyone believe</i> later in the sentence signals a fronted negative time phrase: <b>Not until</b> (or <i>till</i>) <i>X did anyone believe</i>. <i>Not when</i> and <i>not before</i> are not used to trigger this inversion."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["up","in"],"answer":"up","explanation":"To <b>give up</b> is to abandon an effort, which is what <i>after that failure</i> and <i>Instead they tried again</i> point to; <i>give in</i> (surrender) is also acceptable. <i>Give out</i> means to run out or distribute and does not fit."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["to","with"],"answer":"to","explanation":"You <b>link</b> one thing <b>to</b> (or <i>with</i>) another. <i>Between</i> would need a plural object on its own (<i>linking the continents between them</i> is not idiomatic) and <i>by</i> would describe the means, not the partner."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["little"],"answer":"little","explanation":"<b>Changed surprisingly little</b> — the following sentence describes the same slow drum-and-ship method, so the contrast is that the technology moved on but the method did not. <i>Few</i> is for countable nouns and <i>less</i> needs a comparison."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"<b>Without ever seeing land</b> — <i>without</i> + -ing is the standard way to say something does not happen during an action. <i>Before</i> would suggest the ship eventually sees land at a specific point, and <i>never</i> cannot precede <i>ever</i>."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["where","because","since","as"],"answer":"where","explanation":"The clause explains why the cable is buried <i>near the coast</i>: the relative adverb <b>where</b> (= a place in which nets and anchors are the danger) fits, and a reason linker (<i>because</i>, <i>since</i>, <i>as</i>) is equally defensible. <i>Which</i> fails because <i>fishing nets and anchors</i> already fill the subject slot of the clause."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["when","whenever","if","where"],"answer":"when","explanation":"<b>Repairs, when they are needed, are…</b> — a conditional-time linker inside commas; <i>whenever</i>, <i>if</i> and <i>where</i> are equally defensible. <i>While</i> fails because repairs do not happen during the need, they follow it, and <i>which</i> cannot introduce a clause with its own subject <i>they</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["which"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A sentence relative: after a comma, <b>which is why</b> refers back to the whole idea that the work is slow and expensive. <i>That is why</i> would need a full stop before it, and <i>what</i> cannot refer back to a preceding clause."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["have","did"],"answer":"have","explanation":"A fronted <i>So + adjective</i> forces subject–auxiliary inversion: <b>So dependent have we become… that</b>. Present perfect matches the present result <i>can leave</i>; <i>did we become</i> is grammatical too, but <i>are we become</i> and <i>were we become</i> are not English."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-25-2","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"From Shade to Shower","topic":"the history of the umbrella","passage":"The umbrella is such an ordinary object that it is easy to forget how recently it became one. For most of its history it was not a shield against rain (1)____ against the sun, as its name, taken from the Latin word for shade, still reminds us. Carvings from ancient Egypt and Assyria show servants holding broad canopies (2)____ the heads of kings, and in those societies to walk beneath one was a mark of rank that no commoner would have dared (3)____ claim. It was in China, several centuries later, that someone (4)____ on the idea of oiling the paper so that the canopy would repel water, and the device we know today began to take shape. Europe, however, was slow to adopt it. Well (5)____ the eighteenth century, an Englishman seen carrying an umbrella still risked being jeered at by coachmen, who feared, not (6)____ reason, that a portable roof might cost them their customers. One London merchant is said to have endured thirty years of mockery before the habit finally (7)____ on. What changed public opinion was not fashion so much as arithmetic: asked to choose (8)____ a daily cab fare and a few shillings for a folding frame, a sensible person eventually chose the frame. (9)____ the steel rib was patented in the 1850s, the umbrella became lighter, cheaper and far more reliable, and by the end of the century no respectable household was without several. Had it never crossed from the sun to the rain, the umbrella (10)____ still be a ceremonial curiosity rather than the most borrowed and most forgotten object in the world.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["but"],"answer":"but","explanation":"The correlative pair is <b>not… but…</b>: <i>not a shield against rain but against the sun</i>. <i>Nor</i> would deny both, and <i>or</i> would leave the sentence undecided, which contradicts the explanation about the Latin word for shade."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["over","above"],"answer":"over","explanation":"A canopy is held <b>over</b> (or <i>above</i>) someone's head to shade it. <i>On</i> would put the canopy in contact with the head, and <i>upon</i> the same, which is not how a servant shades a king."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"<i>Dare</i> as a main verb takes a <b>to</b>-infinitive: <i>would have dared to claim</i>. <i>Of</i> and <i>for</i> cannot follow <i>dared</i> before a verb."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["hit","chanced","stumbled"],"answer":"hit","explanation":"To <b>hit on</b> (or <i>chance on</i>, <i>stumble on</i>) an idea is to think of it suddenly. <i>Came on</i> is not used for ideas — the phrasal verb would be <i>came up with</i>, which needs different prepositions."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["into"],"answer":"into","explanation":"<b>Well into the eighteenth century</b> means a good way through that century, which the adverb <i>still</i> confirms. <i>Before</i> would clash with <i>still</i>, and <i>in</i> cannot follow <i>well</i> as an intensifier."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["without"],"answer":"without","explanation":"<b>Not without reason</b> is a fixed understatement meaning 'with some justification'. <i>Not for reason</i> and <i>not by reason</i> are not idioms."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["caught"],"answer":"caught","explanation":"A fashion or habit <b>catches on</b> when it becomes popular; the past narrative needs <i>caught</i>. <i>Took on</i> means to acquire or employ, and <i>went on</i> means continued, neither of which makes sense after thirty years of mockery."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["between"],"answer":"between","explanation":"You <b>choose between</b> two alternatives joined by <i>and</i>. <i>Among</i> is for three or more, and <i>from</i> would need a single group noun rather than <i>X and Y</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["Once","once","When","when","After","after"],"answer":"Once","explanation":"<b>Once</b> (or <i>when</i>, <i>after</i>) <i>the steel rib was patented, the umbrella became lighter</i> — a time linker showing one event followed the other. <i>Since</i> would need a present-perfect main clause, and <i>while</i> suggests the two things happened over the same period."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["might","would","could"],"answer":"might","explanation":"A mixed conditional: an inverted past-unreal condition (<i>Had it never crossed…</i>) with a present result, so the main clause needs <b>might</b>/<i>would</i>/<i>could</i> + <i>still be</i>. <i>Will</i> and <i>may</i> belong to real conditionals and clash with the unreal <i>Had it never</i>."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-25-3","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Carried Arm to Arm","topic":"how vaccines spread","passage":"When a country doctor in rural England announced in 1798 that matter taken from a cowpox sore could protect a person against smallpox, the reaction was far from unanimous. Some physicians dismissed the claim (1)____ of hand; others, more cautiously, asked for proof. What eventually won the argument was less the doctor's writing (2)____ the simple fact that the vaccinated did not fall ill when smallpox next visited their villages. Within a decade the practice had spread across Europe, carried largely by letters and by dried scraps of cotton thread (3)____ had been soaked in the fluid. (4)____ far the greatest obstacle was distance. The living material lost its power after a few weeks, so a shipment that set (5)____ from Lisbon might be useless by the time it reached Brazil. The solution, when it came, was as ingenious as it was uncomfortable. A group of orphans was put aboard a ship, and every ten days or so the fluid was passed from the arm of one child to that of the (6)____, keeping it alive across the whole Atlantic crossing. By such means the vaccine reached the Americas and, later, the Philippines and China. (7)____ we may now think of the ethics of using children as containers, the expedition is estimated to have protected hundreds of thousands of people. Not everywhere, though, did the idea take root so easily. In some regions it was resisted (8)____ religious grounds; in others, local healers saw it as a threat to their livelihood. Only where governments made vaccination compulsory, and (9)____ so within living memory of an epidemic, did the numbers climb quickly. It was, in other words, fear rather than reason (10)____ did most of the persuading.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["out"],"answer":"out","explanation":"To dismiss something <b>out of hand</b> is to reject it without considering it, which contrasts with the cautious physicians who asked for proof. <i>Off hand</i> exists but is written without <i>of</i> and means 'without preparation'."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["than"],"answer":"than","explanation":"<i>Less</i> earlier in the clause sets up a comparison that must be completed with <b>than</b>: <i>less the doctor's writing than the simple fact</i>. <i>But</i> would need <i>not… but</i>, and <i>as</i> pairs with <i>as much</i>, not <i>less</i>."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["which","that"],"answer":"which","explanation":"A defining relative clause about <i>scraps of cotton thread</i>: <b>which</b> or <i>that</i> acts as subject of <i>had been soaked</i>. <i>Where</i> cannot be the subject of a verb, and <i>they</i> would make a run-on sentence."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["By","by"],"answer":"By","explanation":"<b>By far the greatest</b> is the fixed way to intensify a superlative. <i>So far</i> means 'up to now' and <i>as far</i> needs <i>as</i> to complete it."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["out","off","sail"],"answer":"out","explanation":"A shipment <b>sets out</b> (or <i>sets off</i>, or, being on a ship, <i>sets sail</i>) <i>from</i> a port. <i>Set up</i> means to establish, and <i>set forth</i> is rare and needs no <i>from</i> in this sense."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["next","other"],"answer":"next","explanation":"With a whole group of children passing the fluid along in turn, <i>from one child to that of the</i> <b>next</b> is the precise word; the idiom <i>from one to the other</i> is also accepted. <i>Another</i> cannot follow <i>the</i>, and <i>same</i> would defeat the purpose."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["Whatever","whatever","However","however"],"answer":"Whatever","explanation":"<b>Whatever we may now think of the ethics…</b> is a concessive clause: no matter what our opinion is, the result stands; <i>however we may think</i> is an accepted variant. <i>Although</i> would need a full statement (<i>although we may now disapprove</i>), and <i>whichever</i> needs a noun to choose from."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["on"],"answer":"on","explanation":"Something is resisted, refused or defended <b>on</b> <i>religious grounds</i> — <i>grounds</i> meaning 'reasons' always takes <i>on</i>. <i>For</i> and <i>by</i> are not used with this noun in this sense."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["did"],"answer":"did","explanation":"<b>Did so</b> is the pro-verb standing for <i>made vaccination compulsory</i>, keeping the past tense of <i>made</i>. <i>Were so</i> and <i>had so</i> cannot replace an action verb in this way."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["that","which"],"answer":"that","explanation":"A cleft sentence: <i>It was fear rather than reason</i> <b>that</b> <i>did most of the persuading</i>; British usage also allows <i>which</i>. <i>What</i> is impossible after a cleft <i>It was X</i>, and <i>who</i> would need a person."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-25-4","level":"ch","mode":"open","title":"Before There Were Books","topic":"the first libraries","passage":"Long before there were books, there were libraries. The rooms (1)____ the rulers of Mesopotamia stored their clay tablets some four thousand years ago were not places of quiet reading but of administration: records of grain, tax and treaties, filed in baskets and labelled by subject. (2)____ was only gradually that such collections came to hold poems and prayers as well, and gradually again that anyone thought of them as worth preserving for their own sake. The most celebrated ancient library, at Alexandria, was founded (3)____ the express purpose of gathering every text in the known world. Ships that docked in the harbour were searched, and (4)____ scrolls they carried were copied before the originals — or, according to one story, the copies — were handed back. No trace of the building survives, and scholars still argue (5)____ whether it burned in a single fire or simply faded from neglect over centuries. What is certain is that its scholars invented much of what we take for (6)____ in a library today: shelving by author, alphabetical lists, and the idea that a text has a correct version worth arguing over. Elsewhere, libraries grew up wherever wealth and writing met. Chinese emperors employed armies of copyists; monasteries in medieval Europe chained their few precious volumes to the desks (7)____ they be stolen. Not until printing made books cheap did the notion of a library open (8)____ the public seem anything other than absurd. Even then, the earliest public collections were funded by private donors, most of (9)____ expected a statue in return. The free lending library that we now take as normal is, in historical terms, barely a moment old, and (10)____ we lose it, the loss would be felt long before anyone could explain how it had happened.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"text","q":"Blank (1): type the one word that fits.","accept":["where","wherein"],"answer":"where","explanation":"The clause describes what happened inside <i>the rooms</i>, so the relative adverb <b>where</b> (= in which) is needed. <i>Which</i> would have to be the object of <i>stored</i>, but <i>their clay tablets</i> already fills that slot."},{"n":2,"type":"text","q":"Blank (2): type the one word that fits.","accept":["It","it"],"answer":"It","explanation":"A cleft sentence emphasising the adverb: <b>It was only gradually that…</b>. <i>There</i> is used to introduce a noun that exists, not to front an adverb, and <i>This</i> would have nothing to refer back to."},{"n":3,"type":"text","q":"Blank (3): type the one word that fits.","accept":["with","for"],"answer":"with","explanation":"Something is founded <b>with</b> (or <i>for</i>) <i>the express purpose of</i> doing something. <i>On purpose</i> is a different idiom meaning 'deliberately' and cannot take <i>the express… of</i>."},{"n":4,"type":"text","q":"Blank (4): type the one word that fits.","accept":["any","whatever","the","all","what"],"answer":"any","explanation":"<b>Any scrolls they carried</b> — a determiner meaning every one there happened to be; <i>whatever</i>, <i>what</i>, <i>the</i> and <i>all</i> give the same sense. <i>Some</i> would suggest only a selection was copied, which contradicts the aim of gathering every text, and <i>every</i> needs a singular noun."},{"n":5,"type":"text","q":"Blank (5): type the one word that fits.","accept":["over","about"],"answer":"over","explanation":"People <b>argue over</b> or <i>argue about</i> a question. <i>Argue on</i> is not idiomatic before <i>whether</i>, and <i>argue for</i> means to support one side rather than to debate."},{"n":6,"type":"text","q":"Blank (6): type the one word that fits.","accept":["granted"],"answer":"granted","explanation":"<b>Take something for granted</b> means to assume it without noticing it — the shelving and lists nobody thinks about. <i>Take for certain</i> and <i>take for given</i> are not fixed expressions."},{"n":7,"type":"text","q":"Blank (7): type the one word that fits.","accept":["lest"],"answer":"lest","explanation":"The bare subjunctive <i>they be stolen</i> is the clue: only <b>lest</b> (= for fear that) takes this form. <i>In case</i> is two words and would need <i>they were stolen</i>; <i>unless</i> would reverse the meaning."},{"n":8,"type":"text","q":"Blank (8): type the one word that fits.","accept":["to"],"answer":"to","explanation":"A place is <b>open to the public</b>. <i>Open for the public</i> is not the collocation, and <i>open with</i> would mean the public was the first item on the programme."},{"n":9,"type":"text","q":"Blank (9): type the one word that fits.","accept":["whom"],"answer":"whom","explanation":"After a preposition in a non-defining relative clause about people, the pronoun must be the object form: <i>most of</i> <b>whom</b>. <i>Who</i> cannot follow <i>of</i>, and <i>which</i> is for things, not donors."},{"n":10,"type":"text","q":"Blank (10): type the one word that fits.","accept":["should","if"],"answer":"should","explanation":"<b>Should we lose it</b> is the inverted form of <i>if we should lose it</i>, a remote future condition, and matches the <i>would be felt</i> result; plain <i>if</i> is also accepted. <i>Were</i> would need <i>to</i> (<i>were we to lose it</i>) and <i>had</i> would need a past participle."}]},{"id":"cl-ch-25-5","level":"ch","mode":"mcq","title":"Cold on Demand","topic":"the ice trade","passage":"In the winter of 1806 a young Boston merchant loaded a ship with blocks of frozen pond water and sent it to the Caribbean. His neighbours thought he had (1)____ his mind. Who, they asked, would pay for something that fell out of the sky for nothing every winter and melted the moment you turned your back? The first voyage lost money, as did several after it, and the merchant spent time in a debtors' prison before the business (2)____ began to pay. What turned it around was not the ice itself but everything (3)____ it: insulated ice-houses at the ports, sawdust packing that slowed the melt, and horse-drawn cutters that scored a frozen lake into neat squares in a fraction of the time hand saws took. By the 1830s, ice from New England was (4)____ unloaded in Calcutta after a four-month voyage, and had become, improbably, one of the region's largest exports by weight. (5)____ the trade grew, it changed habits far from the lakes where it started. Doctors prescribed ice for fevers; brewers found they could work through the summer; fishermen could send their catch inland instead of salting it. The people who cut the ice, (6)____, worked in brutal cold for a season's wages and were often laid (7)____ the moment the lake was cleared. The end came quickly. Once artificial refrigeration became reliable, no one saw any (8)____ in waiting for the weather, and by the 1920s the last of the great ice-houses had been pulled down. It is tempting to dismiss the whole episode as a curiosity, (9)____ it was the ice trade that taught the world to expect cold on demand, and (10)____ that expectation, the modern kitchen would look very different.","questions":[{"n":1,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (1): choose the word that fits.","options":["left","missed","lost","dropped"],"correct":2,"explanation":"To have <b>lost one's mind</b> is to have gone mad, which is what the neighbours' mocking question implies. <i>Left</i>, <i>missed</i> and <i>dropped</i> do not combine with <i>mind</i> to mean this."},{"n":2,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (2): choose the word that fits.","options":["lastly","latterly","lately","finally"],"correct":3,"explanation":"<b>Finally</b> marks the long-awaited moment after repeated losses. <i>Lastly</i> introduces the last item in a list, <i>latterly</i> means 'in recent times' and <i>lately</i> needs a present-perfect verb."},{"n":3,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (3): choose the word that fits.","options":["around","beside","along","across"],"correct":0,"explanation":"<i>Everything</i> <b>around</b> <i>it</i> means the whole system that surrounded the product — the colon then lists that system. <i>Beside</i> is merely physical position, and <i>along</i> and <i>across</i> describe movement or extent."},{"n":4,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (4): choose the word that fits.","options":["formerly","routinely","lately","shortly"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>Routinely unloaded</b> — the point is that a once-absurd voyage had become ordinary. <i>Formerly</i> would place it in an earlier period, <i>lately</i> needs the present perfect and <i>shortly</i> means 'soon'."},{"n":5,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (5): choose the word that fits.","options":["Whereas","Until","As","Whether"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>As the trade grew, it changed habits</b> — two developments running together over time. <i>Whereas</i> sets up a contrast, <i>until</i> would stop the change when the trade grew, and <i>whether</i> needs an alternative."},{"n":6,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (6): choose the word that fits.","options":["moreover","therefore","likewise","however"],"correct":3,"explanation":"The sentence contrasts the workers' hardship with the benefits just listed, so the connector is <b>however</b>. <i>Moreover</i> and <i>likewise</i> would add another benefit, and <i>therefore</i> would make the hardship a consequence of the benefits."},{"n":7,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (7): choose the word that fits.","options":["off","out","down","up"],"correct":0,"explanation":"Workers are <b>laid off</b> when there is no more work for them, which is what happens once the lake is cleared. <i>Laid out</i> means arranged or spent, <i>laid down</i> means set as a rule, and <i>laid up</i> means confined by illness."},{"n":8,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (8): choose the word that fits.","options":["reason","point","need","cause"],"correct":1,"explanation":"<b>See any point in</b> doing something is the collocation for judging whether it is worthwhile. <i>Reason</i> and <i>cause</i> take <i>for</i> or <i>to</i>, not <i>in</i> + -ing, and <i>need</i> would take <i>to wait</i>."},{"n":9,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (9): choose the word that fits.","options":["so","for","yet","nor"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Yet</b> introduces the objection to dismissing the episode: it was, in fact, important. <i>So</i> and <i>for</i> would present the importance as a result or a reason for dismissing it, which is illogical, and <i>nor</i> needs a preceding negative."},{"n":10,"type":"mcq","q":"Blank (10): choose the word that fits.","options":["beyond","unlike","without","despite"],"correct":2,"explanation":"<b>Without that expectation, the kitchen would look different</b> — a hidden conditional (= if that expectation did not exist). <i>Despite</i> would mean the expectation opposes the change, and <i>beyond</i> and <i>unlike</i> do not express a condition."}]}];
if (typeof module !== 'undefined' && module.exports) module.exports = { CLOZE_PASSAGES };
